      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>ALWAY 3 U" M 0 DOTTGA</head>
        <p>ALWAY 3 U" M 0 DOTTGA</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>TUF.RE 13 but little difference between the man .who is tolerably good and the man who is tolerably bad.</p>
        <p>"Tins is what I call a stiff drink," said Jack, when he found the water frozen in his pitcher in the morning.</p>
        <p>THE man who talks when he has nothing to say is not so bad as the man who talks when he has something to say which he ought not to say. THE IMPORTANT PART.—"They say Van Dab- ble's wife helps him to sell his pictures." "She does. She selects the frames."</p>
        <p>ITS DISADVANTAGE. —Teacher : What is the distinguishing feature of classical music ?" Tommy Toddles: " Yer can't whistle it."</p>
        <p>AFFLICTION SOIL—She: " Hasn'tplrs. Brown a terrible cold?" He: "Yes, indeed. She's so hoarse she can't talk about it."</p>
        <p>_ Pim—First Tramp: "Them Eyetalians has curious notions." Second Tramp: "How?" First Tramp: "Look at that feller grindin' the organ. I s'pose he thinks that ain't work."</p>
        <p>TIIE AUTHORITY.—Mr. Suburbs: "I see there's a new family moved to Lonelywood." Mrs. Suburbs: " Yes ; but I don't think they amount to much. The station agent hasn't heard anything about them yet." A LIKELY SOLUTION. Srnithers : "I wonder what was the origin of the saying, It will all come out in the wash' ?" Rinktums : "Don't know. I suppose it referred to indelible marking ink."</p>
        <p>THERE ARE OTEERS. —Teller : "Confound that fellow Cheekley ! I hate to do him a favour." .Askins : "Why so?" Teller: "Because whenever I do, instead of appreciating it, he flatters himself that he has done me."</p>
        <p>WOF.ST KIND OF BUBBLE.—RuraI Raggs: "I've been readin', Tatts, about that South Sea Bubble, an' what a terrible thing it was." Tramping Tatters: "What was it, Roory—a soap bubble?" A NATURAL INFERENCE.—DimpIeton : "I am not living with my father-in-law any more." Van Blumer: "Great Scott! You don't mean to say that you have separated from your wife ?"</p>
        <p>- ITS VALUE.—" Is your picture in the Academy a Success ? " "That's what I am wondering. Some one said it was worth the price of ad- mission."</p>
        <p>AT T.IIB OPERA.--Tiarry (as Othello comes on) : "How fierce the Mooch looks!" Cholly (shudder- ing) : "Yes, indeed! Ugh! What a tailoh he'd make!" •</p>
        <p>" WHAT are you going to drink my boy?" "I don't know what to have, I'm sure." "Have a liqueur. Have a Gold water." "No, I don't care for the Eau d'or."</p>
        <p>PUZZLING THE LAWYEL—A sharp fellow once asked a lawyer this question—" If a peacock be- longing to your neighbour came into your garden and laid an egg there whom would the egg belong to by law?': The lawyer answered that it would belong to the owner of the peacock. The other then replied—" Have you ever heard of a peacock laying an egg 1"</p>
        <p>Tourasr (on country walking tour) : I say, my good man, there used to be two windmills here once, didn't there 7—Hodge: 'Es zur, that be zo, but they found that thur wur only wind enough to work one. MAKING USE OF HIS FORTIINE.—A man in a western county, who had just been through the bankruptcy coun, went into a grocer's shop and ordered a sovereign's worth of goods. When they were ready for delivery he laid down a couple of half-crowns in payment. "This isn't enough," called the shop assistant, as the man was about to leave. "Oh! yes, that's all right," replied the customer. "I've got permission from the judge to pay five shillin' in the pound!" BETTER To STAY.—In an asylum two worthies named Sandie and Tam, formed a plan to make their escape. Sandie says to Tam, Bend doon and I'll get on your back and get on the tap o' the wall and haul you up." Sandie gets on the top of the wall and slides down the other side, saying, "Tarn, I think you'll be better to stay anither fortnicht, for you're no near sane THE SHEPHERD AND THE SHEEP.—A clergyman catechising a Sunday-school. When comparing himself—the pastor of the church—to a shepherd, and his congregation to the sheep, put the follow- ing question to the children:"What does the shepherd do for the sheep?" A small boy in the front row piped out—" Shears them."</p>
        <p>I TELL you what's wrong with your cow, Farmer Oatstraw ; you don't feed her on sound principles. Hadn't got none, sir. They don't do on our soil, and I have to make shift with turmuts !</p>
        <p>SOMETHING IN THE CYCLE'S FAVOUR.—Wheeler: "I don't see why railroad baggagemen are so 4is- agreeable about carrying cycles." Sprockets: "Nor I. It's a great deal easier to smash a bicycle than a trunk." NEGRO PRISONER BFXORE JUSTICE: "Yes, Bah, I tuck de chicken. I was gwinter make some chicken pie, and I tuk de cook-book and read de directshuns, and hit says, Take one chicken.' Hit don't say buy one chicken, or borrow one chicken, but hit says take one chicken. Hit don't say whose chicken ter take, so I jess tuck de first one I could lay my han's on. I followed de directshuns, sail, in the de book."</p>
        <p>STRATEG/0 MOVEMENTS. First Boy: "See here I Didn't 1 see you running down street yester- day, with Bill Bounce after you, wantin' to lick you ? " Second Boy: " Y-e-s. ' "What did you run for?" " was only running se as to get him away from home, so his mother couldn't see him fightin' ; but by the time we was out of sight of his house we got in sight of our house; and then, as my mother would see me if I stopped to hit him, I went in, so as to be out o' temptation." SORRY Fon HE HEARING. —An Irish fish hawker was severely beating his donkey for furious braying, when a humane-hearted elderly lady, who was witness of the chastisement, pa- thetically exclaimed—" Dear me, why are you ill-using the poor, dumb brute so?" " Dimb, did you say?" emphatically ejaculated Pat; "thin bedad, mum, yez must be deaf."</p>
        <p>Nor Tuna Fauvr.—ln a certain part of Banff- shire, were the toads are as nearly precipitous as may be imagined, a little girl was one day employed in her usual task of herding the cows. A minister, newly settled in the parish coming suddenly upon her, remarked: "Them are awful hills you have here, laosie." Overcome, doubtless, by the feeling of awe for the cloth so common in rural Scotland, the frightened lassie answered, in apologetie tones: " Deed, sir, they were here afore we cam'!"</p>
        <p>_ Nov WHAT IT USED To Bs.—" No use talkin'," said the farmer, mournfully, to the conductor of the Piccadilly omnibus, " Lunnon hairet the town it used to be." "Why, what's the matter with it, guv'ner " "I've been here three days, and nobody has tried to work the confidence trick on me, or tried to snatch my watch." FOUND OUT His MISTAKE.—A short time ago an Irishman paid a visit to INewcrotle-on-Tyne. After strolling about for some time he came to an octagonal building. Not having seen a house built in that manner before, he stopped and looked at it. As he was gazing at it, a man who was sitting at one of the windows, saw Pat staring about. Rais- ing the window and putting out his head, he asked Pat rather roughly if lie took the house for a church. " Bedad I did," said Pat, "till I saw the devil looking out of the window." Su was a modern boarding-school miss, and being told by the governess to parse, "He kissed me," consented reluctlantly because opposed to speaking of private matters in public. '"He,' she commenced with unnecessary emphasis, and a fond lingering over the word that brought the crimson to her cheeks, is a pronoun, third person, singular number, masculine gender, a gentleman, and pretty well to do; universally considered a good catch ! ' 6 Kissed' is a verb, transitive, too much so, regular every evening, indicating affection; first and third persons, plural number, and governed by circum- stances. Me '—Oh, well, everybody knows me.' And she sat down. THE remarkable unanimity that is so pleasant to observe between man and wife, is nicely illustrated by the following letters of same date : .. country, August 20.—Dear John,—l am going to stay another week. Am _shaving a splendid time. Affectionately, Julia." "City, August 20.—Dear Julia,—You can stay another week. Am having a splendid time.—Affectionately, John." For some reason or other she concludes to pack up and start for home immodietely, to see about his "splendid time."</p>
