      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>S' LETTER.</head>
        <p>S' LETTER.</p>
        <p>The punchinello hats, with front rosette and loose, swaying plumes above, are immensely fashionable, and somehow seem in perfect harmony with the sloped front of the mucn-befrilled cape. Unfortunately, the mere fact of turning up the front of a hat and adorning it with jewelled rosette does not make it into a Punchinello, as many apparently believe, and with disastrous result. The round hat is especially ugly in this style, as the long, pointed aides of the proper shape are half its charm.</p>
        <p>The turned-up hat is a powerful rival to the toque, which is still worn with afternoon gowns by the matron; but the younger wearer only dons it with the morning gown in tailor style. There are some exquisite floral toques, but, possibly owing to dull weather, have not become favourites. The turban shape, with rolled edge, draped in sequined tulle over chiffon, is most in favour, and handsome plumes invariably figure at the sides. But it is not everyone who can wear a hat; cer- tain faces require toques. I will mention some of the most effective. A white plaited straw is covered with Chantilly, on the left side rise three white plumes spangled with sequins and fastened by a diamond star. A toque of very soft maize crinoline is trimmed with silk straw, and large bows of black velvet holding ripe wheat ears. Another, in red velvet, is crushed beneath clusters of cherries, while a large white bonnet, crowned with myosotis, supports on its right side an enor- mous Louis XVI. bow of turquoise velvet.</p>
        <p>A pretty blouse for evening wear is of turquoise blue silk, with white satin baby ribbon gathered into fulness to decorate the sleeves, again to pro- duce something of a bolero effect on the front of the bodice, and finally to cross in trellis-work fashion at the back, while an appliqud of cream lace must by no means be forgotten. There is, too, a full front of white chiffon as background for a scarf of the same soft fabric, with ruchings of baby ribbon to finish the ends, and the 1ap415, too, are white, the material satin with bordering tucks.</p>
        <p>Very stylish blouses can be made in any of the fashionable thin textures, as a bodice for a gown of cashmere, voile, or nun's veiling. A blouse would look especially effective in grey or any of the blaet tints now in vogue, and the collar may be of ivory silk, covered with applique lace, or in silk openwork passementerie, matching the material, and this latter makes a really smart collar when outlined with silk ruching of the shade of the gown. Three deep tucks go completely round the bodice, and there are buttons of cut steel on the fronts. The vest is of white silk, slightly full, and with a jabot and cascade of ivory lace sewn down the centre, and a folded stock of the white silk. The sleeves have two deep tucks, raising the slightly full top, and the belt matches the collar. A blouse of this kind would do well for coloured linen or drill, with yellow lace appliqued on, 0: used as a covering, and the edge finished with a stitched binding of the coloured linen. Buttons of smoked pearl are effective on linen, and the front may be of cambric, with centre pleat frilled each side, and turndown collar, worn with a tie.</p>
        <p>A fashionable costume for afternoon wear is made in the new grey alpaca. The skirt touches the ground and is glove-fitting on the hips. With this is worn a jacket to match with Eton fronts and receding basque, bordered with blue or cherry velvet. There are incrogable revers faced with white satin. The blouse worn underneath the noat is of flowered cambric, draped neckband, with single end to the waist in foulard in keep- ing with the trimmings.</p>
        <p>There are two or three ways of making a pina- fore-frock, but the effect is much the same when the dress is completed. Perhaps the simplest shape is that which is cut all in one with two box. pleats back and front, these being held in place at the waist by wide tapes sown to the inside of the pleats, which can, of course, be untacked for wash- ing. The top of the frock is cut out either in a square or round according to taste, and finished by a narrow frill of embroidery or of the materiel itself, and the shoulder-straps are similarly trim- med. The sleeved chemisette to wear under the over-dress is made on a thin cambric or lining under-bodice reaching to the waist.</p>
        <p>Your loving JENNIE.</p>
