      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>Great improvements have been made in the Royal stables lately, and upon the last occasion that the Queen was in residence at Buckingham Palace, Her Majesty while driving in the grounds in her pony carriage paid an informal visit to this department, expressing himselt Ratisfied with what was being done. There are about 120 horses in Her Majesty's stables, and needless to say tha grooming is kept up to an exceptional degree of ex- cellence. .</p>
        <p>The task of naming every fresh horse that is acquired for the Sovereign's stud, devolves upon an understudy of the Duke of Portland, Master of the Horse. A hand- some pair of colts that have recently take their places in the Royal stables, he has named "Midnigly." and "Gloaming." The first named colt has a coat of inky blackness, the second possesses one several shades lighter. The names are therefore particularly appro- priate. No small ingenuity has sometimes been shown in inventing names for horses. An espeeially neat ex- ample was due to the inventive genius of the late Earl Stanhope. He was once asked to name a horse which had "Rubens" for sirs and " Election" for dam. He ,ffected an illusion to both parents by naming the off- spring "Canvas." The health of the Princess Louise, Marchionesa of Lorne, still continues to cause her relatives and friends some anxiety. Her Royal Highness is of a highly active disposition, which coupled with a nervous temperament, occasionally produces a break down. Excitement of any kind has therefore, fer some time past been carefully avoided for her, and the launch of the Ocean by the Princess was looked upon, with considerable disfavour by her medical attendants. However, ah's well that ends well, and it is to be hoped this recent appearance of her Royal Highness is but a forerunner of many others to come. The Archduchess Stephanie of Austria who, though making wonderful progress, has not yet succeeded in throwing off all her invalid habits, has taken a house near'Ventnor—lsle of Wight—for the summer season. Her Imperial Elighnesi is an accomplished musician,and h,ls a magnificent contralto voice. She is also clever with her pen and pencil, and an ardent ameteur photo• grapher, possessing a marvellous collection of photo- eraph; taken in all parts of Europe. Mr. Thomas Code-arm who has recently been made D:puty Governor of the Isle of Wight, and custodian of Carisbrooke Castle, is the son of the late Admiral Sir Thomas Cochrane, Mr. Cnchranti's mother, Lady Coch- rane, resides at Quart Abbey, and his sisters are Mrs!! Lousia and Miss Minnie Cochrane, the latter being lady- in-waiting, to Princess Henry of Battenhere, wense eenpemoen it will he remembered, was spent Sr Qearr Ahter. Miss Minnie is a great favourite of the Queen, who likes her bright charming maauer. She has always much to do with the arrangement of Windsor Castle theatricals, and is a frequent guest at royal dinner pa rtiff4. At all dinner parties given by the Princl and Prencess of iNahs,thP menu is invariably couched in the French language. No matter what the nationality of their Royal Highnesses guests may be. But with the Queen, no hard and fast rule obtains with regard to the lan- guage emploed for the menu cards. And these, decor- ated with views of Buckingham Palace, are printed in colers. Yet another Pinw phetographin fancy work consisti of photographs finely printed on silk, so that they look like drawines. They can then be delicately and beautifully colored, with paints prepared for the purpose, and used for sachets, fans, screens, etc. The idea is, without doubt, an exeellent one. The sun does your drawne you, but then the sue is no "impressionist." The result is set-retirees a little too hard and definite to please ima- ginative f dk, who like to see only the beautiful repro- duced. This however, can all be put right by the latter process of painting. The famous American miniature painter Miss Eussner hie arrived here on a short visit, and is painting minia- tures of the comae Duehes.a of Marlborough, Lady listen Vincent, and Lsdy Nqlor-Leyland. Later, it is said, Rile intend, visitina. Russia, with a view of painting aCtne t:Pall..ieS of the Rus•dan Court. Tlae continued de- mand for miniatures is haVillrf it 9 eff.'et neon portrsit ! - 1 nslntsrs, and not a few have taksn to working on ivory. ! The Queen isvery partial to rniniaturee and possesses a : seperh collection, That the aline of anthorta have gone very. muet of late years, is u- Tenitiors able, Mrs. Brnwnine thought £2OO a goad price for a novel, and it was the price paid to Mrs. Geskell " Mary Berton." N)wadave thines are altered. I vrill quote two instenees. T.) Mrs Hedg- son-Barnette the dramatization alone„of her took "Little Lerd Fanntlerny," brought.22o 000,and Mrs. Iliennhrny Ward received a like sum for " David Grieve." Few British authors though have turned more of their liter- ary profits to philauthrophic purpose than Miss Char- lotte Mary Yonge, the author of the "Heir of Itelcliff e" the proceeds of one of her hrsoks went to the fitting OW of a missinnarn scheel, and thess of another towards building of a New Zealend missionary eenege. Her various other charitable deeds are too numerous to mention here. The d.mand for antique fans; this season has been. considerable. especially Spanish and French examples, with perforated and highly ornamental stickg in ivory, and mother-of-pearl, the mounts more or less beautifully Painted. In modern fans, quite the newest thing is the ' 1).14 elt specimen with hold desions in Russian applique worked with silver paillettes. This Records well with the plittPrinc trimmings almost inseparable from an up-tre date frock. Lady Charlotte Schreiber,upon many counts a very remarkable woman, mav well be called the British Gransle Dame of hebbies of the latter end of thus nineteenth century. She loved clllecting beautiful and eurinus things, but above all she loved collectine, fans, and before her death, had gathered together a unique collect inn of these interesting, and exquisite weapons of femininity. Ls t.lc, many women have appeared at afternoon ferivities in gowns built with transparent yolks and skeves. And how they have maiueged to live through such an experience is a mystery. Of enfl,ao such a molie during a hot sultry summer, would be delightful, hut by no stretch of imaginatirn can We consider the present seasers favorable to transparent outdoor Rowena. The more sensible content thems,lvee with flesh colored Unions, for yolk and sleeves. The effect is not so good, but—our health tames first. It is no longer necessary that a hat sl'ould match the costume with which it is worn. So long as it just acknowledges the costume's leadership by eXhiKting its dominating note in either bow, feather, or flower, that Is enough. By the way, the old Dunstablo unbleached straws, with a shiny surface, and in a fancy p'ait, have come Mtn fashion from that remote pr.rind when our grandmothers were voting,—an item CO he grateful for as then are yenhful looking, flutist intial, and well-wear. ire. Our childhood's friend, the fief-mine leehorn too, is in hieh fever at the moment, tiimmed with apparently esreiess hunches of cornfliwers, poppies, oars of eon, clover, and blowawave. But this latter form of head. ensr it must he remembered, is permissible only for quite young girhe</p>
        <p>BIRDS. ANIMALS AND FISH STUFFED, AND mounted in Glass Cases ane design. littADS and HORNS mounted, aleo for sale. Birds made into Hand Screens or Fire Screens. Peale in British Awl ForsOgn Mid. and Animals' Skins. Butterflies, Ririe' Eggs. Gloss similes, Stags' Heads. Cabinets. Store Boxes, Nets and all ervitites for Botterfly end Egg Collectors --B. Coogß, oirßligt and Furrier, 21, Benallaw - street, Liverpool st.s.cS stied 2.4 years.</p>
        <p>In this world there are always compensations. The summer we have bad no summer, we have also had no dies ! Flies object to a summer such as we are enjoy- bug (2) this year, one that is neither one thing nor another, but appears to be an odd season slipped in for the benefit of the country, and the confusion of the Meterological Office. They have therefore remained in seclusion, and refuse to be lured out of their retreat by deceptive ealenders, that persist in calling this the month of July.</p>
        <p>el woman who is a gentlewoman, always delights in dainty receptacles for her " things "—tho recognised generic title for her personal belongings, shoe bags, brush and comb bags, sachets, etc. Silk night dress sachets are very pretty, They are made up with wide herns,the design for embroidery is traced upon them and every artistic colour is represented. Then soiled linen bags,in white with the mouth of the bag lined with col- oured linen,which shows as the draw string closes it,are most useful. When the entire bag is made of coloured liven, the cost of it is a fraction over a shilling. Such articles are necessary to every woman of refine- ment,who is nothing if not fastidioua about the tri!ling helon2ings which those whose taste is less cultured think little or nothing of. However simple in character. these create a kind of atmosphere about their possersor which is always acknowledged and felt by a class who think more of a smare dregs or bonnet than of the details of the toilet,and that evidence of refinement expressed rather in neat gloves and neat shoes, and the little etceteras that are never absent in the surroundings of a gentlewoman, however poor. POTLTO CHEESE Cum.—Take one pound of potatoes well mashed, a quarter of a pound each of sultanas, sugar, and dripping (good beef dripping), add two eggs well I;Nsteri, mix well together, and bake in patty pans lined with a light paste. #1 MADGE."</p>
