      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>RUSH TO KLONDYKE. SOUND ADVICE FROM AN OLD HAND.</head>
        <p>THE RUSH TO KLONDYKE. SOUND ADVICE FROM AN OLD HAND.</p>
        <p>IN the rush towards ttn!Klondyke rddfiolds there are thousands who are ill•fitteci to stand the straid of hard- ohio and exposure to be encountered on the journey U‘ere, Einem, disease, and death claim many of th.e ill-prepared adventurers, and the follnwing letter from one who has undergone these hardshipa and arrived triumphant, will prove interesting to readers, whether they intend travelling to the desolate gold-laden country or riot : _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Skagway, December 12th, 1897. DEAR SIRS,—My object in wating this letter is to give a word of advice to those who contemplate going to the Yukon goldfialds. F,r ten yers I have followed the occupatism of prospFcing, timber estimating, and mining, and the hardships and privations which one has to undergo are enough to wreck the stren got con- stitutions. In the spring of 1897 I was stricken with pleurisy, as the result of exhosure. and this left behind the seeds of disease in the form of heart and kidney troubles. I managed to reach Var. couver, but did not have much hopes et recovering. There I was advised, however, to give [Jr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People a trial, and purchased two boxes. Before these were gone I found beyond a doubt that they were doing me good,and thnir coatinuel u9e " put me on my feet again." I then engaged ta go to the Yukon country. and only those who had been the journey to Dawson City can form even the faintest conception of the hardships that have to be borne. I went in and returned to this place by the Dalton trail, which consists of 350 miles of old Indian trail, starting at Pyramid Harbour. In going over the trail one has at timf-li to wade through mud more than a foot deep, and ford streams waist deep in ice-cold waters. B•fore starting I added to my outfit two dozen boxea of Dr. Williams' Pink Pille, and I can honestly say that they proved of invaluable service to me, and I would stroogly urge every man who gore in to take a amply with him, When I started for the Yukon my w4aht was only 149 ;muck, and now, thanks to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, I wtiah 169 pounds. lam soon farting for another trip to Dawson. This time, how- ever, the travelling will hr! on ArourOmes, and you may ci,pemi upon it Dr, Williarns' Pink Nis will again form part of my outfit. _ .</p>
        <p>I write this letter that you may know what your medicine has done for me. Every man,who undertakes the trip to the YukOrl, will rt quire something to lueP his con,titution sound in that country. My home is at C:ipper Cliff. Out, where my wife now regidea. Yours truly, JOHN Plena. For climatic and ol her reasons Uquid medicines Can' ri,t be carried by the prospector, and here Dr. Williams' Pink Pills prove their uperioritv. They are not only the most effective tonic and strengthening medicine,but are in such a compact form that a supply can be carried in the iymin-t at all times. They cure ariemia, p ilpitation of the heart, indi.clestion, general muscular weakness, rheumatism, sciatica, chronic esysipdaa, and restore pie and sallow complexions to tho lzlow of health. They are also a splendid nerve and spinal tonic arid thus have cured many CAACIS of pa ralytis, locomotor ataxy, neuralgia. St. Vitus' dance, and nervous head- ache. Obtainable of all chAmists,and from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, rFillarirn.viaduct, London, at 24 91:1. a bax, or six for 13i. 91; oenuine only with full name, Dr. Williarm.' Pink gills 'for Pale PeoPl6." AP substitutes under somewhat similar titles are worth- less.</p>
      </div>
