      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>(Copyriff,fir.,)</head>
        <p>(Copyriff,fir.,)</p>
        <p>A WARD OF THE KING. A</p>
        <p>By KATFIARINE S. MACQUOID, Author of "Maisie Derrick," "The Story of Lois," "Patty," "Louisa," "At the Red Glove," "Miss Eyon of Eyoncourt," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: Jeanne Kergrist, a wealthy heiress and an only child, has become on her father's death ward of the King of France. Jeanne is only thirteen when the King commands her to marry the Count of Laval, who is twenty years her senior. The story opens in the garden of Jeanne's house, where Jeanne tells her friend and playfellow Rolland of her dislike to the proposed marriage. Rolland, a fine young fellow, dislikes it too. He has been brought up with Jeanne, and, if he were richer, would marry her when she grows up. The Count of Laval comes to the castle accompanied by a handsome young cousin, Gilles de Retz. Jeanne likes the Count, but she takes a dislike to De Retz, who flatters and pities her. He and the Count of Laval leave the castle directly after the marriage ceremony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CHAPTER 111.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER 111.</p>
        <p>FAREWELL.</p>
        <p>It was the day after the wedding; Jeanne was on her way to the garden, clad soberly in blue, when at the castle entrance she found Rolland de la Touche. He stood bending over the old deerhound Montfort; the dog had set his front paws against the powerful figure, clad now in buff coat and steel thigh-pieces, and was crying over the young Baron in a kind of sorrowful babble. The assemblage of strangers yesterday had bewildered the faithful creature, and its joy broke loose at the sight of this old friend.</p>
        <p>Rolland heard Jeanne's footsteps, and pushed the dog away as he turned to greet the young girl. From babyhood she had always offered him her forehead to kiss; now, without knowing why, she held out her hand instead.</p>
        <p>He looked at her, and laughed. "I come sooner than you expected me, but my orders were sent on to me at Giietfrec ; I am only here to say farewell, ray child." She gave him a saucy smile. "Ah, indeed; that was your idea? It is not mine; You are coming with me to the garden, where you shall sit on the grass, and listen to my news; I have a story to tell you." As he followed her, she said over her shoulder: "To begin with, I am no longer Jeanne Kergrist!" and she ran away to the garden gate. He stared, and hurried after her; his brows were knit, and his blue eyes were full of pain. He followed her to the old sun-dial against which they usually sat; then he said gravely: "At least, Jeanne, I have no spare time for riddle-guessing, so you must be plain with me, my child." Jeanne only obeyed two persons—her mother and Rolland de la Touche. She was sometimes fractious and wayward when her old playmate teased her, but when he spoke seriously she yielded at once. _ _ .</p>
        <p>She looked at him, clasped her hands behind her head, and pirouetted on her heels. Then she blew through her closed fists, imitating the sound of a trumpet. "Know, then, by these tokens," she said loftily, "that I am no longer the little girl, Jeanne Kergrist ; I am that high and mighty lady, Madame la Comtesse de Laval!"</p>
        <p>_ She stopped, frightened at the fierce light she saw in his dark-blue eyes. "If this be true, when were you married? " he said sternly. "At this hour yesterday the ink was not dry on the book we signed. But, Rolland, why are you displeased? After you left us, a week ago, I said to my mother you wished to be consulted about my marriage. She listened, and then she answered that it was not possible ; she had received direct command from the King to wed me without delay to Monsieur le Comte, and that she could do nothing but obey. Besides, lam to stay here for three instead of two years. What does it matter? Maybe I shall not see the Count again; he may fall in battle—who can tell? " she said airily. Rolland shook his head. "Child, you must not so speak, or even think. The Count of Laval is now your husband; your duty is to him, to speak of him with fitting reverence. He is a good and noble lord, and will merit your love ; his name and his protection will shield you from all harm." Jeanne looked pensive. "I pray you, do not chide, Rolland. But have you no curiosity? You have not so much as asked how I like my husband, or what I feel about my marriage. I shall tell you without asking. Sit down on the grass till Gillonne comes to seek us. There! that is as it should be ; now I can speak. I like Monsieur de Laval. He is old, but he looks kind and honest ; but I would rather have stayed as I was. Listen! there is one person I can never like, and that is the cousin of my husband; he looks cruel; I shiver when I think of his snake- like eyes." "Are his eyes dark, and is he tall and slender ? " She nodded.</p>
        <p>lie had spoken anxiously. "You mean Etienno de Retz," he said thought- fully. "No, he is not like his cousin; but the Countess of Laval can have nothing to fear from him."</p>
        <p>Jeanne sat a moment silent; then with a little shiver she said:</p>
        <p>"I do not know that; he oppressed me like a nightmare. I could not forget his presence; I felt that those sly, cruel eyes were fixed on me, reading my thoughts. Even through the priest's holy words I was thinking of that man, and of his power to harm me. I loathe him, Rolland, and I feel that some day he will try to do me a mischief." Again she shivered, and then squeezed herself close to Rolland, as they sat side by side on the grass.</p>
        <p>- - He took one of her slim hands, and kept it in his.</p>
        <p>"You need not fear him ; he has no power "—he spoke reassuringly—"unless Monsieur de 'Laval should die childless. In that case, De Retz succeeds to the Countship. But that need not trouble you ; try to forget this fancy, my dear little sister, and remember that whenever you need help I am at your service." There were tears in her bright eyes. "And when you are in Italy, Rolland, wounded, perhaps a prisoner, what then? " She looked wistfully into his honest, fearless eyes. He bent down and kissed her; this time she did not draw away. "Marriage has disagreed with you, my child; this is not the brave Jeanne who longed to lead her troops forward to victory like Jeanne la Flamme, and wear armour like La Pucelle ? "</p>
        <p>She wiped away he;";t4u;,-E-311-oTik her head, and looked up at him, once more his fearless, saucy Jeanne.</p>
        <p>"Ah, but that was when you were to be my captain and fight beside me! You must not mis- take, Rolland ; I liked some part of the marriage; for instance, I liked Monsieur de Laval—what I saw of him—and the casket of jewels he gave me. The worst part of all was my gown; without doubt it is lovely, it shimmers like sunshine ; though Gillonne vows it is old-fashioned; I believe I look like a countess in it—oh! I should love you to see me in lt! But alas! it seems to be lined with lead. I could not run in it if I tried; it must be as heavy as a suit of armoUr. And then, my shoes were tight and pinched me; I felt crippled, and longed for a trainbearer, but there was not time to seek one." _</p>
        <p>Gillonne had come silently into the garden. "Monsieur le Baron, my lady would see you in her upper chamber, if it so please you." Rolland rose, and turned to obey the summons. Jeanne began to follow him, but the woman said : "Mademoiselle is not summoned ; she will stay here with Montfort."</p>
        <p>. . The dog heard his name, and rubbed his head against his ruing mistress. Jeanne pushed the creature away; she drew herself up, and tossed her fair head. "You are to remember, Gillonne, that it is not fitting to call me mademoiselle; I am Madame he Comtesse de Laval."</p>
        <p>Madame Kergrist had sent away her maids, and Rolland found her alone in her large, pleasant chamber, with its rush-strewn floor and industrious- looking spinning-wheels and embroidery frames. The tapestried walls looked brighter than those in the ball ; there were flowers of varied hues in the foreground. The Countess smiled at Do la Touche as he came in, and greeted him as affectionately as though he were really her son. "We shall sadly miss you, my boy, but you must do like your fellows ; no brave gentleman, I think, will willingly absent himself from this campaign. Is it true that the King nil' command in person, or will he leave it to our brave cousin, the Con- stable de Bourbon ? "</p>
        <p>Rolland hesitated she tancie4 no looxea</p>
        <p>troubled.</p>
        <p>"It is thought that the King will command in person, when he joins us, but that will not happen ill we have crowd the Alps. There is a glorious list of commanders, and if the soldiers are paid success is said to be certain; but we have to de- pend in a measure on Swiss auxiliaries, and the Swiss are cold-blooded, they will not fight without pay. The Cardinal of Sion, too, is all-powerful with them, and he is ever a thorn in the side of France." Then he said abruptly: "Madame, have I not your permission to counsel old Leon, the seneschal, to increase the guard, and to keep a more diligent watch, by night as well as by day ? " He spoke urgently, and she looked surprised. "If it seems to you. needful, you can speak about this with Leon; but, Rolland, my husband was wont to say thact when France carried war beyond her frontiers there was usually peace at home." Rolland rose and paced up and down the chamber; he knew that the Countess disliked any change to be made in her household plans. "That was true in the days of Ring Louis; soldiers were then paid their wages. But I must tell you, madame, what is now common news: France is overrun by hundreds of soldiers who cannot get paid by their commanders; they leave their ranks to plunder the defenceless peasantry, andio seize on the crops and cattle; it is not so long since Château Preleon itself was attacked, in the absence of its lord." "Château Preleon is not far away," she said; "but its means of defence are inferior to ours; ur. dear lord considered our garrison sufficient to repel marauders. However, talk with Leon, if you will; but, in my judgment, the walls of Longuedee are a tough nut to crack for mere updisciplined soldiers." Her unbelief chafed Rolland, but he knew of old that argument would fail to convince her. "I will talk to Leon. And now, my second mother, farewell." He bent down and kissed her. "Be careful of your health; it needs not to bid you take care of Jeanne." She put her arm round him, and returned his kiss. "Farewell, my Holland; once I hoped that you would be my son indeed; but that is over. Wed some sweet woman who will be as an elder sister to my little Jeanne. May the blessing of God go with yowl°</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>• CHAPTER IV. BESIDE THE GRAVE.</head>
        <p>• CHAPTER IV. BESIDE THE GRAVE.</p>
        <p>A long procession of mourners came slowly down the hill from the Castle of Longuedec to the village churchyard. The death-bell sounded out its mournful, monotonous summons, and in answer there came from the small thatched cottages, that clustered round the old church spire as chickens gather round a hen, men and women and children, the women in their large-winged caps. till the whole population of Longuedec stood in groups waiting to take part in their lady's buriaL The long, grave Breton faces looked sadder than ever; the people knew they had lost a bountiful mistress and a friend in need in the late Countess; and though Madame de Laval—or Mademoiselle Jeanne, as some of the women still called her— remained with them, it was well known that her husband, the Count de Laval, had several months ago announced his intention of coining to claim his young wife, and that he had yielded to Madame Kergrist's request that her daughter might be allowed to close her eyes before she left her. There could be no doubt but that the young Countess would have to leave Longuedec directly after the funeral rites were solemnised.</p>
        <p>The people stood mute, while Father Jehan placed them in two bodies, men and women apart, on either side of the church porch. Each person held a lighted candle, which flickered and guttered in the wind. Some of the older men had gone up to the castle with the priest, and now they headed the approaching procession, wailing out a monotonous dirge as they came, to the strains of the melancholy biniou. The grey sky was low; for early September the wind was keen, and Jeanne shivered under her long black cloak as she walked alone behind the bier._</p>
        <p>She was only seventeen, very tall and slender. It seemed sad that there was no fellow mourner to walk beside her, but the Countess's kindred had either fallen in the Italian campaigns or were already on their way to join the army levied for a new effort on the King's part to regain the Milanese. Rolland de la Touche had been sum- moned, but no answer had come from him ; he was thought to be in the Low Countries. The cross-bearer went 'first of all; then the acolytes, swinging censers, or bearing tall torch- like lights, preceded the curo and the chaplain, who walked before the bier.</p>
        <p>The seneschal of Longuedec, Leon. Berzevec, and Gillonne, the duenna of the Conntess's maids, had both tried to dissuade their young mistress from following the bier, but the young chatelaine said it was an imperative duty laid on her in the absence of any male relative. ,</p>
        <p>"It' is all she has left me to do for her," the girl said to Gillonne through her streaming tears. Now at the grave she was very calm. When all was over, she gave a last look into the grave, and walked away, closely muffled in her cloak, back to the Castle, followed by the chaplain and Ronne. Jamie had meant to say a few farewell words to her people, but she found that she could not trust her voice.</p>
        <p>As the tall, blaek-cloaked figure went uphill, the people strained their eyes to watch her till she had passed in at the Castle gate. She had been born and bred ainong them, and in their troubles and sicknesses she had never failed to come down the hill to help and comfort them, especially since her mother's prolonged illness had kept the Countess a prisoner to her room. A low, murmur of pity passed from one hard-featured villager to the other.</p>
        <p>"God be with her in all her ways!" said old Barbe Daoulas, who looked more like a bundle of rags than a woman. "There will soon be no one left to care for us ; the spirits of the departed will gather under the Castle roof-tree, wailing the days that are gone. Yes, neighbours, mark me, the glory of Longueclec has for ever passed away." Her voice had risen to a sort of high-pitched chant as she poured forth her lamentation. As it reached the deadened hearing of Father Jehan, an old man clad in dark-coloured breeches and jacket, a broad leathern belt, and gaiters reaching to his knees, with white hair falling to his shoulders, he held up his thin hand at her, and bade her cease. "Pace, woman! Get within to your distaff! This is no time to croak over what has ever been the common lot of humanity! Our chittelaine— God rest her soul !—was a pious and virtuous lady; but she had long suffered lingering pain and dis- comfort; mayhap she is taken from the evil to come." He turned away from Barbe to the crowd. "Brothers," he went on, in a shrill, cackling voice, "there is heavier trouble abroad; let us therefore stay at home, and keep wives and children quiet, so that we may not draw on our- selves the pillagers and maranders who ravage from one end of the land to the other, robbing and murdering all that comes within their ken. Let us bide quiet, I say." His outstretched hand did not change its attitude till the women and children had retired to their cottages. Then Jehan called out: "A word with you, Lao liergoel, and with you, Mathieu Lanleff.' Two tall: young men, with broad, rather sloping, shoulders, were garbed like Jehan himself in dark cloth jackets, waistcoats covered with buttons, with full trousers reaching to the knee, where they were met by leathern gaiters. As they sauntered slowly away from the rest with old Jeban, their black broad-leaved hats seemed precisely alike. Lao looked over his shoulder at the diggers as they shovelled the earth into the grave ;for the tomb in which lay the bones of Madame Kergrist's long line of ancestits was outside the church, though not far from the projecting apse at its eastern end.</p>
        <p>"The news the pedlar brought was true," Jehan said. "I was in Guingamp, yesternoon. The Count of Laval is sick—' a mischance while hunting,' 'tis said—and so he could not stand beside his lady at the Countess's grave." A grunt from Lao and a nod from Mathieu @hewed that they heeded him. "There is worse news than that, my masters—news that might well bleach your hair and furrow your cheeks before years, send a ploughshnre through them." Both the young men looked up with quickened interest. ‘ll-ews that our great Prince the Consisble of Bourbon has broken his allegiance to Xing Francis, and takes part with the D.mperor Charles—a murrain on him!—who has never loved our noble King." Mathieu looked unbelieving'. "'Tis a font slander!" he said defiantly. "Ily lord of Bourbon is the hest friend I ever kneiv. I'll list to no tales about him!" Old Jehan looked grave; he was accustomed to be listened to as an oracle in Longuedec. "I had forgotten you were an alien," he said loftily. "You have been reckoned among us ten years or so, Mathieu, but for all that you are not of us; I mind me you came from the Bourbonnais. I grant you Duke Charles has been a great and ruble man, and I was slow to 4oubtjairn ; vet mark</p>
        <p>tne "—tie snook ills open nan-a in Mathieu's face, and his voice rose to a shrill note---" Duke though he be, if he is the traitor men say, he is of no more account than a horse that throws its rider. cur that bites the hand that feeds it. We must look to ourselves, lads; as soon as this news gets noised abroad the country will swarm with cut- throat plunderers; till now they .have been kept somewhat in check by the very name of Bourbon, for was he not of kin to our late lord? I heard, too, that the army is on its way to Italy; our brave Baron of Coetfrec belike is on his way there, or he would have been here to-day. It was bad for Longuedec when he came into his inheritance of Orbec, so many leagues away. We can count on no one but ourselves. What have you to say, Lao?"</p>
        <p>Lao had been chewing a bit of straw while he listened; he answered doggedly: "Seeing is believing. When my lord of Bourbon is proclaimed a traitor at the Market Cross of Guingamp, and a price is set on his head, I will believe—not before, so help me God! Good faith! I can but wonder at you, Father Jehan, eager to swallow the trash of some pock-pudding, and next to belch it out, forsooth, for our bewilderment! But for your white hair, I would say 'tis a crying shame thus to befoul the noblest lord in the king- dom! My lord Constable has been a pattern to all, gentle and simple; a pattern husband and father too and now, forsooth, he being left a widow- man and childless, a certain Royal harpy—for she is no bette4r-seeks to beggar him, and you seek to spread the lies she has coined for his ruin!" _ .</p>
        <p>"Peace, peace, Lao; the Regent may not be defamed."</p>
        <p>"Look you, Father Jehan," Mathieu said; "tis but a month ago I was in Autun ; my uncle is seneschal to the Lord Bishop. Well, I was at the palace, and my uncle said: Be wary of your words till need comes to speak them, then say boldly that the Lord Bishop will answer for the Constable as he would for his own soul.'"</p>
        <p>Lao struck his hand- hard into Mathieu's palm. "It is a bargain, comrade!" he said cordially. "Sainte Croix! we will stand shoulder to shoulder for the good faith of my lord the Constable." Jehan crouched down on his staff, shaking his head in vehement disapproval as the younger man turned to him. "Look you here, Father Jehan you should offer proof: you cry treason, but you say not where it lies." • Mathieu and Lao had both served in battle under the Constable: they had been beyond the frontiers of France, and their outlook was Wider than that of a stay-at-home like Jehan; they had come home badly wounded, and for a while had been exempted from active service; but they no longer bowed implicitly to the dictates of the village oracle. Jehan was too angry to answer the last gibe; white with mortified pride, he shook his head and made for the door of his cottage. •</p>
        <p>. . . . . . . . Jeanne had gone to her own chamber without $ word to either of her companions. She had greatly' changed in outward appearance; she looked evee taller than her mother had been as she stood bl the Window gazing out on -the hills. Her bright dark eyes shewed traces of sorrow, but as she threw off her cloak and hood, her face and figure displayed all the beauty they had promised four years ago. Her hair, free from a coif, had golden threads amid its auburn as the light touched it. She was paler than usual to-day, but her skin was exquisitely clear and delicate ; her lips were redder and fuller, and had the same whimsical twist when she laughed; but her rounded chin looked firmer and more thoughtful, for Jeanne had known a full year of sorrow and runlet,' beside her mother's sick-bed.</p>
        <p>The responsibility thus forced on her—for On had been virtually chfitelaine since the beginning of her mother's illness—had rapidly developed the girl's character, and now, at seventeen, she was se old as she might have been five years later; her natural sauciness had not fled, but it was for the time hidden under this cloud of sorrow. , Her life now seemed to her empty; every moment for so many weeks had been devoted to her suffer ing mother, she had almost forgotten her marriage; for the Count de Laval at the end of the thre: years had been sent on a distant embassy, altu when at last he wrote to say he was coming to claim his bride Madame Kergrist was so near bet end that a delay had been asked for, so that Jean° might close her mother's eyes. Madame Kergrist caused the Count to be tr;-, formed of her approaching death when she.' that it was near; she thought it would comfort...het' child to be under her husband's protection. fate willed that this should not be, and that the girl must stand alone, the sole mourner, stl'ef mother's grave. Rolland de la Touche_ lasei.t°o revisited honguedec. Jeanne had sent to rf• o inform him of her loss. She was now Q1111161)4 Rolland, "Some day my letter will fin—lnto his hands. Oh, if he were here to go with me to Laval!" The Count de Laval had sent her a kind letter of condolence, but he told her that an accident while hunting prevented him from paying the last duties to her mother, as he was unable to mount a horse: He told her he was sending a litter and a sufficienr escort under the command of his cousin Etienne de Betz, so that she might be conveyed, with ba„ maids and the baggage she wished for, with due respect and honour to Laval. This letter had reached Lopguedec on the pre- vious day, and it announced the arrival of the escort for the morning after the funeral; her mourning she told herself must therefore be se', aside for the present. Jeanne felt like a stone, without spirit or energY ; she could not lie down and sleep as if this daY had been like its fellows. She sat thinking over the past. She had nothing to regret in quitting Longuedec. She took two portraits from the table; kissed that of Madame de Chateaubriand ; then, 3•4 she looked fondly at the man's portrait, which vg° that of the Constable of Bourbon, she said softlY: "Ah, noble heart—the noblest in France, I hare heard my father say—if only I could seek your counsel I should not feel so forlorn; I am sure you would befriend your poor little cousin!" When all was still in the Castle she took lamp and went to the chapel. Kneeling before tbe altar, where she had so often knelt beside her mother, she prayed for guidance in the new life about to open before her. She rose at last, conr forted and quieted, resolved to trust entirely to her husband's kindness and wisdom. ( To be continued.)</p>
        <p>I</p>
