rence would never be shown, be- cause at that particular point the road opened out there, being the corner of Rylend3-3treea Therefore the eoes. rnittee came to the conelu ohm, eefleieeri ea tile oeveraiee in the way of the new liar:, that they would aehere to the old one. It would be ridic lieu; west of rie oloy t attempt other:vie:3. The Meyoa thought it injulieinue to disculi the matter further, a 3 it was not settled, and mieht yet be settled eitiefecterily. The piens wars passed absolutely on the 60 feet line. The amendment wee not seceadel and the minutee were approval. THE CLO3INCi OF INeANII'ARY eVELLIN 13. LIVELY DEfIe.TE, COUN'eILLOR CeNNELL Had Ti) WiCillen IW. WARRING' CON IND THE ST. HELENS CORPORA.- TION BILL. At a rneetint,,ofatrialveint,,s;itche,nti7iditz of a pig m arket it WIA repolvel 4 itehatthea Ql"Teac3 00 -4teeet ruleketi ansl tiler, 'eel Tema Clisiik be instructed to take the n1C3531.1•57 13:)1di12,4 the the Medical aßaer of Heil,:h al unfit for hem an hah1a- ti,,,,,..,T2;.60,5248,ti0aunadea 3aor.epaesmfio,l%l7;sf,:i7N,. ilerfloguosfi Rousing rotfa the eh °Wu; rlks .1 na.Ti co Ire ewsoixh. 3,:(183b0a,,, bane 3rt, reportedei ,be; Nos. 156 178,, an 0 i 1T7°;,w1Nna;,F1.1.5115,;* PN7ielegr.sa'-yard, ed 93:, fow • 1. Narrow-weint. Town 29ald ne Town-hill! i; 5. e;1 ,) Nis c.. :89: r7l6r, 5,8,aT, 3nol. .aln 131;d, Bill ; Nos.- 4, 10, 1,3, and 5, W ainwright's-yard, Buttermarket-sereet , Noe. 1,2, and 3. Cheshire Cheese- yard, Winevick-street ; Nos. 1,2, 3,4, 5, and 6, Cheater's-yard, Winwickeetreet ; Nas. 1. 2, arel 3, Winwick-street ; Nos. 4,3, 7, and 11, Brown's-yard,Bayhorue-yard Winwick-street ; Nos. 1, 2. 3, and 4, Rvlande-vard, Foundry-street ; Nom. 24, 26, and 28. Foundry-street; Nos. 1, 2 and 3, Perrin's-yard ;N ). 2, Back D alienadane : Nes. 5), 52, 51, 53, 58, 1,2, airl 63, Geskelei-yard, Chlborne-ltreet ; Nos. 1,3. 5,7, an] 9, Hughes'-court ; N)3 2,4, 6,8, 10, 11, and 13, Cambria- place. Alderman Sur-rote moved the cenfirmetion of the miniree, ani enacted to the St Helene Corporation Bill. He said the petition had beau heard, and the operation ef the Bill suspended until the Local G wsraitisgt Board gave a certificate that the contemplated works for the dealing with the sewage in St. Helene hail been comple- ted satisfactorily, so that as far as the Cerooration of Warrington were concerned they had got all they esked for. (Hear, hear). The Corporation of St. Helena might have seen its way to have granted what was wanted without requiring any opposition from them in Parliament, and the expense would have been less than Ind bees incurred. He must express his surprise at the action of the Lancashire County Council. It was pretty well owing to their action that the C orporation wore in- dived to oppose the Bill,and very much to their surprise they found that the County Council had withdrawn the opposition to the Bill without consulting the Warrington Corporation on the subject, an] were sittieeel to receive from the St. Helens Corpention, either verbally or orally, an undertaking to carry out certain works in two years. It was not likely that this Corporation could be satisfied with such an undertaking. The County Council not only withdrew their opposition limit the County Council Meeical Officer of Health gave evidence, in hi a opinion, very much endanzering War- rington's chance of Success. What he said was very snrprising. He tried to convince the committee that the addition of some 8,000 pails of effluent to Sankey Brook would make no more nuisance than at present ex- isted. He thought they might congratulate themselves that the committee were not convinced, and that St. Helens could not now proceed with their arrangements for dealing with sewage until they obtained a certificate from the Local Government Board, The Alderman then proceeded to refer to the question of the uninhabitable houses. He said the Medical Officer of Health had strongly urged the committee to deal with all such dwellings throughout the borough, and the committee were bound by law to act on any report Dr. Gornall might submit to them. The committee agreed to his suggestion that it would be better to deal with the cases of one dietrict at once,so that on his presenting the present list the committee inspected all the properties, and they were unanimously of opinion that various pro- perties should be demolished or certain necessary alter- ations made. Other lists would be submitted at regular intervals. Alderman Roamers seconded, and in amplification of what the Chairman had just said, remarked that the position in regard to St. Helens was practically this - that four or five years ago St. Helens went in for an extension Bill, and in that Bill claims were inserted by a Committee of the House of Commons requiring St. Helens forthwith to complete ceatain works for the elimination of that which polluted Sankey Brook. From that time up to the present St. Helens had been carrying on experiments with a view to produce by mechanical precipitation an effluent which the Local Government Beard would con- sider satisfactory. Then they went toitho House of Commons for a further extension of the Bill and to con- vert the pail and midden system to the water carriage system, with the object of having the excrementitious matter turned into Sankey Brook,and yet the Sr. Helens Corporation felt outraged benause the Warrington Cor- poration objected to the addition of 15 tons of excre- monthlong matter into the brook, which was already in an abominable condition. (Hear, hear.) He thought that the Corporation of Warrington might congratulate themselves on the efforts which had been made for some time past to call the attention of St. Helens to the heinous offence upon them, and that it had at last been successful. He endorsed everything that Alderman Sutton had so well said about the action cif the Lancashire County Council. It was only another instance, to hie mind, of the abso- lute importance and the urgency for tee conversion of Warrington into a county borough at the earliest pos- sible moment. He did take the opportunity of telling the Chairman of one important committee of the Lanca- shire County Council that Warrington, rightly or wrongly,had the impression that it was not the slightest use to ask the Lancashire County Council for the slight. est assistance, no matter what their claims or their position might be, they were treated with something worse than contempt, and as an alliance had been made between the County Council and themselves, upon a joint report prepared by the Medical Officer of the County Council and their own Medical Officer, it was a question which required a great deal of explanation. What were the circumstances which induced the County Council to withdraw their onposition to the Bill, and to allow their own medical officer to give evidence prac- tically to the disadvantage of Warriegton ? Whether they got an explanation or not they had come to the conclusion that the sooner they got out of the hands of the County Council of Lancashire on all matters the better it would be for the prosperity of Warrington. (Hear, hear.) Councillor PIERPO/NT remarked that as a member of the County Council of Lancashire he quite concurred in the condemnation that had been imposed upon that body because he felt it was Tully deserved. At the same time he wished to say,in explanation of his own position, that he was not a member of the committee which had Ito do with the matter. He was quite of opinion that the County Council of Lancashire owed an explanation to him in reference to the position they had taken up. The MAYOR : It will need a great deal of 'explanation. Councillor Dr. CANNEL/. : I should like to know who the Medical Officer of Health (Dr.Gernall)is not present? We have a Medical Officer and a Deputy Medical Officer, and neither is present. I should like the chair- man of the committee to tell me whether DaGornall has had the permission of his committee to leave, because I have some serious matters to go through io-night, and they are both absent. I wish to allude to the wholesale condemnation of poor working men's property. I find that in going from my house up Buttermaiket-et. that houses 9,7, 5,3, and 1 are condemned in the Narrow</p>
