        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JULY 8, 1898.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JULY 8, 1898.</p>
        <p>Weint. There is a through draught, and lam prepared to tell the Council and their Medical Officer of Health, or any other Medical Officer of Elealth, that they ought not to interfere with them, and that they are perfectly satisfactory. There is one point. They have not got sufficient air space, hut you have no power to interfere. Then there is Wainwright'-yard in Butter- market-street. There inay be a little improvement needed here, but are we to be simply the toots of the officials and of no one else ? We have none of the offi- cials enecoraed here, and I want to go into the matter seriatim. I have got Wain wright'e yard and the Little Weitst. Tom are hous here that lam prepared to defend for the poor people of Warrineton. No report has been wesented to the Cruncil and I propose as an amendment that the minute relating to the houses be deferred for another month, so that the Medical Officer can be here to answer for himself. _ 1</p>
        <p>Tha Tow/ CLERIC : Irv) owlet propos. any amend- ment to that puticular minute. _ _ "</p>
        <p>()weenier Dr. CANNELL : Where is he? The Tows CLERK :1 Arn awry to say he is unwell. Orminelllor Dr. CANNELL : Does anyone know where he hi.</p>
        <p>Alderman ST/TTOI*T : The Deputy Medical Officer of Health wrote.rne to say that his father was so unwell that hs had been obliged to rto from home. He has gone to Scotland. but his son is undertaking his duties during his alrence.</p>
        <p>Councillot Dr. CANNELL : N 4 Offii%l of the C.)rpora- tion hts a rizht 0 louse dutieA without p.rrnission from his committee, and Dr. G)rnall hag nut had per- mission and--</p>
        <p>Atieral tfl SUTTON YOU cant say sn. Councillor Dr. CANNELL : But I want to know wbero he has gone. Tun fidDkvoa : lam afrail we cannot give you any better information. Councillor Dr. CANNEL : I want to kw-- _</p>
        <p>The -MAYOR : I did not know he was away. The Tows CLERK: Councillor Cennell cannot propo,se an amendment. The law prescribes the manner in which procee Brigs shell be taken,and upon the report of the Medics! Officer of elealth the committee arc bound to take steps. Councillor De. CANNELL : You need make no excuse. I want to know where Dr. Gornall has gone. The TOwN CrAtue : lam not makin; any esemso. Alderman BOLTON seconded the amendment. The Tows CLERIC ruled that Aldermen Bolton Was Out Of order, and that ha could:not seoond the amend- ment. The Myron : You can speak to the resolution, but you c innot second the amendment. It is qnite useless. Alderman BOLTON : If you don't want an amendment there c on he a negative. The TOWN CLERK: You can vote against it of course. Ahilrrnvi BOLTON : Ole reaeon why I seconded the amendment is this, that I consider it a great hardship to condemn these houses in this wholesale manner. This is not the ordinary way of doing business, and not a single report has been presented to us as members of the Council to tell us why these houses have been con- deraned. Simply a man goes round, takes a walking stick and says" This house is wrong, and that." A re- . Dort to show why the houses are not habitable ought to be laid on the table. To condemn property wholesale in this manner without a reason is neither fair to the Coun- cil, to the property owners, nor to the tenants. Aldermen BrAcarturesr : I should like to know if any Drovieion has be m made for the housing of the people ? If they are to be turned out wholesale in this manner you outht to make some prevision for them. Councillor PLUUPTON : I should like to be allowed to seY, as one who has visited the property, that I don't think there is a house in the whole list that is fit for hurnia hebiteion in its present state. _</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON : the renort then ? Councillor PLUMPTON Councillor Dr. Canned has pleaded the cause of the working classes, but anyone who pleads their can-e must condemn this property. (Rear, hear.) I don't think that it is the poor he is look- ing after, but the interests of the property owners. Councillor 19 7. CANNRILL rose to spoilt, but was re- ceived with cries of 'Chair, chair." Councillor PLIMPTON : I will tell Councillor Dr. Cannell that the property is not condemned on the evid- ence of one man. The Medical Officer briag,s his report to the Health Committee, they consider it, and then three, four. five, or six thembers visit the propsrty.They don't go into the street only, they go into the houses. I have been through house, after house, and they were in an abominable state.</p>
        <p>Councillor Dr. CA-NNELL I rise to a point of order. These houses are rorrectly healthy and perfectly clean, and for Councillor Plu!npton to say that th,l. ar,. filthy is an ahominanle and deliberate lie. (Cries of "Chair," and "Order.") The MAYOR: Withdraw it.</p>
        <p>Councillor Dr. CANNELL : I shall not withdraw it. l'he MAYOR : I expoct to hav'. the supprt of the Connell. (fletr,llear, and cries of "Withdraw.") I shall leave the chair if--</p>
        <p>Dauncill9r Dr. CANgstr. : I shall tot withdraw it, be- cause it is a deliberate lie.</p>
        <p>Couneillar PLUDEPTON : Ho saya again it is a deliberate lie.</p>
        <p>Councillor Dr. CANNEL', I maintain it. ("0h,0h.") Alderman ROBERTS objected to the use of such language. The MAYOR : It is simply abominable. Councillor PLUMETON : I said the property in its present state was not fit for human habitation. Councillor Dr. CANNELL : And I Say it is.</p>
        <p>Councillor PLUMPTON: Do you want to gain something in your constituency ? (Continuing) Councillor Plump. ton particularised some of the houses he had visited,and said that the stench in some ef the houses was frightful. It was impossible to improve some houses. Councillor CANNELL : I challenge that remark—(cries of _" Chair, chair.")</p>
        <p>Councillor PLIMPTON : I cannot for the life of me think that Councillor Cannel! has visited these kisses. Councillor Dr. CANNSLL : Fair play is fair play and—</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : I shall not allow you to speak, (Cries of " Chair, chair.") It is too bad altogether. _ _</p>
        <p>, Councillor Dr CANNELL : I call attention to two points —Wainwright's-yard and the Little Weintin Buttermar- ket-street.</p>
        <p>Th e MAYOR 13s as quick as you can. Councillor Dr. Coma. was endeavouring to continue his speech when _ _</p>
        <p>CoUscilbr WOODS rose and, addressing the Mayor said : If he cannot recognize your authority I and several others below me will leave the Council Chamber and leave.Dr,Cannell to himself,</p>
        <p>Councillor Dr. CANsELL: I always:bow to the Mayor's decision.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: I should like to say to Councillcr Cannell that there are one or two words that he ought to with- draw. (Hear, hear). He ha charged Councillor Plump- ton with telling a deliberate lie. Now that language is quite unparliatnentary. Councillor Dr. CANNEL'.: I withdraw anything that I have said that is unparliamentary. (Hear, hear). The MAYOR : I am glad to hear you say that. Councillor Dr. CANNELL But when a man gets tin like Councillor Plumoton—(Cries of " Chair, chair," during which Dr. Cannell resumed his seat). Councillor SHAW GREEN said however much it had coot to reach the present condition of things with regard to St. Helens it had been money wisely expended. The information which had been given that evening proved that Sankey Brook had been made into a nuisance by St. Helens. They knew Councillor Pierpoint was not afraid to hold his own in the County Council, and he ought`to try to ascertain how it was that Warrington had been given away in the manner it had been by the officials of the County Council. Thera was another matter ha would like to mention. Deputations were being sent out of town at considerable expense, and he should like them when they returned to present a written report. Councillor Dr. CANNELL : Not necessary. sir. Councillor Slum Gnus : It may not be necessary, but it is advisable. (Hear, hear.) Councillor BzbarErr said the suggestion of Councillor Shaw Green was an admirable one, as the public ap- peared to have the wrong impression that the deputations were mostly of the nature of picnics. (Laughter.) The best way to disabuse the public of that erroneous hn- pression would be for the Chairman or Vice-Chairman to tell the Council what had been done. (Hear, hear.) Then they could See that the deputations wore really useful. With regard to Sankey Brook,Councillor Bennett said the result of the opposition to the St. Helena Bill showed that when the Corporation had the pluck to act upon its csnyictioos, in nine cases out of ten it would have a satisfactory ending. (Hear, hear.) Four or five years ago they were told that the Corporation was Powerless to deal in the matter, but they had now suc- ceeded, and he congratulated the Council on the fact. I Councillor Dr. Caiimati, again rose to speak and to submit his amendment, but the Mayor ruled that he vrati out of order. The MAYOR : You cannot speak. You are out of Order. I am sorry to have to tell you so often. Councillor Dr. CANNELL : I propose the amendment. The MAYOR: It is out of order and cannot be put. Alderman ROBERTS, in the absence of the Chairman of the Committee, replied to the questions raised.—Reeir• ing to Councillor Bennett, he said that if it had not been for the fact that St. Helens had been going in for an Act of Parliament. the Corporation of Warrington would have had to take an entirely different process. It would also have been more expensive, and would not have been half as satisfactory. They could only have taken an action in the High Court to restrain by injunction the people of St. Helens from contaminating the brook. Alluding to Councillor Shaw-Green's suggestion, he re- marked that he had only been with one deputation, but the amount ot information obtained at the Health Con- gress repaid many times over the expenses incurred. Councillor Dr. CANNELL : Nonsense. (Laughter). Alderman ROBERTS: It would perhaps be difficult to convey the infermation adequately to the mental capacity of the doctor, but still I am Speaking of the average members of the Council. (Laughter.) Continu- ing, Alderman Roberts said that in regard to the terrible scene which had ended in a calm, he was glad that the doctor had at last come to his proper state of mind, and had made the apology which was most desirable in the ' interests of good order. The recolution with regard to the insanitary houses was simply in the direction in which it was required to be dratted for the purposes of</p>
        <p>the Act. It did not follow that everyone of the lumen scheduled would be perfectly clos,d. When the notices were served it would be seen what was defective in the property. AldSrman BOLTON : They diin't in fly case.</p>
        <p>Aldermen ROBBRTS (continuing) said, that property capable of being repaired and inhabited would be re- paired and inhabited, and the order to close would have to be given by the magistrates at the ?aline Cour,where the property owners concerned would have an opportun- ity if they liked of showing why the property should not be closed. The duty of the committee was clear, and defined, and no man' whatever his social position,should inhabit a house, either by himself, or with his wife and family, which was unfit for human habitation. That duty the committee endeavoured to carry out as courts - ously and in sympathy with the parties involved, but without fear or favour. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were then put to the meeting and passed, Alderman Bolton dissenting.</p>
        <p>SANITARY WORKS COtimITTEE—MORE EXTEN- SIONS AT LONGFORD. At a meeting of the above committee it was resolved that the Deputy-Chairman, Councillor Shillcock, and the Engineer be appointed to attend the annual congress of the Sanitary Institute,to be held in Birmingham,and the annual cone,ress of the Royal Institute of Public Health, to be held in Dublin. Councillor MoNxs.in moving the adoption of the min- utesourplainad with regard to the acceptance of the ten- der of Mr. It. W. Collin for the erection of office, com- mittee room and dwelling-house at Longford depot at £395 that the matter had been brounht forward by the committee with some hesitation,bocause they had never liked to come in the middle of the year with the proposal to spend such an amount of naoney,but they were forced to do so for two reasons. It would be within the recollec- tion of the Council that they were authorised to build a wash-house and messroein for the men at the two depots. That seemed to arouse the jealousy of the men at Long- ford, who worked under conditions even worse than the other men, and there was an agitation for a similar privilege to be extended to them. It had been intended to utilize the present committee rooms by turning them into a mess room amd bath room, and so quarters would have to be found for the two clerks and foremen. It had long been known that the house was not fit for habita- tion. It was defectively built,and even with cement and slate it had been found impossible to keep it dry. Those two things had rather compelled the committee to bring their present proposal forward. This expense of £395 of Genre@ was not provided for in their estimates, but they thought they.could save something on the year which would go tovvards the amount,and the Borough Treasurer had told him that at the end of the year he would be able to see his way to make up the remaining portion. It would, therefore, not be a very serious matter from a financial point of view,and would impreve thelconditions under which the men at Longford carried on their work and the foreman and his family lived. Councillor Joint Eveas seconded the adoption of the minutes.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR inquired what position the cottage would occupy. Councillor MONKS said it was at the near entrance to the approach road leading to the destructor furnaces. The MAYOR asked if it was 10te0de4 to do away with the old cottage. Cannelllor MoNirs said it had not bean decided. The MAYOR said it seemed to him that it would be as well if it were taken down. COLIEICitiOr MONKS : I think so, Councillor GRACE wished to know how much money had been spent by the committee out of the money they had not got the sanction of the Llcal Government Board Vet to spend. Some 14 or 15 mouths ago the committee came before the CAincil wishing to spend £16,000, and the Local Government Beard was written to and asked for sanctioe to borrow the money. Se far as his know- ledge went,that sanction had not yet been obtained. The information from an outside source was that a large amount of that money bad been expended, and to his mind that was very unwise and a very unsatisfactory way in which to transact the business of the Corpora- tion. Not very long ago—some eight or nine months—a certain town'council in Lancashire were carrying out similar sanitary operations and before they obtained the sanction of the Local Government Board for the borrow- ing of the money they spent it, and when they went be fore the Local Government Board the sanction was re resed,and the money spent was thrown on the year's ex penditure. fle did not wish to see their Committee in a similar position. Councillor MONKS ire reply, said the committee had spent £1,400 out of the £16,000 estimated, and every item had been submitted to the Council. This £395 was on a different footing, and would come out of the year's revenue. On going over the proposals submitted two years ago they found that with further experience,some alterations were necessary,and the committee was going through the proposals again,with,a view to re-submitting them to the Council. The minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>PAVING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE. The minutes were of a form It char. Acter. The MAYOR proposed their adoption. Councillor HEATON seconded, and it Was carried.</p>
        <p>smoor. ATTENDANCE COAMITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE NEW BYL&amp;Ws.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the above committee, the Town Clerk submitted an order of her Majesty in Council approving of the new bylaws ma de by the committee. The minutes wore approved, on the motion of Coun- cillor Saws' Grant, seconded by C ouncillor SHILLCOCK. SUGGESTED NEW CATALO GUE FOR THE REFERENCE LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>- - • At a meeting of the Museum, Lihrary,Arta, and Tech- nical Instruction Committee, various gifts were reported and the list of new books purchased submitted. It was resolved that application be made to the Lancaahire Sea Fisheries Committee for a loan of the " Fisheries Exhi- bition," and that the Chairman of the Parr Hall and Gymnasium Committees be added to the Art Gallery Sub-Committee for the purposes of the exhibition. The new Catalogue of the Reference Libtary having been submitted by the Librarian, it was resolved.. That this committee have pleasure in noting the satisfactory manner in which the catalogue has been compiled, and lin recording their appreciation of the excellent work I which Mr. Madeley has perfor:ned in connection there- with." Alderman RODEUTS, in proposing the adoption of the I minutes, said they had had a good many gifts during the month, but he thought there was one, that from Mr Tunstall, which was worthy of special mention. He had presented them with a collection of British birds' eggs, to the number of 623, and a cabinet. He (Alderman Roberts knew that it had taken Mr. Tunstall some years to collect them, arid they would form a very valuable addition to the pretty good ;collection they had in that branch of natural history. Councillor WOODS had very great pleasure in adding testintonY to the valuable gift from Mr. Tunstall,and he was sure those who did value that kind of work would be very interested in it indeed. Th,re tvas one point he I would:like to draw attention to. He would suggest that the Chairmon of the Parr Hall Committee and the Chair- man of the Gymnasium Committee should join the Museum Committee and give them some assistance in the proposed Art Exhibition. Councillor BENNETT said he quite approved of that sug: gestion. He mentioned that in the library th -re were 2,599 local books and 750 local manuscripts. It was gratifying to know that with their industrial prosoerity they had not been neglectful of the intellectual side of</p>
        <p>life. Councillor Sum:fumy asked when they were going to have a new catalogue for the lending department of the library. The one in use, with all the new books, was ncomptete. Alderman ROBERTS said that if the Cowacil could see its way to vote a subscription for a few years the com- mittee would be glad to provide a new catalogue. would cost £3OO or £4OO, and they had no A c taloaUe -aans of refunding themselves except by selling the cmoepies at a ridiculously low price. That money had to come out of the money allotted to them under the rate. They were tvihe only committee of the Council which was absolutely rtuous in regard to the question of expendi- ture. (Laughter.) They had no power to spend money. They had to cut their coat according to their cloth, and they bad not enough cloth generally. They had a cata- logue compiled some years ago, since which time they had had a supplement printed, and from that time they bad to rely upon written sheets containing the names of new books. They contemplated revising the catalogue after some few years' time. The minutes were approved. CEMETERY COMMITTEE. it was reported at a meeting of the above committee, held on the 27th June, that the receipts for the past four weeks were £9O Is. 9d. ; corresponding period last year, 1£75 17s. 101.—On the motion of Alderman Harrison, seconded by Councillor John Evans, the minutes were I confirmed.</p>
        <p>TOWN HALL, 'AR % AND BATHS COMMITTEE. The members of the above committee met on the 16'th June, when it was reputed that the income for the past four weeks was £7l 184. 10d., against £75 103 for the corresponding period last year, and a decrease of £3 us. 2d.—Allorman B 9 lton moved the adoption of the minutes.—Alderman Harrison seconded, And it was carried.</p>
        <p>OTHER COMSaTTEES. . _ '</p>
        <p>The minutes of the G.te, Rivers,Fire Engine,and Water Committee. were passed without comment.</p>
        <p>NEW MEMBER OF THE GYMNASIUM COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION proposed the adoption of the min- utes of the Gymnasium Committee, and remarked, that owing to a clerical error, one or two resolutions of the committee had been omitted from the agenda. One was the resignation of Councillor Smethurst,and the appoint- ment of Councillor James Evans in his place.—Council- tor SMETHURST seconded the adoption of the minutes, which was agreed to.</p>
        <p>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE-MR. W. IL LONODIN.</p>
        <p>The Meson proposed the adoption of the minutes of this Committee and Councillor Smethurst seconded.</p>
        <p>Alderman ROBERTS said he thought it would not be out of place if they wished Mr. H. W. Long,,din, pros- perity and success in his future career, and congratu- lated him on obtaining the appointment. He was a child of their own in the sense that he had been brought up in the office of the Corporation. He was a credit to the training he had received, and Alderman Roberts moved that they ad gl a resolution expressing their appreciation of Mr. Longdin's services. The MAYOR seconded, and after some remarks of a similar nature from Councillor Heaton, the resolution was passed. THE PLAYGROUND FOR SANKEY BRIDGES— QUESTION FRO COUNCILLOR BURTON.</p>
        <p>Councillor BURTON aske'd the Chairman of the Open Spaces Committee "Whether the Open Spaces Committee have secured a piece of land near Sankey Bridges for a playground, if net, are they still negotiating for the same, or have the negotiations fallen through, and if so, for what reason." Ile said they were doubtless aware that previous to the district of Sankey Bridges being included in the Borough, the Parish Coun- cil were negotiating for a piece of land, and he had no doubt that if the rforoueh had not been extending in that direction,and the Parish Council been still in exist- ence,that they would have secured this piece of land long sincts.He considered the land suitable for the pur- pose for which it was intended, and seeing that it was offered as a gift he was surprised that the Open Spaces Commit'ee had net been able to take it overlie had con- tinually been asked by people in the district and mem- bers of the late Parish Council how it was that the land had not been taken over,'and he had not been in a posi- tion to answer the question. That was the reason be now brought it up, and he hoped the Chairman of the Open Spaces Committee would be able to answer them that if the committee had not already taken the land over, it was their intention to do so in the near future,or provide a playground equally as large. Tee MATOrt said he could give some explanation about , the matter. In March 1896 they thought the conditions were rather more than they could fall in with. It was his intention to calla meeting at a very early date, and he thought it more than likely they would re-consider the offer. They had not lost sight of it, and he hoped they would be able to re-open the question with Lord Lilford or Mr. Selby,and perhaps these gentleman would gee their way to grant a little more concesgion. It was the street-making and fencing question that caused the matter to be put off. He thouelfc the matter was worthy of re-consideration,and that the people of S Inkey Bridges were entitled to a playground. This was all the business and the Council then rose.</p>
