      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>INSIDE AND OUTSIDE.</head>
        <p>INSIDE AND OUTSIDE.</p>
        <p>Fr is the fellow who stands inside the house and looks out through tho window that most enjoys the storm. How jolly to see the passers-by struggling against the wind or scudding before it, their coat collars up to their ears, their bats jammed down on their heads, slapping and slashing over the flooded pavements, soaked to ths skin, maybe,cursing the weather with what breath they may have left, as they pelt and writhe along the road. Great fun, I say, this is for the man who looks out through the window. Not his are the wet clothes "clinging like cerements," as poor Tom Hood said about the girl who bad ended her sorrows, as so many do, in the Thames. Not his the able-bodied chance of catching a bad cold and getting floored with rheumatism or pneu- monia. Oh, no; he is on the right side of the brick wall, sang as a bug in a rug, and dry as a bone. So he just fills himself up with what is probably one of the meanest forms of delight, the enjoyment of witnessing the miseries of others. You may not believe it, but I know people who take a distinct and positive pleasure in at' tending funerals for the sake of looking upon the grief of the afflicted and hugging the idea that it is somebody else and not they themselves who is being macerated and ground up in tlaelmerciless mill of sorrow. On the same lines we may regard strong, healthy per- sons ; they who have been hearty as pigs all their lives; they who can eat like hungry horses and sleep like policemen off duty; who have scarce ever had a pang or pain,and fancy that illness in others is a culpable weak- ness,if not an obsolute sin. I have a notion to add that chronically well people are the coldest blooded wretches on earth. There ! I have said it, and it's true. If the reader is one of them, he can skip the following letter if he likes. Thera are plenty more to whom it will be an advantage to peruse it carefully. " For over ten years," says the writer, " I had suf- fered from indigestion and weakness. I felt weak, low, and heavy. I had no appetite, and when I sat down te the table, often I could not touch a morsel of food. After meals I had excruciating pain at the chest, through to the back: also a gnawing constant pain under the shoulder blade. I had frequent bilious attacks being so sick that I could not lift my head from the pillow. " At night I was restless, getting no proper sleep ,and in the morning feeling worse tired than when I went to bed. I was mush swollen about the waist, and was constantly belching wind. I get gradually weaker and wasker,it being a misery to gst about. I continued like this for ten years, never being well, and not; knowing what it was to enjoy my meals. I saw • doctor after doctor (I am sure that I consulted twenty doctors), who gave me medicines,but I received no benefit from them. They did not *Mt my case.</p>
        <p>"I gave up all hope of ever getting well again, and wished that I was dead, always fretting at my weak state. In April of last year (189 t a pamphlet was sent me by post, and I read about Mother Siegel's Curative Syrup. My symptoms were accurately described in this Small book, and I determined to try it.</p>
        <p>" I sent to Mr. Coppin's Stores, Maraham Street, Westminster, for a.bottle of this medicine. After tak- ing it a few days I found it was doing me good. I could eat and enjoy my food, which agreed with me. This gave ins confidence, and I continued with it, and soon all the pain left me, and I felt as strong and well as ever I did in my life. I felt brighter, and could eat anything, nothing coming amiss. I have since kept in good health, taking an occasional dose when required. You can make what use you like of this statement, and refer anyone to me. (Signed) (Mrs.) Annie White, 117, High Road, New Southgate, London, N., August 10th, 1897." This lady is the wife of Mr. H. White, butcher, New Southgate. In Private conversation they both speak very enthusiastically of Mother Seigel's Syrup. Mrs. six White says that although during her long illnesa she could ou ly walkad that t all, she can nsw walk miles th t is not half a bad tramp even without fatigue. An for a woman who has never been ill. Many of them cannot do as much. That our correspondent can is a striking illustration of the power of the medicine that cured her. I who write these lines have not always had tho luck to be looking out of the window at other people fighting the bad weather. I have been bowed down under che tempest of sickness and pain myself. And so have millions. And it is re who syMpathise with Mrs. White and rejoice with her on her recovery.</p>
        <p>Etscrusis PATENTS.—The following patents amongst others were applied for, and published in the" Official Journal of Patents," during the week ending June 25th, 1898. No, 13,783 S. H. and W. HUMGOTI, of Rochdale, for" Improvements applicable to ring-spinning and doubling frames."-21st June. 13,839.—J. H. Cooke, E. H. Stansby, and Balstone, Cooke and Ca. Ltd., Men. cheater, for "Improvements in bindings or ecleinza for garments and the like."-22nd June. 1893.-13,931 A. and J. Turner, and H. F. Clough, of Southport, for the "improved composition for the protection and preserve- 'tion of metal or other surfaces."-23rd June. 13,744 E. Hollingworth, Dobcross, for "Improvements in looms for weaving."-21st June. 13,849. W. Scholes, Fails. worth, for" Improvements in signalling appliances for uee on railways and in coal mines, factories and other places."-22nd June. 13,927. R. Hoyle, Manchaster,for "Improvements connectea with the drying of China clay and other substances of like nature."-23rd June. 14,055 J. Hiehton, Castleton, for "Improved under- neath shedding motion for all kinds of weaving looms.' —2sth June 1898. List supplied by George Davies, C.E and Son, Chartered Patent Agents, 4, St. Ann's Square Manchester.</p>
