      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>JULY Bth, 1891.</head>
        <p>JULY Bth, 1891.</p>
        <p>A New Tug Unionist M.P.'s from Ireland, Irish Party., who have spoken and voted against the Government on the tlancial relations between their country and 't Britain, are now in a singular predicament. Government they support has declined to 'an to their demand, and they are therefore in ,416 Position of men who must either abandon their 14)117ictions or abandon the agitation. We do not %,1)38a, they will do the former, because the con- are too deeply rooted, and the hope of tlelal. gain is too great and general.llenre they Le e°lnpelled to form a now party, by whatever tVae they choose to call themselves. This has been 4.7.ians to all shrewd observers from the moment ii(;,7lllandlords,merchants,and representative bodies rael into line with the Nationalists on the 1E714 ef the finance of the Union, the promises of Owe A 'Let of Union, and the existing anomalies and Lt29llalitiee. The independent Uuionists, as they 14 3' Prefer to be called, represent the landowners, a tenants, and the large majority of the tax- Yers. They have been boldly informed that a ° ,talnon basis, and a common purse, and a coin- taxable capacity, are the New Unionism, and CeaY decline to give their sanction to it. It will tt interesting to see what will follow. We wla..a.taa that the agitation will go on, and that it taliihrlerease in force and extent. But we may be d staken, and men like Colonel Saun- tersou L. and Mr. Horace Plunkett, Mr. i ee-Y and Mr. Carson, may subside, submit to the , ties and as they may think it to be, and cease ~ clamour for reductioas and subsidies equal to 'uatwee lob n two and three millions a year. Party tY may crush out their independence and 4 leate their excursion into the domain of the leitators for revision and "justice to Ireland," tWi° strong reasons occur to us why this is unlikely. Ithe arst is that Irishmen are always in earnest it an there is any money to be got, and success in illg an equivalent grant for rating relief to ,thrleulture will not quiet but rather embolden 41144 to go further. The second is that the most t%eaeaful movements, politically, in Ireland have 4:llnearly always mixed in character. The original tttaln,a Rule movement, consequent upon the dis- . ,uliehment of he Irish Church, was composed .14 4,4 ationalists, Tories, and Orangemen,who agreed 'lll4' 4„neW agitation because they were successful 14 e one side and beaten on the other. Accord- predict the formation of a new Irish Aithli a party of independent Unionists, in line kb: the Nationalists on every point but the /14;:x.ate Parliament. This is the least that can tlfCen, and the most moderate result of a checked 111t-hat'T`l• But there may well be something more. 'rieh landlords and the Irish taxpayers, by tikiiverwhelrning majority should come to see tall ea and Home Rule policies can be united, Protwthat there is no way to obtain what was laiie laed but by making common cause for self- he limits of Unionism will begin to contract, ettijaetiomic motives may blend with Nationalist elit.linents, and the present agitation may be tlltill'alY transformed. These are possibilities, and tirdeeel-headed politician cannot afford ,to disre- 04,1ittheta. The attitude of the present Govern- trb4,! in short, is a direct incitement to the % t'ion of an independent Unionist party, as well the formation of a new Nationalism, con- trag,l that economic salvation lies in self-rule and 4k44‘13 a costly connection, maintained with effort, 4116 Leaasing to represent the views and wishes of °eat persons in Ireland.</p>
        <p>,rea, 't4e</p>
        <p>liate% Cr</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>IT has been said that it „ nootaing, is premature to draw many 1:441, practical lessons from the tleti tin progress, and one or two obvious de- -44,;.°113 only have been current. It is clear that wait awhile, until we can judge of several</p>
        <p>' ktuit</p>
        <p>things. But the Americans are a go-ahead people, and they are beginning to do,on an extensive scale, what we have in this country declined to do—to regard the Spanish navy as representing a first- class Power, and its defeat as establishing the American navy's superiority over European navies which are first-class. It is perfectly natural. We learnt some valuable lessons from the former navy war in America, and we shall be certain to I earn others from what has now been going on. We may as well be frank, however, and say that we cannot accept everything the Americans claim for their ships, and their guns, and their fighting. They have established their superiority to Spain, but Spain was a third or fourth class naval Power, anti what has occurred in fighting Spain would not have happened in fighting Germany or Italy, leaving France, Russia, and Great Britaia entirely out of the question. The Spanish ships were poor in quality and badly handled. Their guns were incapable of doing any damage at ordinary naval ranges. As for the torpedo fleet,it seems to lupe been inferior, obsolete, and most inefficiently handled.poes any sane man imagine that either at Manila or Santiago first-class torpeto-boats,handled by French or German officers, would not have succeeded in blowing up two or three of the best ships opposed to them? Lord CaAILLE3 BERES- FORD will some day give his opinion as to what British torpedo boats would have done under these conditions. British ships are not in clues- ' tion, except as the Americans contend that their ships would have done as well against them as they did against the Spanish ships. The assump- tion is one of remarkable audacity. It is not war- ranted by the facts. We n'ied say no more on the point, because we think boasting should not be mot by boasting. But when details are given as to armour and guns and design we may be excused for saying that the Americans are boasting too much. We could match any of their vessels, without coming off second bast in a combat. Our battleships and cruisers are as good as they mike them, and it is unfair to com- pare a first-class American with a second-class The lesions drawn as to British vessel. As to gun power, we have no and building are rather wild. reason to be alarmed. not build any of our battleships for futureAni eri cda de ga lads less money than we d..). It cannot even build merchantmen and liners. The coming boom in American orders for naval vessels must make the Elswick and Fairfield people rather humorously inclined. The fact is that American armour- plating in experimental tests, has been time after time beaten by our own guns, and American guns are no better than ours. Our ships r.re built for Imperial purposes, with groat coal-carrying caps- city. American ships neither carry so much coal nor travel so fast. Their yards have turned out many failures, and their luccess against inferior ships does not justify sweeping deductions as to what would happen if there were a duel between, say, the Alabama and our Majestic. It would be wiser for the Americans to " blow " a little less just now. But they are Americans, and we must expect them to imagine that, in ships and guns, they can "lick creation" because they have smashed two of the very inferior fleets of Spain.</p>
        <p>The Vicar of Rencoin (Ray. Canon Maitland Wood) nas taken occasion in the current number of the " Parish Magazine" to deliver a little lecture on the blessedness of Voluntary schools. His chief argument in support of Voluntary schools as against Board schools seems to be that of cost. The Voluntary schc,ols, he contends, are saving the ratepayers of Runcorn sixpence in the £ and should therefore receive the assistance of those who have to pay rates. Even supposing that Voluntary schools in Rune Jrn save the amount indicated it may be very seriously ques- tioned whether the cheapest article is the best in the end. Education is too precious a thing in these days of keen competition with foreign rivals to be weighed continually in the economical balance. But studying further the worthy Canon's remarks we find that he is astute enough to omit any mention of the additional grant to Voluntary schools which has been given by the present Government, and denied, in part, te the Board schools. Under the recent Act the Volun- tary schools of Runcorn must be receiving some hundreds of pounds yearly from the Imperial Ex- chequer, and although it may be true that the Voluntary schools managers are unable to dip into local ratepayers' pockets, it is also true that they are receiving an additional sum—exceeding that of the Board Schools—from the general body of taxpayers, which is pretty much the same thing. It is perhaps not surprising that the manufac- turers and tradespeople of Runcorn are loth to give afurther subvention for the maintenance of a system of education which is far from popular or efficient,and which, after all, is chiefly used for sectarian purposes. ----- Cyclists across "the herring pond " receive a lot more attention from the authorities there than their brother wheohnen do in this country. The excessive importance of the cyclist in the States has both advantages and disadvantages, though the latter are only just coming to light. From an English point of vie w,cyclists in America have fre- quently been regarded as remarkably lucky mortals. We hear of cycle-tracks along the highway sacred to the wheelman,and oth sr such luxuries unknown and undreamt of in the more staid and slow- moving mother country—and sigh enviously. Bt another view of the question is afforded by the latest development of cyclomania in America. Brother Jonathan has hit upon an idea for con- ferring a dual benefit on cyclist and pedestrian at the same time. A court of law has just given a decision averse to the use of dropped handle-bars on cycles. Handle-bars in which the drop is greater than four inches below the seat are regarded as dangerous to pedestrians as well as to cyclists, and severe penalties are to be inflicted on all per- sons violating the regulations which have been made to restrict their use. The danger to the foot passenger seems obscure on first thoughts. Yankee legislators, however, conceive the evil to be in the:fact (?)that the cyclist with a handle-bar dropped below the specified limit cannot see more than 18 to 20 feet further than his nose, and so is liable to run into unsuspecting members of the public. Cyclists in America, we fancy, must con- tinually keep their"noses to the wheerif such care has to be taken of them. While admitting that a very low handle may also be physically injurious, the cycling fraternity here would raise a great I outcry if their choice of position awheel was fixed by a court of law for reasons such as actuate the legislatOrs of America.</p>
        <p>Bye-elections just now, are events of almost weekly occurrence, and the result of each has so far provided much food for thought and specula- tion But the Durham result is not one of any part icular significance, though it may give unbounded gratfiication to the Tories. The conversion of a Liberal majority into a Liberal minority is such a rare occurrence just now, that it would be idle to expect anything but Conservative rejoicings at Durham's change in political representation. In the Liberal camp, not much attention is paid to he failure of Mr. Boyd to fill the vacancy caused by the death of the late Mr. Fowler,as the balance of political power in Durham City is so even that the result was not likely to cause surprise in any eventuality. The late members' strong personality, and popularity with all sections of the community, was the principal reason for Dur- ham's representative being a Liberal, while Mr. Boyd's attitude on the Temperance question caused the abstention of quite sufficient votes, which would otherwise have been Liberal, to give victory to the Hon. A. D. Elliot, the nominee of the Conservative party. We should imagine that</p>
        <p>Ito consider Durham's return to Conservatism a vote of confidence in the Government, would be the last thing even the most confirmed Tory would dream of doing.</p>
        <p>Death-dealing inventions still occupy the time and minds of many people in all parts of tho globe, but the majority of them after once being exploited are consigned to the limbo of obscurity. An American officer, Lieutenant Graydon, has hit upon an idea for the construction of aerial torpe- does, as an improvement on the sub-marine weapon now employed by the navies of all the powers. The chief objection to the present tor- pedo is that it is only effective at something like 600 yards,and occasionally it takes advantsge of its invisibleness to wander abroad, or even to treacherously return on its projectors. Moreover, the enterprising torpedo boat on destruction bent finds it a difficult matter to get within 600 yards of a battleship spouting shot and shell enough to blow it to atoms not mighty but infinitesimal. Lieutenant Graydon's invention is designed to in- crease the range of torpedo-boats to 6,000 yards, the weapon of offence being a missile containing 1,400 lbs of explosive more powerful than dyna- mite, and which will, of course, after an aerial ' flight, fall upon the objective battleship and annihilate it forthwith.</p>
        <p>Our own War Office has found reason to com- plain of the non-effectiveness of the Lee-Metford bullet as an expedient" killer "and has adopted a new type of service bullet which will be used for the first time at Khartoum. This is described as the " man-killing " bullet, and is preferred to the "man penetrating" bullet—the L3e-11fetford—be- cause of its superior deadliness. Actual warfare has shown that it does not suffice to perforate one's enemy. With daylight" let through him ' he has been found to continue his onslaught, and do more damage than the traditions of the service allow under such circumstances. It is to do away with this possibility of a shot-pierced fanatic romaining a combatant that the new bullet has been adopted. The experiment on the unfor- tunate dervishes is expected to prove that one shot will be sufficient to place a man hors de combat. If not, well, some sort of bullet will have ,to be devised which can be guaranteed to do ' prompt and permanent execution,</p>
        <p>As we hinted a few weeks ago, the agreement come to at a meeting of Voluntary school mana- gers held at Warrington with reference to a i uniform commencement of holidays has ended n a miserable fiasco. It will be remembered that at that meeting a resolution was passed, with only two dissentients, that the summer holidays should uniformly commence on July loth. The Scho(.l Attendance Committee were particularly anxious that the holidays should commence at a stated time and thus prevent them from extending over a period of several months. If the ciecia Ton ar- rived at had been adhered to this difficulty would have been overcome,and it would also have enabled the school managers to have included August Bank holiday week in the summer holidays. As a rule this period is an e ceptionally bad on for school attendance. But the arrangement has this week fallen through. With the exception of the Catho- lic schools, the elementary schools have already commenced the holidays fcr varying periods, and as the Catholics will not commence theirs until the 115th instant the holidays will extend over as long a period as ever, and the work of the School At- tendance Committee will be made more difficult by some schools remaining open whilst others are closed.</p>
        <p>1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>• •••••m••••.:,0,m.p.P.• FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>• •••••m••••.:,0,m.p.P.• FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>CRICKET. —The following team has been selected to represent Frodsham, against Weston, at Fro isham on Saturday :—W. Norris Jones (captain), F. Roston, J. Entwistle, A. Halford, E. limnerley, C. E. Linaker, W. N. Linaker, J. Price, J, Rodgers, F. Touralin, H. Wright, with A. Ashworth and L. Sutton as reserves.</p>
        <p>, MISS CROSFIELD'S SUNDAY SCHOOL—The scholars attending what is known as Miss Crosfield's Sunday school, went for their annual trip to Llandudno—the queen of Welsh watering places, on Mooday, where a most enjoyable day WA q spent. There were about 150 schnlars and teachers who partook in the outing. METHODIST FREE CHORCH.—On Sunday last, the l:lunday Sehoole in connection with the above church 'field their anniveraary,-vhen two eloquent sermons were , preached by the R 97. S. Wright, Runcorn, to large cob- ' gregatione. Special music was rendered and the collections, which were Wi aid of the schools, amounted to £l5 83. 63. THE OFF,RTON CHURCH SUNDAY SCHOOL —Ol Wednes- day the scholars to the number of about 120 belonging to the above schools, had their annual t!dp, in the form of a voyage up the Weaver to Yale Royal. At their de,;.. tination a capital tea was partaken of, and the day spent in fun-making and sight-seeing. The scholars were under the care of the Misses Wright and Ashaeld and their able assistants.</p>
        <p>CYCLING.-OCI Stturday the members of the Runcorn Ramblers' Club, and the Litchford C.C., arranged an inter•club run to Chester' the starting point being the Bear's Paw, Frodshain. The run was a most enjwable one. A substantial tea was provided at the Bowling Green by Mr. Chapman. A pleasant afternoon was spent in sight seeing,and the gaily decorated boats imm the Dee, in honour of the Hospital Saturday celebration, were much admired by the cyclists, whose sympathies in that direction are well known. PAROCHILL COMMITTEE.—The Parish Council for Fred. sham Lordship, sitting as a parochial committee, held a meeting on Monday night,at which the following gentle- men were present : Messrs. Henry Tiley, (in the chair), A. Tbomasi,Thomas Lewis, T. V. Cs.rter,James Percival, John 11. Davies, with Mr. John Ashton (clerk to the Runcorn District Council), and Mr. William Divgle, (surveyor for the Frodsham District). The only business of importance was the consideration of the tender submitted for the extension of the main sewer through Five Crosses.—After a lengthy discussion the tender of Mr. F. J. Bennie, Liverpool-road, Warrington, for £922 Is 61 was accepted, stubjent to a reduction for part of the length of sewer.—At the District Council on Tuesday the recommendations were duly passsed. FROD3NAM AND Tye WATER LOAN.•-•••Mr. Herbert Law (inspector of the Local Government Board) held an in. quiry at the Tasvn Rail, Fradsham, on Friday, in the matter of the application of the Runcorn Rural District Council for sanction to borrow £350 for works for the supply of water to the township of Frodsham. Mr. John Ashton (clerk) and several members of the Rural Dili. trict Council, and also of the Parish Council,and Mr. W. Timmins, the engineer, were alas present.—Mr. Ashton explained the nature of work for which they re:lnked the lean ; it being proposed to have a new additional engine and pump at the waterworks. There was some difficulty experienced in keeping up the supply of water in the district,v.ithout keeping up the pumping at night 1 which was a very expensive arrangement. With the proposed additional engine and pumping apparatus it was thought that they would be able to meet the demand without recourse to night work, and also they would be In a position to cope with shy emergency that might arise in the future.—Plans of the proposed works were produced and explained in detail by Mr. W. Timmins, and the inspector also paid a visit to the pumping station.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>WESTON.</head>
        <p>WESTON.</p>
        <p>THEINEW GRIMM' —We are informed on gond I thor- ity that a gentleman,who is desirous that his name shall not be mentioned,is about to present a magnificent new organ to the churn of St. John the Evangelist, at Wes- ton. The organ is to cost from LBOO t) £703. and will be of the latest type.</p>
        <p>, Tun PARIS I COUNCIL—It the meeting of this council held on Friday night, for the purpose of discussing the road q ieston,Mr. James Tudor was elected to the chair. There were also present, Messrs. S. Saurage, James Rasuett,—Allen, David Ssmerville, and the Clerk (Mr. James Collier).—Mr. Tudor said he had called upon Mr. Bankes and the result of the interview was that he received the same answer as before. Mr.Bankes said he was in the position of trustee and could not sell or give the land as it was not his. Some slight discussion took place as to the desirability of interviewing M erred, in reference to the part of the proposed road that would pass through hi 3 estate, Mr. Somerville was hr referring the matter as it stood to the District Council at Dutton. ! Mr. Allen, however, thnii,..rht it best to adjoorn the Council, or refer the matter to the next or,iioiry Coun- cil mecting,as there were so few present and the •• ibject was such an important one. After some fur' ner re- marks the latter course WM adopted onanimoua:,-, Mr. Somerville withdrawing his motion.—The meeting lasted but a few minutes. - - - -</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY, —On Sundsr last. the anniversary of the Wesleyan Sunday schoo!s, Weston, was duly observed, when the Rev. John Nlartin preached special sermons in their behalf at bath morning and evening services. In the afternoon a service was held at whickl the Rev. T. Sydney Morris (past, of St. John's Presbyterian Church, Runcorn), occupied h. pul- pit. Great praise is due to Mr. Jnhn Ball far I'l-10 able manner in whith he coniucted the singing of lila choir and scholars,ond also to Mr. Alfred Ball .he presided at the organ with'his accustomed ability. The coll,ctione, which will be devoted to the school funds amounted to £37 17s.1d.,being nearly double the total of the previous year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, JULY 8, 18</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, JULY 8, 18</p>
        <p>RUN'JORN AND v.rtDNES.</p>
        <p>SUCCESS OF Mg. A. ABEL.</p>
        <p>Manning in the C. and B.C. Sports at Crornpton,Mr.A. Ab:3l,of Rahicorn,secared the fit prizi in the q larter. (np,n) handicap for the 32 yard 4 nark. lit time was 531 sectnds.</p>
