      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>SIR JOHN BRUNNER..</head>
        <p>SIR JOHN BRUNNER..</p>
        <p>Sir John Brunner has given notice in Parliament. on bihalf of the Commons Preservation Society, of opposi- tion to the Great Northern Railway Bill, the Rochdale Corporation Water Bill, and the Todmorden Corporation Water Bill. TUE BEGGING NUISANCE AT _WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court. on Monday, two rough looking men named Samuel Hadfield and John Sinith, were charg,rl bAore the Mayor with having solicited alms in St. Miehael's-road, Dittoa, on the previous day, bat were dismissed on the understanding that they left Widnes at once. WIDNES RUNCORN AND DISTRICT BIRD Snow.</p>
        <p>The fourth annual exhibition of canaries and mules, in nest feather, under the auspices of the Widnes, Run- corn and Distric`, Bird Breeders Association, was held at Runcorn on Saturday. The class ot birds shown was of excellent quality ; but the exhibits fell far slicrt of any previous show ever held in point of number.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>ST. LUKE'S P S.A.</head>
        <p>ST. LUKE'S P S.A.</p>
        <p>At St. Luke's Church, Pennington-rad,on Sandav,the u ual monchlv meeting of the P.S. k. was held, Mr. P. Bradbury presiding. The R )v. J. Oiborne. delivered an interesting address upon " Disapsointments " and was listened to witli marked attention. Miss St.me, and Messrs. Luinh, 11-therts, and Goivaclook sustained toe musical portion of the !fleeting in a creditable manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>MUSICAL §irccitss</head>
        <p>MUSICAL §irccitss</p>
        <p>We are pleased to not. that Mies May E. Hicks. daughter of Inspector Hicks, of Runcorn and pupil of Miss C. Tracy, Widnes, has success,tally passed her Primary examination in connection with the Victoria College of Music. She was examined before Mr. J. H. Lewis, Mn. Bic., at Liverpool. on the 27:h June, and passed w;t'n honours securing 85 out of a possible 103 marks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>APPOINTMENTS.</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. 11. C. Barnes, the popular caretaker at th Run- corn Liberal Club, who has filled that offl3e far the past 8 years, his been selected out of a large number of applicants for a similar plst at the Liberal Club at Colne, Lancashire. We wish Mr. fumes evnT success in his nsw undertaking, and his departure from Run- corn is looked upon with regret by the members of the club with whom he is a prime favourite.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>FALL FROM A LADDER.</head>
        <p>FALL FROM A LADDER.</p>
        <p>0.1 Friday evening a man narn.d Patrick resid. ing atlo, Marv-street,Moss Bank,employed as a labourer at Sallivan's Works, was standing on a ladder in the works (althoogh it is stated that he was not working at the rime), when he (ell. He was at °nee conveyed to the Widnes Accident Hospital, v.her3 D. Hutchinson was soon in attendance. The man had his left leg broken. He is now progressing as well as can ba ex- pacted. ACCIDENT AT HALTON CASTLE.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon, a rather serious ac.cident; oe. curred to a boy named Srephon Ro-e. of G.ltyirt-s;re.?c, Runcorn. It apoears that the boy f wed the demon- stration of the Oddfellow's Clubs which went to Halton, and upon arriving there he attempted to climb the wall into the green,when one of the large s andstene 3 to which he was clinging gave way and ha foll backwards drag- ging the huge stone on top of hind. f-1, was carried to a cottage close by and afterwards t tken home. upon ex- amination it was found that he had sustained serious in. juries to his hip. VICCORIA.-ROAD WESLEYAN CrICfRCII, WI DNES. Anniversary services were held in the above place of worship on Sunday, when the Rev. James Bryant, for- merly of the Widnes circuit,preached.ln the afternoon an address was delivered in the church to the scholars and friends by Councillor Lloyd, of Liverpool. Special twines &amp;c, were rendered by the choir and scholars, who had been trained by Messrs. E. Evans and W, Hudson, res- pectively. Mr. W. Stanley ofE.Aated at the omran. The offertories amounted in the aggregve to £lOl Os 6,1 1.. £75 of which was contributed by the scholars, who had collected that sum during the past year. MILTON.ROAD P.S WrioNns.</p>
        <p>_ The first In'e7t-ing of the new half-year was held on Sonday,when sevaral new members were enrolled. There was a good attendance, and the President (the Rev. E Austin) occupied the chair. Miss Holm sang the hymn entitled "Peace" as a solo,the men jaining in the choru3, while Miss Tnomas rendered the solos" My Father" and "The Promised Land." Both efforts were wall received. The speaker ((r.Porter, of Widnes) selected as his sub. ject "Tribulation," and delivered a very irsteresting dis- conrse. He encouraged his hearers to sustain their spirits in time of trouble,assuring them of God's increasing help I if sought for by prayer. FUNERAL OF A FIREMAN.</p>
        <p>On Thursdayaiternoon the mortal remains of Mr. Albert Swann, White-street, who has been a member of the Runcorn Fire Brigade, were laid in their last restinr,. place at Greenway-road Cemetery. The deceased was connected with the Brigade for the last 4 years or so, and during that time gained the high respect of iii fellow-comrades by his exemplary cmduct. The body was conveyed from t'le /MVP, to the gracnnido upon the firr-angine of the Brigade, whikit his comrades acted as bearers—rile members of the local .3oilermakers Society, of which he was a rrprah„r, also took part in the funeral e.ortefe.—Mr. Swann leaw,4s widow and a large family of young children to ar urn his loss, which is the more lamentable a 4 tie was but 32 years of age. Be passed away on Sunday last after but a short CRESUIRE MASONIC CHARITIES.</p>
        <p>On Sunday—;fternoon a apecia! matrinic—s.Prvice was held at Norley Church, which was attended by a crowd- ed congregation. The brethren assembled in th. schoolroom, where they donnad their regalia,and thence proceeded to the sacred edifice. There was a large gathering, representatives being present from the follow. log lodges --Sincerity, Northwich ; Ovanda`e, wich_; Elleam ' ers Runcorn ; Cholmondeley. F„d‘ham ; and Samaritan, Sandbach. Bre. tin Ra.v. C. R. Nunn, chonlain, delivered a brief address on the advantages arLing from the Masonic Charities. widovvs and childrag of deceasd brethern receiving valuatile altanc %. In the musical service solos were interpreted by Miss Louise Cestria, Bro. E. Pritchard (Northsvich) and Bro. a. Branch (Runcorn). M.sicl p,rformanCn went oiven by Messrs. J. Patterson Shaw, F. R. Yarwood,aad R. F. Yarwood. The collection on behalf of tho Cheshire Misonic Charities realised a nice sum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>WEDDING AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>WEDDING AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On FridaY morning, at the Bsthea la Celgregatienal Church, the wedding of Mr. Frederick Adam Smir,h, of Victoria-road. Runcorn, and Miss Lily John secondion. daughter of Captain Johnson, harbour roaster, Runcorn, was duly scleinnisecl in the presence of a laree number of friends and well-wishers, which testieted to their popu- larity in Runcorn and district. The Joseph O'Shorne was the officiating minister. Mr. John Potter fulfilled the duties of " best man," and Miss Annie Johnson (sister of the bride) made a charming brides- maid. The bride looked exceedingly pretty in a smart and wore a Legborne hat fawn travelling costume, trimmed with white chiffon. As the haunt" couple were or the way down the aisle after the ceremoov. 1.. vv. Wedding March," and as Cook, A R.C.0., Ward the' ' they em.rged frcin the Church the? were deluzed with rie,o and confetti, showered linen them ty their many friends. The weddinz breakfast was partaken of at the hom9 a the bride, aft r which the, newly-m irriel couple left for Prestatyn, where the hsneytmoon will he anent, accompanied by the good wishes r,..f their friends. A I number of useful and costly presents were received by I both bride and bridegroom.</p>
        <p>---- -- CHURCH PARADE OF Ti RUNCORN VOLUNTEERS. The Runcorn Detachment of the 2dd V. B. Cheshire Regiment, accompanied by the Church Scheele Boys' Brigade,on Sunday morning last, turned out in goo ily numbers for the purpose of a e.hurch parasle t 3 Halton. Headed by their excellent brass hand the company of volunteers, with the boys' brigade in the roar, smartly riteved off by way of Greenway-road, Vmtoria-road,and Halton-road,to Halton Church. —The Rev. G. D. Wray (vicar) preached a most appropriate sertnon,and suitable hymns were selected for the service,which were heartily joined in by all. upon their return to Runcorn,Ctptain G. Ashton (Runcorn),expreased his great pleasure at the Way the corps had conducted itself during the parade, and complimented them upon their general •mart ap• pearance.—The officers present included :—Major iisen (Frodsham). Captain Ashton (Runnorn). and Lieu- tenant Morrice (Runcorn).—The strength of the elm. pally was 106, made up as followl : —Officers, 3 ; ser- geants. 8; buzlers. 3 rank and file, 86 , and the Bova' Brigade numbered 68 ; being made up of three officers, three sergeants,and 62 rank and file. The day was beau- tifully fine and the march out proved quite a treat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>FETE DAY AT WIDNES-.</head>
        <p>FETE DAY AT WIDNES-.</p>
        <p>The scholars of the Sunday school's-c-o.nnected with St. Mary's Church, Widnes, to the number of 150, held their annual field day on Satueday afternoon. They assembled at the church, and from thence walked to th. Bel,/ in processional order, headed by the Widnes Subscription Band. who played some beautiful selections ea, route. On arrival at the field tea was served out on the grass,after which every kind of amusement was engaged in br the scholars until the hour of departure arrived.Ths children were then re-assembled, aid they proceeded down to the church, together with the band, in the same order as before.--The scholars attached to the Milton-road Con- gregational Church also had their annual outing, when the customary drive to Prx Hill in lurries, kindly lent for the occasion was undertaken. On their arrival various amusements were engaged in until the tea hour, when the youngsters were regaled in the pavilion, after which races were imialged in, some very good prizes in the 01403 of books and toys being offered. Sabseq,aently about 60 teachers and friends partook of tea, following which an adjournment was made to the hill, where they endeavoured to entertain the younger scholars. The party commenced the return journey at 0-30, and arrived in Widnes at 9-15 p.m. rCYOLING and TOURING HOU: MAP3.—Tens GRKIN, Stationer, 31, Brldze-street, Warrington ..rgN VNHY LaTBST DISSIGNS and COLON/SINGS , in Millinery, Dress Fabrics. Silks. Blouses, Senshides, Umbrellas Ise.. &amp;c.—W.T.WILLIams, Sankey-street, and Legh-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOA.RD MEETING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>APPOINMENT OF A CLERK.</head>
        <p>THE APPOINMENT OF A CLERK.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening,the Runcorn School Boarl,met at the School Board Offices, prior to the time fixed for the general business of the Board, for the purpose of inter- viewing the four sekcted cendidatee for the post of clerk. It should be etated,that in answer to the Board's advertisement for a suitable person to fill the vacancy athing out of the retirement of Mr. E. Mese, about 30 applications were received, but on the previous Friday these were weeded down to the following four,who duly appeared before the Board in support of their candida- ture, on Tuesday :—Mr.J. W. Lightburn, Mr. B. Crosby, Mr. W. E. Lancaster, and Mr. J.Mayston. After the candidaces had been interviewed, the Press representatives were admitted and the usual business of the Board resumed. The members present were: —Mr. James Pritchard(chairman),Rev.Canon Wood,Rev.Father Gore, W. S. Taylor, Mr. F. J. Norman, and Mr. E. Imi- son, with Mr. G. E. Moss (clerk) ; and Mr. Bowyer (ecnool attendance officer). Mr. Pritchard said that the Board bad met upon that occasion to make the very important appointment of a clerk to the Board. This was the third occasion in the history of the Board, upon which they had been called upon to do so. It was, however, the most important. Previously the duties had been light in comparison with what they were at the present time. Simply the taking of corresponeence and the recording of the observations of the Board. Since thou the Board had built or acquired schools, and were now responsible for the education of ' more than half the children of the town, and the teach- ing staff had increased and at the present stood at about 10 which made the position of Clerk a very resnon- aible one, and one of considerable influence. Therefore, they should use their unbiase.d judgment in appointing an individual who would conduct the work to the satis- faction of the Board and in the interests of education. That evening they had been in a position to judge and consider the merits of the four candidates who had been before them. After due consideration and taking everying into account, he (the epsaker) had a neme to submit, which he thought would meet with the approval of the members, and one who had been In the service of the Board, and conducted his duties there to their satisfaction. He referred to Mr. Benj train Crosby. Mr. Crosby, as they were aware, was for a number of years the headmaeter of the Brunswick Schools ; and also had been of considerable service to the Board when they undertook the Science and Art C:assee. He was personally acqiiainte I with Mr. Crosby's work and knew he was attentive to his duties, and syetemati- cal and methodical, and also exact in aetail. Then again, they knew his abilities as a teacher, and in that direction his experience would bs of service. Ole great advantage about Mr. Crosby wee hie age. He had a long life before him, which he could devote to the cause of education in the town. He had pleasure in prop-sing that Mr. Crosby be appointed the C'erk of the B ,rd in accordance with the terms and conditions drawn up by the Beerd, at. a salary of £lO3 per annum. Mr. F. J. Norman said he seconded, beceule lie con- sidered Mr. Crosby a meg', capable man. I', was one of the most important appointments tiv,y coati mike. His seconding of Mr. Crosby meant no diereepeceto th ties of any of the other claildstes. Meer. he ‘r 1 He was a schoolra miter wi,h the VP"V hizhest us wis fresh f rem the work. II e-t-edate in the cede.and the one man, th et seemed to him, to he able to fill into the plea aid carry eel: the wart; atones. He walk] not introduce discussable matter, hut there ev,re many directions in which Mr. Crosby weul I prove useful. Oee edecetional alit!) irity sneaking of a clerk ha I sei I "That he was a mine of knowledge and a machine cap- able of putting into effect all that the S;heol B stri re- Tilted." fl would act in this matter as he 471Uiri I making the appointment for himself. He secended the Chairman, _ _ .</p>
        <p>-..-..... _ The Rev. Canon Wool said that he moved An amend- moat. lie did so with regret.aa it was dosirah'e that they should be of one feeling in a matter of 'his kind, but, whoever was elents.l, he hoped that the Board would give its future clerk its full sunpolt. (II ear,hear) He begg,ei t propose that Mr. J. W. Lie•latburn be elected to the clerkship. He had hoard all that had been said in favour of Mr. Crosby, and if they were going to elect a schoolmaster it would be of weight. But they were not going to elect a schoolmaster. It was a clerk they required. For this plat it was not only desirable to have a knowledge of School Boards but also a practi- cal acquaintance with accounts, hook.keeping etc. Mr. Lightburn as they were well aware, was capable of management, and had gone through the same eKperi- ences of a schoolmaster as Mr. Crosby. He was a man of considerable position in the town. and when a man of this position came forward and offered himself they ' should avail themselves of the opoortunity. With regard to Mr. Crosby's age as quoted ' in his favour, he would say that Mr. Light- burn's age was a greater qualification still. Cn-ainly, when a man was young he was energetic ; but, when a man was old, he was tempered with wis- dom, If Mr. Crosby's youth was on his side, M.'. Light. burn's age was on his side. Ile had a good knowledge of accounts, school managernent,and besideg, was well. known to them in the town, and he (Canon Wool) had great pleasure in moving that Mr. Lightburn he elected. Mr. Samuel Taylor. seconded in a f'w rem orks,saving there was no doubt in his mind that Mr. Lightburn would discharge the duties to their satisfaction and that of the ratepayers. Upon a vote being taken,the Revs. Canon Wond.Father Giore,and Messrs. E. Luis" and S. Taylor (4) voted for the amendment ; andXr. James Pritchard and Mr. F.J. Norman (2) for the original proposition. Canon Wood moved as a subsequent !natio, that Mr. J. W. Ligotburn be elected clerk to the Bari, under the conditions drawn uo by the Board at their meeting on June 21st at a salary of £lOO per annum, duties to commence on August 3rd.—This was unanimously agreed to. Mr. Lightburn was recalled and thanked the Beard for the confidence reposed in him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>INTERESTING FIGURES.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING FIGURES.</p>
        <p>The rerert of the School Attendance ammittee dig- some very internsting figures,as showing the pros- perity of the town. The number of scholars upon the registers was 3,335 being ex%etly equal to that of the Ram period last year. Tim average attenrlanca was 2.817 against 2,679, being an increase of 138.—1 t was remarked that the town was evidently pasiing through its most trying time, as the %elml attendances we a very good index of the population. "GOOD-EYE TO THE OLD SCHOOL." . . .</p>
        <p>It was reported that the boys and girls front to Brunswick and Granville-street school" had ben trans- ferred to the Victoria-road extensions. The occasion was marked by a hit of a demonstration on Friday lag', the children all walking in proces"ion to the new school.after formally Baying "good-bee" to the old one,for the period necPs'ary to carry out the alteration'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>CHAN'GES IN TIE' E TEACHING STAFF ALL BOUND.</head>
        <p>CHAN'GES IN TIE' E TEACHING STAFF ALL BOUND.</p>
        <p>I The School Management Committee, at a meeting held nit June 14th. considered the question of the arrange- ments of the teaching staff and decided to make the ilfollowine. changes :—That three months' notice be given I to Miss Weeks, heod-mistress at Granville-street infants' 'school ; that Mi 93 Garner, head-mistress at Granville- street Girls' School, be retained at her present salary, to I take charge cf Standard I. and the infants.—The Clerk hid before the committee the pupil teachers'examination reports, and the committee expressed their dissatisfac- tion of the same.—A leteAr Was read from Mr. W. Nick- son, centractor for the Victoria Board, school extension, for an ereension of time, and the Gerk was instructed to reply that tho Board could not accede to the request, as arrangements had been made for the schools to be in 'readiness at the date fixed in the agreement.—The caretaker of Vlctorix-road School reported that the preparation of the extension would involve extra labour, before thee were resdr for r,c•ution of scholars, and the Chairman was antisoris-d to visit the , schools, and settle the amount of extra. D syment to be made in corvequenee.—At a quhgrq lent rnsting of the committee the Clerk reported that the plans and specifi- cations of the alterations of Granvilio.street school had been returned from the E location Department EVCOM- nAniM be a letter, and the Clerk was instructed to req seat Mr. Wilding to deal with the plans aceordinn, the suggestion of the Dspartment.—The Committee recommended that the schools shoull be closed for the holidays on the 2214 July, and resume on the 22j ilaukust.—A letter was read from Miss Acton, assistant assistant mistress at Victoria-road School resigning hr post. The same was accepted. WATER POLO AT ST. HELEN'S.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BEATEN IN. SQUADRON It ICE. At the new Corporation Baths, Boundary-rod, St. Helens, on Tuesday night, a couple of matches in con- nection with tho. Liverpool and District Water Polo League were dscided. Toe St. Helen's secont team met Evarton second in the second division of the League com- petition, and Evertan won by 2 goals (cued by New- combe and Sloan) to 1 (scored by Mavity). The St. Helens third team encountered Widnes first team in the third section, and the Saints won by 7 goals to 1. The principal feature of the evening's proceedings was the mooting of the St. Helens and Warrington teams of eight men each in the first round of the Grant Squadron Com- petition. The teams were—St. Helens : W. Walker, C. Pennington. T. Green, J. Lawrenson, W. Preston, W. Riley, W. Burrows, and P. Ripley. Warrington : W. Ashton, A. Ashton, S. Beaman, T. Locker. A. Boscow, J. Hampton, A. Robertson. and T. Davies. Etch man had to swim three lengths of the bath, and after an interest- ing struggle St. Helens won hst a length and a hall.</p>
        <p>1 An inquest was held at Hove on Wednesday. noon the body of Mr. Harry Smith. aged twinty-eight. Dasesse.l. who was O Independent n:inans,was of an excitab:e nature On Sunday night his wife chaffed hint about kissing tho servant. Later he was foand dead with a revolver wound in his breast. The Jury returned a yenta that ne died from a gunshot wound, but that there was no evidence to show his state of mind at the time.</p>
