      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of the above Board was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. J. Mosley Williams (pre,iding). There were also present Messrs. J. Meadowcroft. Samuel Taylor, J.P., A. R. Norman, T. Lea and the members of the Rural District Council with Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), Mr. George Ashton (deputy clerk).</p>
        <p>A SUITABLE ARRANGEMENT.</p>
        <p>In the matter of the increase in the nurses' salary by annual increments of £1 until a maximum of £3O was reached, the Local Government Board forwarded their sanction to the scheme.—Ttp. Local Government Board also wrote sanetioning the increase of the salary of J. Marsden, industrial trainer, from £25 to £3O per annum.—Tha Chairman said it was very satisfactory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>TIE MEDICAL OFFICER FOR HALTON AND HIS HOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>TIE MEDICAL OFFICER FOR HALTON AND HIS HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Dr. W. Hodgson Carruthers, Halton, requesting the Guardians pertnission to leave his district for a fortnight.and stating that in the mean- time his duties would be discharged by his friend Dr. Maxwell.—The permission was granted.</p>
        <p>THE WORKHOUSE NURSES &amp;ND THEIR SALLRY.</p>
        <p>A letter from the Local Government Board was read stating that tho Board had no objection to the appoint- mnnt of Miss Mary D. Hon slay as nurse at a salary of £25 per annum, together with the usual allowances assigned to her by the Guardians in the form of rations etc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>TUE PAUPER LUNATICS.</head>
        <p>TUE PAUPER LUNATICS.</p>
        <p>The Acting Superintendent of the Cheshire County Asylum submitted the usual half-yearly statement uf the condition of the Union pauper lunatics who were in- mates of the County Asylum. There were 30 males and 33 females. One (Charles Price) bad died since the last report was submitted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>FURNIXRINO OF THE BOARD ROOM!</head>
        <p>THE FURNIXRINO OF THE BOARD ROOM!</p>
        <p>M. J. Mosley Williams as Cnairman of the Board Roan Committee, submitted the report of the committee hich had met that morning. They considered the several tenders sent in and recommended the acceptance of the tender of Messrs. Garnett, of Warrington, for the scheme (B) which was to have a round table and not a horseshoe one as at first suggested.—lle proposed the adoption of the Committee's recommendation.—Mr. Woodward seconded.—Mr. Meadowcroft proposed an amendment that the original suggestion of a bore-shoe table be adhered to.—Mr, Knowles seconded.—Upon a vote being taken, 6 voted for the amendment and 12 for the adoption of the Committee's report—The Chairman was reque3tei to see to the furnishing being carritid out properly : a duty hi willingly undertook.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>FINANCE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Tie financial statement shlwe.l the following., amounts were granted for out.rvlief. Runcorn, £B5 ; Frodsham, £26 ; ant. Budworrh. £2l. There was £4,276 is in hand last }hard day ; £5?6 2i Bi, rec•ived during the fortnight ; parmenta required £3,749 13 91. ; leaving a balance of £933 13 lid—The Acton Grange District had not paid its call.—The report was adooted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>SHEDDING ACCONIJIODATION.</head>
        <p>THE SHEDDING ACCONIJIODATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Darlington who had a notice of motion standing in hi i name to increase the shedding accommodation for the guArdians' traps at the Workhouse, wrote stating that he would b 3 linable to be present at the Board owing to b?ing called upon to serve as a juror at an in- veg. 119 also requested that another member be allowed to move the moiou.—fhe mitter was left to stand over until Mr. D trlincton could be present.</p>
