      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>MASTERS JOURNAL.</head>
        <p>THE MASTERS JOURNAL.</p>
        <p>The Master rei;)r-te-i-t7i-at during the week ending July 4'AI, there were 47 men and 19 women inmates of the Workhouse as ClEnptrol with 42 and 15 respectively for the c.wresomding pniol year. There were 31 boys and 25 girls c-rnpire,i with 33 and 22 last year, and the intant were 2 against 6. Tao ta:al inmates were 165 as against 163 far the correamm linv, week lan year. The imheciles in the lvms,, were 9, and the number of vagrants rel!eved was 27. ANOTHER CANAL MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>BODY FOUND IN THE CANAL AT RUNCORN. A atartling semation was caused in Runcorn on Sun- day morning,at about 12 30 as the police were carrying out grappling operations for the recovery of the body of the young boy, who was drowned in the Bridgewater Canal on Saturilav night (reported in another column), the body of an unknown man was brought to the sur- face. It was that of a man about s:t. 81n. in height, hair and whialters turning grey,with a bald patch on top of head, and a moustache slightly inclieed to be sandy. The clothing had the appearance of belonging to a sea- faring man,and was of a blueseage. One of the buttons had the name "Lewis, Liverpool " stamped upon I. At the Court Haase, Runcorn, on Tuesday, Mr. J. E. Worsley, held an inquiry as to the' cause of death. The evidence adduced showed that the deceased must evi- dently have had something upon his mind for some time pas': as two of the witnesses examined, testified to the deneased having made use of rambling statements. William Oat:a:ley, watermarnsaid he saw the deceased on the nrevious Friday in the Duke of Westminster 004 RI )fli?, and noticed that he wa3 strange in his wan s, and occupied a table to hitn3:lf. He would hold out his hat at arm'a drogth, aud say " Cannot you for- give and forge." Then apt up and say " Carnarvon ! Carnarvon !" an I mutter strange things. He alsa said " Good-love !'' rule last wards the witness heard him use were "I shall not pay for drink and you know that!" Ho (witness) thought the man wae unsst in his mind, He anpsare 1 to taisober enough,aud had the appearance of a seaman. Joan Corf manager of the Cocoa Rooms, stated that the deleased came to his house on Friday aftornoon,and again at evening. He stayed about half an hour in the afternom, and about twenty minutes in the evening ; leaving about ten nainutee past ten o'clock. In the after- neon,doceased appeared to behave in a strange manner. Witness allowed him to have a knife and fork, but was sorry :a Fterwarda. hetause he tho ;eat the man might do aline damage. He did not,however, consider the man's c -induct such as would warrant him calling in the police. He was perfectly sober. In the evening the man would look up at the ceiling and sae " For God and Heaven's sake will you forgive me and settle it." Usually Witne3s closed his shop at ten o'clock,if there was no one in; and as the deceased was the only one in that night he told him it was time." He replied " All right ! thank and than added " For heaven's sake will you settle it " and thee awned out crying. Robert Yates, waterman, testified to seeing the de- ceased on the canal bank, by the Sprinch Yard, on Saturday, about 5 o'clock. Witnens's flat, in company with three more, was being towed by a tug. When they arrivel at the " Soapery " the captain of the first fiat told witness, that the captain of the last flat had in' farmed him, that a man had jamped into the water. Witness raised an alarm at the decks hut could not go or send anyone along the canal bank to try and get the deceased out. He thought the man on the last flat would see to that. Sunerintendent Okell, said they had endeavoured tn gtt the man, who was alleged to have seen the deceased jump into the canal, as a witness : but, As his flat had not yet reached Todmorden they were unable to secure him. P.C. Gregory gave evidence of the recovery of the body, which had not been identified. The body bore the appearance of being damaged by the propellers of the Cues, and most of the clothing had been torn off. A piece of blue cloth, found on one of the legs, corres- ponded with that found in the propeller of the tug " Salford" on Saturday. He also gave a descriptdin (similar to the one above) of the man, and added that the body and appurtenances had been photographed, and a description circulated. 'The Coroner, in summing up, said it was greatly to be regretted that the man, why was supposed to have seen the deceased get into the canal, did not go or cause someone eke to go along the canal bank and see what could ha done. It had the appaarance of gross eareless- noes and callousness. He would not say more. The only verdict he wnuld advise the jury to return was" Found drowned."—The jury returned a verdict accordingly.</p>
        <p>ppearance</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>DISTRESSING DROWNING FITILITT</head>
        <p>DISTRESSING DROWNING FITILITT</p>
        <p>AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Curt, on Tuesday morning, Mr. J. E. Woreley, county coroner, held an inquest upon the body of a boy, named Maurice Htllsworth, agen 9, of 26, Rutland-street, Runcorn, whose body was found in the Bridgewater Canal, on Sunday morning, being re- , covered by means of grapples. I Sarah Ha'swarth, identified the body as that of her son. She last saw him on Saturday when he went after the Coddlellow's procession, to Eialton. Her son was not in the habit of fishing in the canal. Eiward Norton Hodson, a playmate of deceased, aged 11, said on Saturday he accompanied the deceased to Halton Castle, and also came home with him along Halton-road. They crossed the canal and came along the towpath to the top of Nelson-street. When there, deceased went ou his knees, and commenced catching " Jack-sharps." He asked the deceased to come home, hut he would not, and he left him still going on with his fishing. It would be about 8 o'clock when he left.—By a Juror : The deceased did not fall into the canal whilst witness was there. William Halsworth, flatman, 28, Ashridgr-street, de- posed to recovering the body at about three o'clock, on Sunday afternoon. It would be about 20 yards from the top of Nelson-street,and was fully clothed. There were no signs in the vic,nity Of a body or anythiug else hav- ing fallen into the canal. P.C. Hregorf gave evidence of examining the body, which was taken out of tha canal in the presence of In- spector Hicks. There wore no marks of violence upon the body. The Coroner in f urnming tip,said the evidence pointed Clearly to the fact that the deceased fell into the canal ; indeed.there could be little doubt upn that point, and he felt that the jury could bring in no other verdict than the one of " found drnwned.—The jury concurred: The foreman of the jury expressed his wonder that there was riot more people drowned than there really were. It was a continual thing for little ones to be lot- in their t somethinghe ir lilives, should be s right tlalong the whole length of the canal; done.</p>
