      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SaTtinDAY.—Before Mr. S. Chandley (in the chair), Mr. J W. Hayward. and Mr. R T. Fairciough. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Catherine McCann was charged with this offence en Friday. The evidence was that about twenty minutes past ten on Friday morning as the Salvation Army was coming by Market Gate, defendant rushed up and pulled the banner down. The case was proved and deffndant was fined 5s or in default seven days.—Margaret TR9 a and and Bridget Milligan for the some offence in Dallam lane on Friday night were each sent to gaol for seven days hard labour. The officer who proved the case said defendant Tess threatened to " cut his b—liver out."—Margaret Oakes for the same offence in Dial-street, on Friday night was sent to gaol for twenty one days.—Albert Butterworth last from St. Helens was found lying on his back in Allen-street, at five minutes past twelve the same morning. The Chief Constable said prisoner earned his livelihood by exhibiting a tin plate on which were painted the worth. "Kind friends, Albert Butter- worth have been affected with a paralytic stroke for over sit years through working in damp at Eccboston Hall colliery, and am now unable to ammort my wife and three children. You generosity solicited." The Chief Constable said he was one of a small army of tramps and beggars who descended on Warrin ten Walking Day and imposed on the people.—Sent to prison for seven days. Dtarnamnyas —John Mather and Ellen Wild were charged with being drunk and disorderly in B-onk Perk the previous night at 7 o'clock.—P.C. Smith and Mr. Ashton park-keeper proved the ease. The facts were nafit for publication. De- fendants were fined 20s and coats each or in default seven days hard labour. MONDAY.—BEfo-e Mr. F. Monks fin the chair), Alderman Blackburst, Councillors Shaw Green and Smethurst and _ _ .</p>
        <p>Messrs. Birch%ll and Wright. DitllNlC.—Thomas Bebbinton. 47. Bridgeastreet, Chester, was fined 2s 61 for being drunk in Bold-street on Saturday night.—Defendant pleaded guilty.—The Police Constable who pr-cred the case rodd he found defendant lying on the fesrpath Ii Bold-stre,t. He had to get a handcart to take bum down to the Police Station. "HIS OWN ACCUSER."—Martin Mitchell, 5. Shin-yard, was charged with stealing a pair of boots value 4s l!d from the shop of Messrs. Scales and Sons. Bridge-street, on Satur- day night.—Prisoher pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable said the manager of the shop, Mr. Little, heard a noise of something falling outside the shop about half-past ten on Saturday night. On going outside he found that an iron bar an which several pair of boots had been hung had been pulled to Me ground. and one pair had been detached. One boot was lying on the ground. He saw the prisoner walking away. He gave information to the police. Sergeant Banana and PC. Monks made inquiries in Ship Yard. Standing outside the prisoner's house they heard him ruminat ng to this effect :—" B -- it, I have only got one." The officers went inside. Prisoner bad the boot in his band. They arrested him and toek him down to the police station. —Prisoner said he was drunk on Friday and Saturday or would never have done it —The bench fined hien 15s or in default to go to gaol for seven days. TifinfiDalt.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (in the chair) and Mr. I Thowas Birchall. A DoITBLE Cn.inGE.—Daniel O'Loughlin. ?.Buckley-street, labourer. was charged with being drunk and disorcierl7 in Winwlek-street on Monday night.—Defendant pleaded not gulity.—lle was also charged with using threats to Bridget Sauce on the 12th May. The charge of threats was first pro- ceeded with. Bridget Sauce when put in the witness box appeared reluctant to give any evidence. After being ques- tioned she admitted she had made it up with him.—The Chief Constable said a free summons had been granted to her, as far back at July. A warrant had been issued in the usual " way. Defendant was arrested last night. A certain class of women he roiel, availed themselves of the summonses and then thought they were able to make it up with the defend- ant and let the matter drop.—The Chairman Tell us the circumstances. The Chief Constable: You know what you said when yon took the oath. Was it not true what you said —Witness ; Yea—The Chief Constable Well just repeat it. —Witness : I can't remember it.—Mr. Blrehall : We do not believe that. The previous charge was then proceeded with. Detective Inspector Monaghan said he arrested th- defendant on a warrant the previous evening. Defendant was drunk en the sofa. When they got into the street no threw himself down on the ground and was very disorderly, Defendant said he had been working in Bedford Leigh. and was sitting quietly at home at the time.—The Chief Constable said it was his Itth appearance. Defendant was sent to gaol for twenty-one days without the option of a fine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. George Comber (in the chair), and Mr. G. Mortimer.</p>
        <p>-- OCCASIONAL LICENCNEL—The following were granted licences to sell intoxicants at the Newton Races on the 12th and 13th July : —Emma Smith. Star Hotel, Hyde; William Henry Clueas. Frodsham-street. Chester; James Whittaker, Cross-lane, Hyde; Samuel Hurst, Commercirl Hotel, Widnes; and Gaorge Naylor. George Hotel, Middleton. MismttLerecous CASKS—Michael Coyle. drunk on the highway at Winwiek on the 21st June was :Iced 5s : fleorge CheswOrth, for a similar offence at 11l zton on the 27th June was fined sa; John Hayes drunk and disorderly t U roft on the 22nd June, and also for not having the control of a horse attached to aced was fined 10s for the two offences ; Albert Nynekvne, of Warrington. for using a float on tha highway at Poulton on the 181 h June without having his name on, was fined Is and costs; Henry Wood,driving without !reins at Rizton on the 2Ah June was fined ss; wtillmliea.mwams a at isne fined for not haring proper control over his horse at Poulton on June 15th, as he was 400 yards away at 51 - William Worthington, drunk and di.orderly at Burton_ wood on the 291 i Jane, fined 5s ; John Ward, for the same offence in Liverpool-road. Sankey, was fined 5s ; William Aspinall, dog at large unmuzzled at Burtonwood on the 13th Jane, Is and costa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. – –</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. - -</p>
        <p>MOWD4l7.—Before Messrs. A. 'MOMS, and W. Timmins. EJECIMPMT ORDMt.—Mr. F. Johnson applied for an eject- ment order against Alfred Harrison. the occupant of a cottage In the Holloway, service of the sever it notices having been girm.—The bench granted the application. Scn sot CASEs.—Bdwin Glover,of Snffolk-street,summoned by Mr. Bowyer (school attendance officer) in respect of his two boys Hobert and William, who had made attendances of 27 and 59 out of a possible 1.12 respectively, was fined 6d.and 45. ed. eosts— The defendant did not appear. " lIIDE-•ND-SEEK."—In the matter of the cause of assault brought by Rose Scott and Benjamin Scott. man and wife,of 3 Heath-road. It will be remembered that the husband ap- peared at the Ist court, and an adjoornment was granted for toe appearance of the wife.—On this occasion the wife appeared and the husband was absent, and an adjournment ' was asked for until Monday.—The magistrates however dis- missed the cause.</p>
        <p>RETIT.IIIIO TO QUIT LICENSED PREMIAFS.—RIehard Griffiths' described as a sailor, was brought up in aust Ay. charged by p.c. Gregory with being drunk and disorderly and refusing to quit licensed premises.—Prisoner pleaded guilty and said be Was sorry.—P.C. Gregory std about three o'clock on Saturday afternoon ho was called to the Holyhead Harbour I he found the prisoner drunk and disorderly. He was creating a great disturbance and would High-street, where nn. not quitdly the prernses when requested to go. He carried on very a • - Pined ss. and 6s. 83. cos-,s, or Bevan days In b i default. AND DisolliDEßLP.—Willinra Owen of Runcorn:seas drunk an disorderly on the previous said.wahlilst In the vicinity n d DRU,Gt Saturday °ne dn I Pig-lane he heard a cry a " '2717.‘_ II :‘ halP " and o going to the sPot faund t" PT2-L"'v""ry drunk. girctipnt. Hsaacroft</p>
        <p>CARTER'S LITTLE IVER PILLS.</p>
        <p>tteat dentifrice in the</p>
        <p>_ Clean les the teeth and spaces bstwoon them eanothing else will. Sound sad pearly white t•eetb, rows lips, and fragrant th engtred. 80ZODONT.</p>
        <p>illusin4 his wife. He was a'so using very bad language. Prisoner who pleaded guilty WAS fined 55 and 6s. 81 costs, or 7 days.</p>
        <p>_ NO-ATPICARLICCE.—EdwArd Fillibin, 129, Surrey street, Runcorn. summoned for using obscene language in Surtey- street. on the 27th of June last, failed to put in an appear- ance —The magistrates said that he would have to appear and the case was adjourned to next Pdonday. STONE THROWING IN THE STRNETS.—&amp; boy aged 14 years, named Jelin Haddock, residing with nis uncle at Surrey- street, was summoned by P.C. Gregory for throwing stones In the streets on the ,7th of June.—ln answer to the charge Hadacesk tearfully replied " not guilty."—P,C. Gregory said on account of the numerous complaints received of the practice of stone-throwing he took out the summons against Haddock, and also against a boy named Shaw. He saw the defendant throwing stuns himself. Witness had a great deal of trouble serving the summons as the " young rascals were like "cats "and could run out of the way UD the roofs and sides of houses," and he hal not seen Shaw since.—The Bench having severely cautioned Haddock, allowed him to depart upon payment,of costs 4s 63 and a premise to reform. BELLICOSE DRlTNEARDS.—Williamlloss, and Patrick Bones, r of Penketh-lane, were brought up charged with being drunk and disorderly and wanting to fight—Both defendants pleaded guilty but each blamed the other for the initiative of the bother.—P.O. Gregory said the men were drunk and wanting to fight, and creating a greet d sturbance in the street.—Fined his and 6s 4d costs in each Case. AN UNREGISTERED LODGING-MDSE.—Patrick Bones, B ack- Penketh-lane (labourer) was summoned under the bye-laws of the local Urban Council for unlawfully receiving lodgers in an unregistered lodging-house in Aleock-street. Oil the 112th June —Mr. A. Lake (law clerk to the Urban Council) appeared fos the prescution, and said the proceedings were taken under the Public Health Act The defendant was the son-in-law of a woman named Farrell, who previously kept the house and caused a great deal of trouble to the Authority. Polic.-Sergeant Lea went to the house and found the place very dirty and one man was sleep- ing upon the landing. The defendant now lived in Back Penketh-street and had also kept lodgers there. Sergeant Lee gave evidence as to visiting'the houee. He found the defendant, his wife and child and a boy of about 16 in one room. On the first landing a man named Jephson and his wife In bed. Witness was gees- tinning th-m as to how long they had been lodging there and how mucn they paid, when the defendant shouted upstairs tell him 3s. 64. per week ; wherenpo they gave him that for answer. Two other men said they paid 4,1 per night, and another man he found sleeping In the attic said he paid 4d. per night. The bedclothes were In a filthy state and the place was very dirty, and there were evidenris of vermin crawling abont the place.—Mr. James Percival (nuis- ance inspector fee the Runcorn Urban Di!trict Council) also gave evidence of visiting the house and finding two men lorlg ing there and of its insanitary condition.—Defendant said he took in !others because his landlord's agent said he could do ; and if he dli wrong it was because he knew no better. As soon as he knew it was WV-WOe he gave It uo. They had never taken lodgers in at Penketti-lane, and only had the other house a matter of 12 days. The Bench imp aed a fine of be. and 143. 6d costs or 14 days in del tult.</p>
