      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>ST. HELENS POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>510IPDLY —Bmto^e Alderman A. Sineliar, Messrs. B. A. Drorngocle. J. Burchall, and A. H. Michell. AlernwPrEn SIIICIDE.—John Green. a labourer, of 12, Phythiim-street, was charged with attempting to commit suicide. It appeared that the priiioner had recently been drinking heavily,and on Friday he was snmmoned by his wife for persistent cruelty towards ker.the ease being adjourned for a month. Police Constable Wood said that the prisouer was drunk en Saturday, and about midnight he was seen to de- liberately jump in the canal near bri•leewater-street, but was rescued be a man named M'Donald. —Prisoner. who said it was all throngh drink, and he wend let drink alone in future, was remanded for a week. DaneßfirM TREMENS.—Jonathan Marsden, II young man, of Cyril-street, was pieced in the dock as a lunatic wandering at large. Dr. Cotton said he found the prisoner suffering from delirium tremens. He appeared to think that some pe spie were creating a scaffold to hang him.—On the sugges- tion of Dr Cotton the prisoner was ordered to be detained in Whin on Workhouse for a fortnight. AL/AGED BunOraay.—Three young men, named John Hesketh. Edward Leahey, and John Connolly. at residing at Smithy-brow, were charged with bnrglariously entering the Bay Horse Inn, Parr-street and steallng a Clock and several articles ot e'othing. the property of Ann Livesey. appeared that the occupants of the house retired to bed about midnight on Saturday, and early or. Sunday morning Coo- stable Bradley saw the prisoners with some of the stolen articles in their possession. He arrested Hesketh, who was carrying the clock, and subsequently took the other two men into custodv.—The prisoners, who pleaded not guilty, were committed for trial at the neat Liverpool Quarter SBssiOtlS. TUESDA.Y.—Before Alderman J._ M:Bryile, Major J. W.</p>
        <p>M'Tear, and Mr. W. J. ThOMSOn. TIIEFT 1,11.0 M THE RAILWAY.—Hannah Hawkins, an elderly woman, of John street, was charged with stealing an iron coupling, value We. from the Ravenhead branch line of the London and North-Western Railway C-impany. Constable Wilkes saw the prisoner carrying a bag containing the cmpling from the direction of the railway, whereupon she threw tbe bag over a low wall into a brook, and afterwar is deni.d all knowledge of it. She was committed for trial at the next Liverpool Quarter Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>OUR LOCAL -CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL -CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The holidays interfered with the attondance at the meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A Society on Sunday after- noon, and there wculd not be more than 400 present. The Rev. J. Yonge pt-sided, and stated that on his re - turn from his holidays he found that most ef his friends had gone on theirs. He heped that those who were now having their holidays would get as much pleasure and profit from them as he trusted he had derived frcm his. There were several ways of spending holidays,some good,some bad; and the sneaker proceeded to refer to the latter, stating that instead of gettieg good from a holiday there were some people who came back with their minds jaded, their bodies wearied, and their whole nature depressed and out of tune. Re- ferring to the I'.S.A., the Chairman said be hoped that when the holiday season was over the P.S,A. would revive considerably. It , would become the duty of every member to put into the work fresh energy and new enthusiasm. They must not attempt to carry it on in a slip-shod and half-hearted way. There could be no doubt that the movement had been the means of making men better men and leading them to live better lives, and it was the duty of those who had benefitted by it to bring it under the notice of others.—The Rev. J. H. Tomlinsof Manchester,was the speaker, and he based his remarks on the text " Why abodest thou in the sheepfolds." The soloist for the afternoon was Mrs.Gleave,who gave an effective render- ing of " The Better Land." The service was also taken part in by Mr. Smiley and Mr. James Ball. WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>WARRINGTON OCCASIONAL COURT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON OCCASIONAL COURT.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. James Tinsley, at the ablve conrt, on Monday, James Fred. Maddock, last from Pendle- ton, was fined 2. 61. and costs for having been drunk in charge of two horses anrt a lurry at Woolston on the 2nd inst. P.C. Billinger proved the case.—Annie Harrison was charged with hav- ing been drunk and disordPrly at Pollton on the 2nd inst. PC. Beath proved the case,and a fine of 2s. 6d. and costs was inflicted.</p>
        <p>Bishop Ancon has accepted the vacant residentiary canonry In Lichfield Cathedral.</p>
        <p>The personal estate of the late Sir Robert Rawlinson,K.C.B. CE , has been valued at £8.2,858. The Rev. J. J. Twist, rector of Hedingham, Essex, is be- lieved to have committed Buie to at /Lepton, near Lovresto`t. For some he had been depressed, and on Monday morning he was found hanging in a barn. James Williams, landlord of the Cambrian Vatets, Wrest ham, ,was, at the Denbighshire Quarter Sessions, on Satur- day, fined £5O for keeping an house open for the purpose of receiving money in connection with betting hazards. A Halifax telegram reports that Leah Ackroyd, a married wonsan,and her son, 14 morehs old, have been found drowned in the canal at Sowerby Bridge She left the lipase on Saturday night with the chill in its nigktdress, and is believed to have committed suicide. The whole or the estate of the late Mr. Edward 0, Blealekley, joint proprietor of the "Sunday Chronicle. " Sporting Chronicle." cad "Evening Chronicle," has been valued at £184,824. There are several bequests chiefly to Manchester and Brighton charities. A sad sequel to a wedding Is reported from Millbr,ok. near Santhamptou. After the ceremony the bride became deliri- ous. and though medical aid was summoned she died with out regaining consciousness. At the inquest on Monday a verdict of " Death from natural causes" was returned.</p>
        <p>•• A meeting te protest against ex treme ritualism in the Chnrch of England was held at Liverpool on Saturday. Councillor W. Bartlett has resigned his membership of the Liverpool Working Men's Conservative Association Owing to staterne• tof an offi3lal that one of its p.inciples wis the upholding of Protestantism. A telemesm to the "Times" from Sydney announces that her Majesty's cruiser Mohawk has annexed the Santa Cruz and Duff groups of the Pacific Islands. The total number of Islands annexed is eighteen. These islands lie to the smith of the Solomon Islands, their positions being approximately 10 degrees south 167 degrees east.</p>
        <p>VA largely attended meeting a pit lads and pony drivers recently employed at Mitchell Main, Darfield Main. Hough- ton Main. Cottonwood. and New Oaks Collieries. now on strike, was held in Wombwell Morket-place orulloaday. The meeting was addressed by the local miners' officials, but the lads decided n-,t, to resume work until an advance was Riven. 3ix thousand hands are still idle. The strike lads have made two unsuccessful attempts to set other collieries down.</p>
        <p>The Earl of Onslow, presiding at the annnal New Zealand dinner held at the Hotel Cecil, ssid the Jubilee celebrations had brought to the eves of Briti4t workmen evidence of the great responsibilities which he possessed as a voter. Events unconnected with the colonies showed we were nearer a union of the Anglo-Saxon race then woull have been thought possible a year ago. Imperial Federation wes dawning upon the horizon, not for conquest, but for furthering the cause e humanity. The Hon. W. P. Reeves, Agent-General. spok- hopetully of the prospents of an accelerated mail service be tween this country sad the colony.</p>
        <p>_ RAIR PRESURVIIID—AHD—fitiIitiTIFIHD by using the only reliable preparation, RowLarrical lliAcsasan Ott, which strengthens and restores decaying hair. eradicates scurf, and prevents baldness ; no other preparation can produce such a dressy and beautiful appearance of the hair or so stimulate its growth as ltowLaxree Ildicassan OIL. If you have never tried it, von are strongly advised to procure a bottle at once and wintinue using it for your own and your children's hair. Also sold in a Golden Colour for fir. haired ladies and children. Sizes, 3s. 64., 71..10,. 6d• ROWLANDS' ODONTO is the (equal to 4 small). most perfect dentifrice. thoroughly cleanses the teeth from all Impurities, produces a pearl-like whiteness, and prevents decay. Sold by Stores and Chemists. Write A. Rowland and Sons, 20, Hatton Garden, London. for cash prices. Avoid spurious, imita- tions.</p>
