      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>EXAMINEE, FRIDAY, JULY 8 1898</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEE, FRIDAY, JULY 8 1898</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>,„/</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>"OAVITAI.," CHAT.</head>
        <p>"OAVITAI.," CHAT.</p>
        <p>The various celebrations in London and provin- cial towns of Independence Day ought to a•atisfy Jonathan that Britain has fully repented of the great blunder of a former generation, and should 1 contribute to the drawing of the veil of oblivion over the past. This feeling has been growing for some years, and the events of the present war are completing, or at least forwarding, the growth. A formal alliance between the two great branches of 'the English-speaking people is not to be looked for but that they are on the point of new understand- ings with each other is certain. And the effect of a friendly relationship, coupled with the education which America is now receiving as to her own ina- bility to assert that influence to which her popula- tion, her energy, her idtelligence her riches and her riches and her resources entitle her, will prove one of the most potent international factors in the history of the next century.</p>
        <p>Meanwhile the Anglo-American Committee for promoting a closer union between the two peoples is growing daily in strength. It includes thirty peers, the two English Archbishops, Cardinal Vaughan, and the two Irish Archbishops,and a host of commoners of influence and standing. Mr. Asp-oth, Sir Henry F )wler, Sir R. Reid, Sir Walter Foster, and other eminent Liberals have accepted seats upon it; about a doz n prominent lord mayors including the Lord Mayor of Liverpool, have joined it, as have all the Scotch Lord Provosts and the Irish Lord Mayors. Between thirty and forty mayors of large provincial municipalities are aleo. on the committee, not to speak of deans and arch- deacons. chairman of county councils, the Vice- Chancellors of Oxford and Cambridge Universities, the president of the Wesleyan Conference, the chairman of the Bantist Union,the chairman of the Congregational Uoion, and some of our most dis- tinguished literary men. In Mr. Bryce, M.P , the committee have found an excellent chairman,whose work on Arnericon institutions fully'justifies his selection for the office. The movement will rapidly grow, and include all that is best and most repre- sentative in the country.</p>
        <p>The great experiment of the Workmen's Compen- satinn Act, on which the country has just entered, has given insurance companies as we:l as employers much rtex.ety. 13th the Home Secretaty's and Mr. Chamberlain's calculations as to the amount of the insuranle which would have to be paid by employers are being everywhere falsified by experi- ence. In most cases the proposals for insurance have been delayed till the last moment by the em- ' plovers. The first month of the new Act will bring forward with proposals for insurance many who have as yet held aloof or been frightened away by the high premiums which are being demanded. The companies do not, in fact, know wnat to charge, as the arrangement made by many shows— namely, to charge in the meantime a provisional rate on the understanding that a proportion of it should be retuned if tNe first year's working of the Act proves that the risk is not so great as is at pre- sent to be expected. Combinations of employers for mutual protection have broken down very extensively, chiefly from a want of any certainty that the protection would be adequate in the imme- diate fuiiire or lasting as regards the remoter future, fnr the employer, above all things, must have adequate protection against all contingencies, end he will act with more prudence by insuring with a substantial company, even if he has to pay more for it, than by trusting to a combination that may collapse with the first considerable accident.</p>
        <p>A man engszed in the wholesale trade in connpe. tion with millinery houses informs me that,in spite of all saltation to the contrary, the sale of osprey feather l has never been greater than durinv the pregent season The fnct—if fact it is—is not very encouragina to those who have tried to put down the cruel practices involved in obtaining the feathers.</p>
        <p>The Poisons Bill,which is directed against tho un- restricted or unrermlated sale of carbolic acid and similar poisons, hss completed its quiet passage through the Home of Lords, but a strong body of opposition has already formed itself to prevent its passage through the ouse of Commons. The point of objection to it originates with the East End ves- tries, who have to distribute disinfectants among the poor people of the East End. They consider that the Bill will prevent this act of sanitary neces- sirv,and that the interests of public health will suffer to an extent far beyond the advantages which the Bill will carry with it in the way of preventing accidents and suicide. Whether anything can be done to meet this new view of the case is not clear. It is a difficulty which is not confined to London. The vestries have appointed a deputation to wait upon the Duke of Devonshire to represent this diffi- culty, and if an effectire amendment cannot be in- trodueod into the Bill to urge him to allow it to be withdrawn.</p>
        <p>The Post Office telegraphists are entitled to the distinction of 'discovering a decided novelty in the way of strikes or hoycotte,and their mutiny against the food supplied to them is natnrally a rebellion that has a good English foundation. The beer pro- vided is satisfactory enough, and is consumed with relish, but the dinner is said to be bad in quality and badly cooked. This grievance has been a long- standing one, and for the greater part of a year vigorous representations have been made to the head of the Department, with the usual Post\Office result of nothing being done until the men are driven into insurrection. The clerks have begun to carry their own dinners with them,which they now consume in the dining-room. I believe a better dining-room is being built for them, but it is a long way from being readv,and will not meet the griev- ance of the quality of the food The consumption of Post Offiee meat has fallen off from 3,000 dinners to three dozen, and the authorities will have to abandon the red-tape customs which prevail so largely at this post Office. A cure for the evil would he to lot the clerks dine outside for the neighbour- ing restaurante, but they are not allowed to leave the building. The ritualistic controversy so far as London is concerned is practically at an end. It may not be the fact that all the clergy of the ritualistic churches which at the time of Canon Carter's con- ference adopted a non-possumus attitude have fallen into line with the rest of their brethren so far as to accept the principle of the Bishop of London's letter ; but I have the bsst authority for stating that as the outcome of a very representative gathering, held on Friday,their principal objections are removed, and their general acquiescence may be shortly expected. The next move is awaited with interest. The Bishop of Lendon is anxious to meet the wiehes of ritualistic clergy and congrega- ts'" to the utmost po4sible extent. He has lately been busy in examining the various books, of 'meek! Guild services, and these boolv, as re- vised by h'm are being stamped with hiz, imprima- tur. Certainly it, will be no part ot his policy to interfere with the wearing of Eucharistic yes-- menas, fer lim; with those points of ritual which Were held to be legal in the Lincoln judgment. But no one expects that Mr. Rnseit, and his friends will rest contant with such s victors, tie they have ob- tained. Moderate evangelicals are for the most part quite willing to live in harmony with old- fashioned High Churchmen, and wsuld gladly accept the London eirenieon, hut this is hardly the View of others in the Church, who already accuse the Bishop of conceding more to the Ritualists than the law permits. Practically, now that the extre- mists have yielded the situation is almost a repro- duction of the Public Worship Act struggle of 1875.</p>
        <p>--- , On Monday next the Duke of Cambridge will °pee the new line of electric railway which runs underground from Waterloo Station to the Bank of England. This will be a great boon to all living on the south of the Thames who travel to business by the London and South-western Railway, as it fur- nishes them with a short. direct and speedy line, to the her of the City. The railway is only a mile and a half in lengtb,but it has cost half a million of money. kt the Bank the platform of the terminus is 65ft below the level of the street. It will be con- nected underground by means of subways with two other electric railway systems,and a series of radia- tinz tunnel a will give to the public crossings to all : the streets round the Mansion Rase, the 13ank,and ' the Royal Exchange. The committee have com- mitted a great mistake in not providing a station Ludrzate Circus to meet th F leet-wreet and Waterloo traffic. They have been over-ridden with , the idea of having no stopping place between Waterloo and the Bank, and have accordingly cut off an enormously lucrative amount of traffic.</p>
        <p>A serious acei4-nt enciirrel Tuesrhy mnrrtinfr at the Edlnburgh Academy. One of the boys was showing a loaded revolver to some of MI comnaiinne whey' It went ofl the charge wounding a boy n,,med VV.ster. At first the Injnry wee not thoneht serious, hit on being removed to his hlme the lad'a coadttiort agiumed a grave aspect.</p>
      </div>
