      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>RICHNION'D'S EMPLOYiIS AT BLACKPOOL.</head>
        <p>RICHNION'D'S EMPLOYiIS AT BLACKPOOL.</p>
        <p>Once again the employes of Messrs. Richmond and Company, Limited, gas apparatus manufac- turers, of Warritigton, enjoyed the exhilarating breezes of Blackpool on Saturday. Last year the excursion was postponed until September on account of the Jubilee celebrations,but this year the firm reverted to the day following Walking Day. For holiday seekers—and especially ono day trip- pers—fine weather is essential, and in this respect the trippers from Warrington were fortunate,beauti- fully tine weather preeailing throughout the day. A start was 'nada from Bank Quay station, Warring- ton, shortly before half-past seven, when about 700 persons entrained. An uneventful run of two hours brought the ex mrsionists to their desired destina- tion, and they at Once made their way to the pro- menade. The tide was at the full at the dry of arrival, and the visitors experienced a typical Blackpool breeze. Toe places of interest were visi- ted, a popular place being the Tower to which the excursionists were admitted free throughout the day. The arrangements were in the hands of Mr. If. E. Ransom°, the Warrington General Manager, ' and were very satisfactory. At twelve o'clock the managers, foremen, and offiee employees with their friends assembled at the Paiatiae lintel, on the promenade, and partook of an excellent dinner. Mr. E. W. T. Richmood,the chairman and managing director. was at the head of the table, and there were about 80 perseas present. After dinner, a briefltoast list was submitted. The first toast was that of "The Queen,which was proposed by the Chairman. Mr. BALL afterwards proposed the toast of the arm. He said although he was a loyal subject of the Queen he , ventured to think that the toast proposed by the Chair- man was not more important than the toast he had the pleasure of submitting, a toast with which he coupled ' : the names of Mr. Richmond and Messrs. Ransome and Thornton. (Applause.) After referring to the great development of industrial concerns during the century, he said the discriminating historian would record that it was marked by no fact more striking and lasting than the rise and growth of Messrs. Richmond and Company. (Laughter and applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Ricumosn, in rising to respond to the toast, bad a very cordial reception. He said it was once again their privilege to assemble at that breezy spot, and once again he saw around him jovial faces,which he was sure represented contented minds. It was very pleasing as the years rolled on to look back and mark the advance which each twelve months showed. Many of them could take their minds back to the first of those trips,and when they compared the trip of six years ago with the present one the advance and change were most significant. Mr. Hall had been too complimentary in what he had said with regard to the firm's place in the hietory of the British Empire— (laughter)—but they did know this, that the firm had made some slight difference to the town of Warrington. (Applause.) They were also glad to be able to think that the improved condition of affairs had been not only beneficial to Warrington, but a benefit to the East End of London. (Hear, hear.) There was also every pros- pect of it benefiting the south-west of the country. Every time it bad been his good fortune to respond to the toast of the firm at those gatherings some fresh de- velopment had taken place between that time and the time when he last spoke, and now he dared say they were all aware that they were now ,starting another branch,this time at Plymouth. (Hear, hear.) He was very glad to say that the prospects of the business were brighter to-day than ever they had been. (Applause.) Their business seemed to ;priestess an inherent capacity for growing upon its own growth year after year. They saw their orders coming in one year, and they wondered perhaps where they would come from next. They did come, however, and more on top of them, and they felt they had a great deal to be thankful for. (Applause.) The toast which had been drunk was the health of the firm. It was necessary that they should all have health iln order to efficiently carry on their work, and he be- ieved a day spent like that promoted their health. They came out to what had been called one of the lungs of Lancashire, and they returned all the better for the bright sunshine and healthy breezes, and felt they were ready to tackle their work in a proper and efficient manner. Last year they held the trip in September; and it was delayed on account of the Jubilee. This year they came back- to the end of June or the beginning of July, and ho was very glad to think that they would have no occasion, so far as he could tell, to break off the trip or throw it as late as last year. (Hear, hear.) He thought a trip like that right in ethe middle of the season was a breathing time for them all. They were aware that it was necessary toeturn out their work promptly and efficiently. (Hear, bear). He was sure Mr. Ransome could tell them of telegrams comine to the works something to this effect," Why don't you send our stoves ?" " Cant wait any longer. Must buy 180111 e inferior ones." (Laughter and applause.) The firm had lost a day or two's work by the holidays, but the employei had got the fresh air, and he was Sure they would go back and turn out more stoves than hitherto. The firm were supported right through by efficient and energetic employee, and they in their turn were controlled and managed by two of the most ener- getic and enterprising young men who had grown up in the gas stove trade. Be referred to Mr, Raesome and Mr. Thornton. (Applause). They all knew their ster- ling worth and character. They had grown up right in their own sight, and it had been a proud moment for him (Mr. Richmond) to feel that those gentlemen were now connected in an official capacity with the direction of the company. (Applause). fie only hoped they might I be spared for many years to work on behalf of the firm 11 and make the company's success, which had so far been ' phenomenal, even greater in the future than in the past. Be also hoped that the crofit-sharing introduced last , year and inaugurated in a most striking manner (with Mr. George Livesey in the chair) some few months ago, might meet with a full measure of success. (Applause). 1 He trusted the beginning of that scheme might lead to ' larger profits and corresponding larger distributions. It I was a system whereby the men were taught to save. If I they only distributed something which would make the men's homes brighter, their wives better supported and their children better fed and clothed, he would consider that the company had not been created in vain. (Hear. hear.) Their object was not merely to sell stoves ; it was also to endeavour to raise the standard and condition of those employed by them, and if that endeavour resulted in that they would be well satisfied. (Applanse.) There never was any scheme promoted for the benefit of any class but that met with the bitterest opposition and the keenest criticism of those who ought to be the last to speak, namely those who did nothing. It was easy to find fault and criticise but let those who criticised find something as satisfac- tory before they attempted to say what others did was wrong. (Hear, hear.) He thought the scheme they had Promoted was calculated to do a great deal of good</p>
        <p>t i</p>
        <p>Hear, hear.) It Was an honest eedeavour to do thing to show time tney appreciated lemestly the Of those whim they emptoyeri. They wished who worked for them to save, and their mite desire in that way to teach them thrift Everyone should shown the road and helped along the patn which we, lead them to something upoe which to &amp;peed in ti old age besides the workhouse. (A.pplause,) Old I. pensions might be the subject of possible leeisletion the future. They must, however, look after themeele Providence helped those who help therneivee, and they followed th it precept there was all the more chew of the future opening up brightly for them. In conr 8100 he thanked Mr. Hall for his remarks, and In"' that fortune might smile upon them in the future aS had done in the past,and that it might lied them we/ of it. (Athelense.) Mr. H. E. itaresous said he experieneed a diffieulty , speaking after Mr. Richmond and Mr. 11 ell for they I taken the wind out of his rails. (Laughter.) He that a great responsibility rested boon him in real ' ine as the repreeentative of the Warrington' works:. ! were aware of the Wei pressure under which teY been working at Warrington but he thought the exPI , tious way M which the work had been got out ' . satisfactory to all concerned, (Hear, hear.) Ile pleased to see Mr. Thornton from tho Stratford If with them on that occasion, and he would like to " that opportunity of exteniine the good wishes of men at the Warrington works to those at Serer( (Hear, hear) E(e quite agreed with the remark of Richmond that outings like the present did goi them all. They felt better fitted for their werk, ae could assure them there was plenty to do when Chef lank. (Laughter aud applause.) Mr. Temexiber in the course of a few remarks is pending to the toest said they could not help feel encouraged with tine development of the hueiness of,e, company. He thought they were progrevaiing at greater rate in the North than in the South. Strat! was commenced about that time lest year, They new 70 or 80 hands, and before another twelve WI; had Palmed the company hoped that number waifl? increased to 200. (Hear, hear). Plymouth alio been opened, and they heped ftrim there to get a I deal of trade for the Stratford Works. Without disparagement to the travellers or others conned with the firm lee could ray that their Cheirman A Managing Director had brought about this great inert in their business. (Applause). They had at the beau the firm &amp;gentleman who not only encouraged theta employe.% hut one who was going round the CO using his influence to bring trade to their works. ofs long as Mr. Richmond continued to do that they Woe go forward until they were far ahead of any other pup. f.'so long as they had him and a g.d staff betliteo the office and in the works they bad not the 003.03 need to fear for the future. He hoped that that Po. next year they would be able to reporz as great a P grass as they had reported this year. (Applause). ,ets The CHAIRMAN then proposed the toast of the 04, staff, the foremen and men,and the visitors, and Plotalei tribute to the good work done by the office etaff,for,9„d te and employds generally.—The toast was by Mr. Hagler% Mr. Moody, Mr. Stirrop and Dr. 1°1; tes MA., LL.D. The latter, in responding on behalf Oeost ' visitors, congratulated the firm on starting tbe sharing scheme. He believed that the proper acaT,7o raise the working man was not by pauperising 1310, big' old age pensions and otit-door relief but by enablWds is with his own efforts to be independent of these,l'cors; aid age. (Hear, hear.) Messrs. Richmond tinietre," pany were engaged in an experiment which,hairl9l°' would lead to the solution of this great ecenebl`" blew. (Applause.) isdf The CHAIRMAN also proposal the • health r the lecturers, and thes_Elaniger of the relaiine Trete! (to. Ericsson) for Ms excellent catering. The manager P log replied the Meeting terminated.</p>
