      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>AD MISS A. LITTON.</head>
        <p>AD MISS A. LITTON.</p>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF DR. REGINALD SMITH</p>
        <p>Considerable local interest was manifested in the wedding of Mr. Reginald Smith, M.R.C.S., L.R.C.S. (London),eldest son of the late Dr. Thomas Starkey Smith,of Warrington,and Miss Amy Litton, daughter of the late Major Litton) P., of Hill Crest, Giappenhall, which was solemnised in Di.resburV Parish Church en Thursday last week.and witnessed by a large and fashionable gathering. The chancel had been beautifully decorated for the occasion by Mrs. John White and Mrs. Herbert Woods, with flowers and plants which had been lent by Mrs. I Jones, of the Elms. A carpeted way led from the I gateway to the porch of the church, and it was lined with a number of prettily attired girls carrying 1 baskets of flowers. During the assembling of the I guests and general public in the church,the organist Mr. John Wright, played several voluntaries on the organ including the March by King Hall, in G., and the March by Scotson Clarke in D. Punctually at two o'clock the bride entered the church leaning on the arm of Captain R. idgway,who gave her away. She looked charming in a dress of white satin soliel souple trimmed with bouillonnes of chiffon,with her mother's veil of an- tique Brussels lace. Her ornament was a diamond star. Her bridesmaids were the Misses Lil and Gertrude Litton (sisters of the bride), and the Misses Dorothy and Hilda Smith (sisters of the bridegroom). 'rhey were attired in white glace silk, trimmed with Valenciennes lace, with green moir6 sashes and white hats to match. The bride's bouquet was composed of white exotics, the gift of the bridegroom, whilst those carried by the brides- maids were sweet peas. Dr. Montgomery acted as best man. The service was fully choral, the sur- pliced choir being.in attendance. The service com- menced with the singing of j Father all creating," and the congregation also joined in singing "0 per- fect love." The officiating clergyman was the Rev. H. T. Clark (vicar), assisted by the Rev. P. H. of the register Walesthe o rg(acnuirsattge Wely, and a march by bcotson Clarke in G. As the gave D a urrienngdetrhie Signingofthe Offertoire by rendering , newly-married couple left the church Mendelssohn's I"Wedding March" was played. They were greeted with the usual showers of confetti as they walked to their carriage, and as the party drove off to Moore, the church bells rang out a merry peal in honour of the event. The guests proceeded to the Beeches, Moore,the residence of the bride's mother. There was a liberal oieolay of bunting in the vil- lage. An arch had been erected over the gate lead- ing to the house, the motto "Happiness and Pros- perity" being conspicuously displayed in the centre. Large flags were also displayed from the tower of the house and at.rieveral other places in Moore. The ' presents numberin g over 100, included a silver tray from the Warrington Borough Police Force, and a handsome marble clock from the members of the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club. Presents had also been received from the villagers of Moore and many of the patients of Dr. Smith. The honey- moon is to be spent in Scotland. The bride's travel- ling dress was blue CaTiViti do chine trimmed with guipure, and blue straw ha:, to match.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON liEjfirl PAROCII[AL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a tneeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Com- mittee, held at St. Thomas's School, on Thursday last week. With Mr. G. H. Mason (in the chair), there were present :—Messrs. G. Pilling, F. Pearson, H. Clarke, Ashton Hayes, W. Mclntyre. J. Ashton (clerk), J. Far- rington (inspector), and J. Swinton (surveyor). The minutes of the last meeting were read and unani Mously passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>CORRESPONDENCE .</head>
        <p>CORRESPONDENCE .</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that he had received the following lowing letter :—Juno 24th. Dear Sir.—l am acting for 1 Messrs. Hayes, Bros., with reference to land they are leasing from Mr. Davenport in Mitchell-street, Stockton heath. My clients have handed me their letters in the matter, thinking that the stables have been erected already on the praperty, and on which the question of passing plans arises, I have advised my clients to enter into the bond you mention in your letter of the lath inst to Mr. Daven- port, providing they would have twelve months notice before the stables have to be removed.—Yours truly, A. J. WILLET.—The following letter bad also been received Mr. John Ashton. Dear sir,—ln answer to yours of the loth respecting erection of stables for me, I will under- take to remove the stables or convert into a store shed within three months from time of being served with notice lfrom the District Council to do so. I am yours I respectfully WILLIAM ARNOLD. Mr. Pilling proposed that six' rn,ntba net Lae he given. The proposition was seconded, and carried unanimously. SURVEYOR'S REPORT. _ _</p>
        <p>The Surveyor's report was read and approved. Mr. Swinton said he bad been instructed a few weeks ago to report on a sewer but he had not had time to go into the question, and if they would excuse him until next month he would have time to go into the matter pro- perly. The Council agreed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>LATCHFORD WITHOUT SEWAGE.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD WITHOUT SEWAGE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pilling informed the meeting that he had attended a meting ot the Latchford Without Parish Council and they wanted to know if Stockton Death would consent to them simply paying the whole of the sewerage rate. It was decided to fm a select committee to consider th matter, the date of the meeting to he fixed later. The following were appointed on the Committee : Messrs. Ashton Hayes, W. Mclntyre, Davenport, Mason, and Clarke. This was all the business.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. Week ending June 25' h Durina the week the following births and deaths were registered : Births. Deaths. Warrington Registration District 45 16 The excess of births over deaths was 29. Birth Rate 37.2 per 1,000 of the population. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 13.2 per 1,000, against 17.9 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 11.7 the corresponding period of last year. The death- rate from the seven chief zvmotic diseases was equal to 08 per I,ooo,against 0.6 the average rate of the previous four weeks, and against 0 8 the correvondiog period of 1897. The sees of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 tip. 3 o 1 1. 8 3 16. The deaths included-1 from enteric fever, 1 from phthisis, 7 from lung diseases, 1 from heart disease, 8 from all other causes. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week:—Nd. Total number of patients in hospital, week ending Saturday, 25:h June Nil. METEOROLOGY—The mean t emperature was 58.1 Fah. and therefore 1.6 deg. higher tl'ari the preceding week. 'Fhe maximum temperature 73. F. was lecord ed On the 22nd inst., and the minimum temperature 47 F. on the 25th inst. JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
