        <head>KIR; GILMOUR AGAIN RAISES OBJECTIONS.</head>
        <p>KIR; GILMOUR AGAIN RAISES OBJECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Chairman having moved the confirmation of the minutes of the previous Council meeting, Mr. (Amour rose and asked whether he could speak on the minutes. The Chairman : That is the same question raised at the previous meeting. You are here to confirm the pro- ceedings of the last Council meeting. You cannot re- view the proceedings. Mr. Gilmour: It is no use coming here if we cannot speak on the minutes. The Chairman: Oh, yea. It is for ths Council to confirm them if they are correct. Mr. Gilmour : I must try to put you right if possible. I must be able to discuss resolution even though I have objected to them in committee. The Chairman : We must do everything in order. The time of the Council must not be ocoupied by these questions which are matters of procedure. Mr. Gilmour: Will you LiVe me the Standing Order which prevents me from speaking on these minutes ? The Chairman: I have already explained why I rule you out of order. Mr. G hnour : I obj.ct to the minutes in clause 131 referring to the contract for a new retort house at the Gas Works.— .</p>
        <p>The Chairman : I cannot hear you further.</p>
        <p>The minutes were then passed... CEMETERY COMMITTEE —THE PROPOSED MORTUARY.</p>
        <p>The Cemetery Committee -minutes stated that plans for a urinal at the Cemetery proposed to be erected be- hind the Mortuary Chapel were submitted, whereupon it was resolved that the plan be approved and adopted.— The question of charges at the Cemetery for headstones was brought before the committee, and it was resolved that the minute of the Highways Committee of the 18th June, 1894, deciding that a headstone of over five feet in height be charged as a monument, be rescinded, and that the Chairman, Mr. Ormand, and Mr. McMahon be a sub-committee to enquire further as to these charges, and also to take into consideration the suggestion of erecting the proposed mortuary at the C3metery, instead of in the yard adjoining the Town Hall. Mr. Simpson moved the confirmation of the minutes which were approved. GAS MANAGER'S REPORF.</p>
        <p>Mr. SwanninThis—niOnTilly report stated that the new steam boiler was now in use,and working in a very sat- isfactory manner. As the small engine and washer scrubber had not yet been covered in, he thought it would be as well to have this done during the summer, as these machines would do their work better if they were protected from the weather. In the water depart- ment they had laid the new 4ft. water main in Rydal-at. and had also put in four new services. During the month the hydrants had all been examined aud tested, and were now in good working order. The consumption of water during the month, as per registering of the station tneters,was as follows :—Newton Bridge meter, 3,390,000 gallons ; Viaduct Works, 2,650,000 gallons ; Parkside, 12,000 gallons; total, 6,052,000 gallons. Cor- responding month last year, 6,846,000 gallonF; decrease, 794,000 gallons. The committee resolved that the small engine and washer scrubber at the gasworks be covered in fortlawith,and also the:the provision of suitable stand pipes and hydrants for use for street watering carts be left in the hands of Mr. Swann.</p>
        <p>PROPOSAL TO SUPPLY ELECTRICITY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>At the meting of the Gas and Water Committee, a I letter, dated June let, was read, from Mr. J. H. Bentley, lelectrician, Eirleatown, stating that it was his intention to generate and supply electricity, on the overhead system, in the Eirlestown district ot Newton-in-M tker- field. Atter consideration it was ordered that the Clerk reply, stating that the Committee cannot recognise tha right of any company or person to generate and supply electricity withi-, their district, unless the Electric Lighting Acts of 1882 and 1888 are complied with with respect to applications to the Board of Trade for the right to supply electricity by license or a Provisional Order, after due notice has been given to and the con- sent of this Authority obtained to such applcatioo.— The Clerk read a report upon the Electric Lighting Acts of 1882 and 1888. which was accepted by the Commit- tee, and after consideration it was resolved that the matter be referred to the Gasworks Extension Commit- tee, for their e.oneidera.tion and rep/rt. The minutes of the committee were approved on the proposition of the chairman. HIGHWAYS AND SANITARY COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>This Committee met en Juno 20th. rho Assistant Sanitary Inspector's Monthly report was read and ac- cepted. It stated that during the four weeks ending Wednesday the 15th inst., the staff removed 158 loads of pails, 83 loads from astmits, and 203 loads of dry rub- bish—total 449 loads at a total cost of £63 4s. or 2s. 911. per load. Since the last report 37 notices had been served on owners and occupiers to abate nuisances, and they were being fairly attended to. He was very glad to be able to report that the whole of the district is clear from all infectious diseases, and there were no cases in hospital. He had to apply for a new tank cart, the one they required replacing was far worn when they got it, about six years since. It was now leaking and past re- pairing.—The Committee ordered that tenders be ob- tained for a new tank cart.—Mr. R. Walton, Crow-lane, wrote stating that he had to complain of a nuisance at the farm adjoining his house. The Assistant Inspector of Nuisances stated that he had inquired into the corn- plaint,and found everything on the premises complained of in a thoroughly sanitary condition.—The Committee ordered the Clerk to reply accordingly.—The Assistant Surveyor's monthly report was read and accepted. Mr. Bowes stated that in the absence of the surveyor he had to report that since the last meeting the footpath in War- grave-road, below Emmanuel Church, had hen com • pleted ; Golberne Dale-road, Ashton-road, Bolton-road, and part of Parkside-road had been trimmed ; and vari- ous repairs to kerbing and channels had been executed. The tanks at the Central Sewage Works had been cleaned out, and Sc, also, had the manholes in St. Helens-road, Crow-lane, and the whole of Newton. The latter portion, of the sewer in Wargrave-road had been flushed ou'..-1 The toll collector reported that during the four weeks ended, June 18th, he had collected £44 16a. 51., as against £35 6a. 41., corresponding period last yeer.—A letter dated June 14, was read from Mr. W. Gilbert, of Ashton-road, formerly withdrawing his six months' no- tice, re nightsoil deposit agreement.—The committee ordered that before taking steps with reference to G. Ronaynees occupation of a wooden structure as a dwell- ing house, Mr. Worsley be requested to give his opiaion with reference thereto.--A letter dated June Bth, was read from the engineer of Post Office Teleerapes, Liver- pool, requesting the alteration of a telegraph pole at the junction of High-street and Ashton-road, from private land on to the footpath as shewn on the plan. It was resolved that the Postal Authorities be allowed to remove the pole in question,but that not more than one- half be allowed to encroach on the footpeth.—A discus- sion took place as to the raising of ways and means for the making of some of the private streets, which most urgently require it, after which it was resolved that a sum of £2,000 be placed at the disposal of the Highways and Sanitary Committee for Works of Private Street lin- provement,such sum to be transferred and allocated as may be neeessary,and as may be directed by the Chair- man of the Finance Committee.—lt was resolved that Mr. Cellingwooi, Mr. Barron,Mr.Ormand,and Mr, Wood be a sub-committee for the purpose of approaching Messrs. R. Evans and Co., with a view of influencing them to prsceecl with the making of Athol, Albion, Sec., streets, and also of recommending to tee committee which other streets should he proceeded with (not ex- ceeding in eost a sum of £2,000) which most urgently require attention. It was also resolved that the quota- tion of the Engine Boiler, &amp;1., Insurance Co., Limited, of 12, King-street, Manchester, for the insurance of the steam road roller for the sum of £5OO, at an annual premium of £1 15s. be accepted ; the policy to cover all damage, not only to the boiler itself, but to surrounding property and plant of every description in case of ex- plosion.—The following resolutions were also passed by ' the committee : That the Chairman of the Committee he authorised to interview Mr. Pardey with reference to the drain from the pits on Newton Common being re- opened; that the Assistant Surveyor be authorised to alter the grid at Emmett's Brow as shown on Man now submitted ; and that the plan for 3 housee in Crow-lane for Mr. William Taylor be approved and passed. A letter, dated June Mh, was read from the London and North- Western Railway Co. ,stating that they propose to renew Penkford Bridge on July 4th, 5:6, and 6t13, and that the traffic on those days would be interfered with.— Before the minutes were submitted for confirmation, the Clerk read the minutes of the sub-committee with refer- ence to Athol, Albion and other streeta. The sub com- mittee recommended that these streets be paved with setts instead of the material usually used.and that in the event of the cost being more, the difference be paid by the Council, and that tenders be invited forthwith. Mr. Couingwood moved the confirmation of the min- utes which were unanimou.lv approved of. GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE. This committee resolved that the tender of Mr. J. G ,C. Starkie of £6 is. 101 for inside painting and paper- ing of the Hallkoeper's house, as per sp 'cification be ac- cepted ; that the matter of obtaining tenders for inside painting and papering of the roal foremen's and assis- tant sanitary inspector's houses, and alteration of the kitchen range in the latter, be left in the hands of the clerk, and that a stationery, Set , cupboard be provided for the collector's office.</p>
        <p>The minutes were passed on the proposition of the Chairman.</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE. It was recommended by this committee that the salary of the junior clerk in the clerk's office he incrosied from 93. per week to 12s. per week, with T d van ces of 33. week per annum, from June Ist, 1.899, until he reacne4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>EX A MINER. FRIDAY, TULY 8, Ib9B.</head>
        <p>THE EX A MINER. FRIDAY, TULY 8, Ib9B.</p>
        <p>rhe age of 21 years.—Mr. Ormond moved, Mr, Evans Seconded, the confirmation of the minutes which were agreed to. THE CONTRACT FOR THE NEW RETORT AT THE</p>
        <p>GAS WORKS. The Chairman moved that the seal of the Council be affixed t the following :—An agreement between Messrs. T: and R. W. Bower' Limited, of Woodlesford, near Leeds, and the Council, for the supply of cannel to the Gas Works, during the year ending May 31st, 1889 ; an agreement between Mr. Thomas Woods, of St. listens. and the Council, for the erection of new retort at the Gas Works ; and an agreement between Mr. R. Jones, of dridge-street, Earlestown, and the Council, for carting and team labour as may be required by the Gas and Water Committee, for the year ending June 30tli, 1899. Mr. Ormand seconded. Mr. Gilmour moves an amendment that the tender of Mr. Woods be not accepted. Mr., Ormand said Mr. Gilmour was out of order.. Mr. Gilmour (continuing) said he objected to the acceptance of the tender in committee. He contended that when Al-asrs. Rothwell and Sons withdrew from their tender, the other two contractors who sent in ten- ch-re ought to have been asked to submit another tender.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said if Mr. Gilmour would move that the seal of the Council be not affixed to the agreement he would be in order. Mr. Gilmour said he would do so. He raisei the questi4n because Mr. Woods name never catne before the committee until the last moment. Two local builders submitted tenders for the work, one £3O and the oth LW above the accepted tender, and he conteade I that when the firm selocted withdrew their tender, the local builders ()wilt to have been asked to submit another tender before the Council decided to give the contract to Mr. Woods, of S. Helens. If the local noon had been asked they would have been glad to have gone down £3O in order to keep the St. Helens men out of the dis- trict. Mr. Woods' name was never before them and was contrary to toe standing orders. The Chairman remarked that the committee adepted Mr. Woods as a substitute for the lowest teader re- ceived. Mr. Gilmour : He was oked after Messrs. Rothwell's had given it up. Mr. Ormand said Mr. Woods was never asked bv the Council to accept the contract at Messrs. It othwell's estimate. He did not think it fair for Ntr. Gemoar to mislead the public, Mr. Woods was a substitution for Mr. Rothwell. Mr. Gilmour sal i Mr. R othwell introduced Mr. Woods name, and stated that Mr. Woods would accept the con- tract at the same mice. His (Mr. Gilmour's) contention was that the two local builders would have been wiling to accept the contract at the same figure if they had been asked. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Ormand said he wag glad that Mr Gilmour had confirmed his opinion that the Gmincil didn't ask Mr. Woods.</p>
        <p>Mr. McMahon said in the acceptance of tenders by the Council, the contractor who was lowest got the contract if he was respectable. H • did not see that the Council should throw ratepayers money away for tha saka of in- dividuals. They were all anxious to keep trade in the town but local men must conform to the general rule re- specting the acceptance of contracts. The minutes were then passed. THE TATE MR. GLADSTONE. Mr. Herbert Gladstone wrote on behalt of his mother and family thanking the Council for their very kind message or sympathy. Mr. Gihnour moved that the letter be entered in the minute book of the Council. This was agree It) and the Council rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>IMPERINL P.RLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERINL P.RLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>HOUSE OF COMMONS.—MONDAY• • In the House of Commons on Monday, the ATTORNEY GENERIL for Ireland, in reply to a question put by Mr. Kilb ride with reference to the recent riots at Belfast, sail the Belfast police bad not carried rifles since 1886. It was incorr cot. therefor, to say that the police were deprived of their rifles on the occasion in qnestion. The men outside the city, in the district of the Nationalist demonstration, were armed in accordance with the regulations—that was one- third with rifles and the remainder with truncheons. Mr. L.:alio, replying to 'sir. ClOrmbie, said that in the course of the operations against rabies muzzling orders had been appl'ed to the whole or parts of 3?. counties. The number had now been reduced to 19. There were twelve cases of the disease during the first 26 arEeka of the present yo,r, as against 85 in the o arrespondliag period of last year, 32.7 is that of 1895, and 413 in that of 1895. Mr CH AMDF.RLAIN, replying to Mr. Hedderwick, said a propotal for the imposition of the hut tax on the Gold Coast w as made by the late Governor. bot he (Tr, Chamberlain) would not take it into consideration until he had recoivel the report of the Royal Commissioner who was inquiring into the opera- tion of the tAx in Sierra Leone Mr RITCHIE, io rep vto Lord Balearres, said fetal pm-eedings were pm Hog ag iinst the driver in connection with the Leyland rat, Way accident, and f pretedent. the report of the Government in- spector would not be putdished until they hid been brought to a cl iss.—Sir H. flloxedlEacs, in reply to Mr. Dillon said th•a war excenditure south of Assouan wae being defrayed by the EgyptLn Government. The extra charge for the British farce north of Assouan was being temporarily met from the Army Votes,but would be refunded by the Egyptian Governnseat. The expense of the expedition to Kartoum would be provided by the Ogyptian Government.—Mr J. REDMOND afterwards rose to call attention to the over-taxa- tion of Ireland, and moved the following resolution a —"That the disproportion between ths taxation of Ireland and it, taxable opacity as compared with the other parts of the kingdom disclosed by the findings of the Royal Commission constitutes a grievance and demands the early attention of the Government with a view to proposing a remedy." Mr. Lecky seconded the motion. Sir M HIOXS-BEloa replied on behalf of the Government.At midnight the debate was ad• journed until to-day. The House role at five minutes past twel a e. HOUSE OF LORDS—TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords on Tuesday, Earl CARRINGTON ' moved the second reading of th Allotments (London) Bill, which, he explained, was to facilitate the provision of allot- ments by the London County Connell. Lord HARRIS op- posed and the Hart ofJCIMMCRLEV supported the Bill, which, on a division, was thrown out by a majority of 62. On the motion of Lord BALFOUR of Burleigh, the Poorlaw (Scat- land) Bill—which proposes to reduce the period required to acquirers settlement by residence from fims years to three— Was real a third time. On the motion of the Earl of HARD- MICICE the Aliens Biil was read a third time, and on the question that the Bill do pass Lord HERRIEI proposed the omission from the third clause of the words wh!ch em- powered the inspectors of the Board of Trade to prevent the landing of any alien who was without means of support. After some discussion, the amendment was, on a division. re- jected by a majority of 50. and the Bill passed. The Libraries (Offences) Bill passed through comm,ttee, and the House ad- journed. HOUSE OF COMMONS.—TITESDAY. In the House of Commons, Sir HENRY CAMPBELL BARNERMAN brought up the report of the Grand Committee on Law on the Vaccination Bill. A number of petitions were again presented from public bodies in Ireland in favour of the resolution on the financial relations question then before , the House. Mr. BALEoUR, answering Mr. 11.seiderwick. said 'lt was proposed to introdoce a bill in the course of the present session with respect to the snp.rannuation of public school teachers, but he could not fix the date at present.—The adjourned debate on Mr. John nedmoncis motion regarding the financial relations of Great Britain and Ireland was re- sumed by Co onel SAIINDERSON, who said the Irish members did not come as be,rgars, but as men with whom the Govermmut of Engl ina had made a bargain Which had Rot been fulfillei The debate was continued by Lord Charles Beresford, Sir John Colornb, and Mr. Swift M.,cl4elll,—Slr WILLIAM HARCOURT, after remarking thst it could not be denied that Ireland ought to be considered as financially a separate entity, said if the Government was to do anything which was effectual, it must either be by a reduction of Hugiand's enormous expen liture, or else by an increase of direct taxation. If the question was to be dealt with wisely andjustly. some system must be devised which should have r•jgard to the taxable capacity of all the people of the United kingdom. The subsequent speakers included Mr. Clancy, Mab'r Ranh Major J., meson. Sir John Lubbock, Mr. Hemp- hill, and others. Mr. Betyoura, in the curse of his reply. said he did not cl.ny that. if a case was shown, Ireland had a right to scparat I fiscal treatment and that was true of other parts of the country. O. a division the figures were —For the resolution, 144 ; against, 285.</p>
        <p>HOUSE OF CONlNlONS.—WEwritaralf. The Irish Loc.-al Government Bill as amended in committee W 5 consicleret in the House of Commons on Wednesday. The Speaker ruled that the first two new clauses on the notice paper In the name of the Chief Secretary. and which related to the powers of county councils as to the purchase of quarries and steam rollers, to obtaining materials, and to recreation and public walks, were out ar order at, the pr i sent stage be- cause they propose to give to county councils power to spend money out of the rates —Mr. G. BALFOUR, who appeared in the House for the first time since his ill ess, said he under- stood that this rutin,: would subsequently be applied to other Clauses and to a variety or amendments proposed by the Government. He proposed. therefor's, that whm the report stage ended the Bill should be recommitted in respect of these aid 50-00 Private members' causes which during committee were postponed at the request of the G wernment.—ln reply ta Mr.Dilloa and others,Mr.G.Bst.yovn said he hoped six days would be enough for the report stage and the retcdminittal of the bill.-..A number of the proposed new clauses having been disposed of. Mr. T. M. itEkLY moved a new chastse providing for the appointment of aud:t ins from in list to be settled by the Locai Government Board of chirterei or other profes- sional accountants in practice in helmet. —Mr. G. BSLYOUR onno3ed the proposal, and on a division it was rejected by 211 votes to 110.—A clause, moved by Sir J. A. WILTAX, woyiding that newspaper reporters should be allowed to attend all meetings of county and district councils other than Committee meetings was rejected by 288 votes to 77, and further consideration of the msasure was adjourned.—On a resolution to authorise the payment of such expenses as may be incurred in the carrying out of the London University Commission Bill several ea irnb.rs objected.—Mr. T. M. HEALY OXplAilled that Eng.and had already .got a great number of universities, an the Irish members believed that she ought not to he allowed to have another until Ireland got a Catholic University. -Tres motion stool adjourned.— The House roe shortly before six o'clock.</p>
        <p>At Thravood Cottaze Hospital a very favourable re- port WAR given as to th? cmdition of Piatt-Betts. HA j 3 makiog satiaf actory Progress, and is regarded as being out of danger.</p>
        <p>wHAT cAuBEi PINIVLES ? Clogging of the puree. The little plug in the centre of the pimple l called a blaekhad, grub, or comedone. The only real preventa• tive is CUTICIIIit SOAP, the most effective skin purifying and beautifying soap in the world, as well as purest and stvestebt for toilet. oath, and nursery. It is the only preveutative of pimples, blackheads, red,rough and oily skin, red, rough hanisovith shapeless nails, dry,thin and falling hair.and simple h thy blonfshes bEcauan the only I preventative of infl tmmation and elogging, of the pores, the cause of minot affections of skin, scalp, and h or. sale greater than tho combined sale of all Darr skin a(1 complexion soaps.</p>
