      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>G;‘,NERAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>G;‘,NERAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Afghanistan has a regular army of about 63,0)0 men. Blind men outnumbcr blin.l women by two to one. The machinery of a first class battleship weighs about 1,300 toas.</p>
        <p>Five hLuired tradfng vessels leave the iharnes daily for all parts of the world.</p>
        <p>Oa July 6th the earth was farther away from the sun than at any other time.</p>
        <p>More cues of consumption appear among needle makers and iilemakers than among any other class of labourers. Playing-cards were first printed about 1350. It is esti. mated that the present annu ii output exceeds 7,000,030 picks a Wear.</p>
        <p>:As a recompense for soldiers who have been wonnded In battle and dischArgel o this account, Spain jallows such men to beg in the public streets. An teronant wtll attempt to cross the Alps in a billoon next summer. His intention is to take photographic views of the mountains and valleys waile in the air. A. dispatch from Monte Video states that the revolt which had broien oat in that city has b.en quelled, the insurgents hiving made their submission to the Government.</p>
        <p>Three stokers were scalded on the torpedo-kat destroyer "Desperate "thrlugh a boiler tube bursting whilst the vessel was cruising In the klmth Se I. Happily tin men's injuries are not dangerous. The death sentence passed upys Alice 11)13!nson, wh3 pleaded guilty to strangling li-r chili and olacia r its body Ira a ditch. at Rowner. near Fareliam, has boen commuted to penal servituie fcr lite.</p>
        <p>In...st novelty in psdiocks is one w;th al auxiliary chamber containing an explosive for ere.tiqg au alarm on the lock ben g tampered with. Tits explosive is tired by a hammer hiside the bk.</p>
        <p>Peat appears to be a most excellent carbonaceous substance for use in blast furnaces It tits heeu found in &amp;tuella that well-dried peat, use wth the hot blast, will yield more than its own weight of pig-iron. At the aij uirned coroner's inquiry ore Tuesday into the death of thirty-three of the victims of the Alnio.i launch dis- aster at Slack wad, me vcrd at of Accidental death " was re- turned, FLU tie tie being attached to anyone. Says Sir Walter Besant in the Author " :—" A good writer, in these days, as easily gets a goo' living as a good doctor. Ile cannot, of ersrs make a colossal fortune like a man in business ; but he is not a pauper nor a dependent.'' Drums,which are now used throughout the British Army. were first introduced in'o Lumps by ths Saracens. The fife was introsluced into the linglish Army by the Duke of Cumberland in 1735 The Guards were the first corps to adopt it.</p>
        <p>A great storm broke over M Arid on Sunday. In a suburb a WOrll4ll wts ',ruck by lightning and killed and several people were injure i The we tth,u. on vloudiy was still tens- Pestuous and reie‘raphio own nunic ttion was only carried on with great dlill .ulty. For one of our fast cruise-s to steam for a whole day at top wenty-one or more kno s —would mean the burning of considerably over 330 teas of coal. At about 17 knots she would burn 253 toys, and at eleven knots only seventy-five tons, in twenty-four hours. Robert Rawlings, who hid acted as postmaster at Sutton Bridge for m tny years. WAS aeateneed by Mr Justice Law- rence at the Lincolnshire As lass on Tuesday to five years' , Penal servitule for ht. ing conmitted a series of frauds on , the Postoffl:e his dtf alcations amounting to £1.200. _</p>
        <p>The Princesi of Wales receives many anonymous letters. They come to her on all sots of subjects, often enclosing p-esents. Recently she received a box with holes punctured In the sides, and, when it wee opened, a tiny white dog Jumped out. Her Royal Highness has kept the dog. _ .</p>
        <p>The Duke of York who gets £l2O a year half-pay, will receive £B3) a year whi!e he is in command of the "Crescent." bir Charles (lust, who is one of the Duke's equerries has been appointed fit st lieutenant of the "Crescent." He WAS for three years in the Royal yacht. Osborne," and previously in the " Alexandra...</p>
        <p>A boring has just been made at Rybintz, in Silesia, in Which the earth's crust has been penetrated to the depth of 2,210yd5. This is the greatest depth ever attained so far .The boring was made for scientidc purposes. No unusual teatures. however. presented themselves, except that the therrnorniiter in-licated at that depth the pretty high tempera- ture of 150 degrees Fahrenheit. The Americ Ins have brought out a ring gun In contradis- tinction to our wire gun, with the advantage of being dis- mountahle. Rings of steel take the place of the wire used by 110 to strengthen the rifled tube These rings 04/1 be taken off when the gun is dismounted for ti-ansport. They are hired on the tube by nu, arrangement of four longituainal bAts. The cannon nits not yet !nen thoroughly tried S English is spoken by 45,0)0,003 persons in the British Isles, by probsbly 57,033,000 of the 60,000,000 inhabitants of the United St ites. b 7 4,0)0,000 in Canada. by 3,000,000 in Aus- Irlitby 3.731,030 Westindians,and by 1,000,003 in India and other British Colonies. bringing the total of the English spaeking race to considerably over 10 ,000,000. A gentle in who has recently spent a month in Samoa reports that till, house in which Robert LJuis Stevenson passed th t last years of his life is going rapidly to ruin. Ac- cording to •• Lounger.' in the "sew York Critic," it is at Present iminhaiiitcd, sod is likely to be so for tie rest or its days. though it is reportisd thali :ars. Stevenson talks of ioing back th-re.</p>
        <p>The enrolees inquiry Into the loss of seven lives throuth the disastrous fire at Scarborough on the 8 h ult was con- cluded on Tuesday night. The hearing of the evidence his o^cupied seven sittings. The coroner summed uo at consider- able l-ngth, and the jury retired about eleven o'clock to con- sider their verdict. After an lames ab,ence they resurned an open verdict.</p>
        <p>- - Jenny Lind's mad lover has just died in America. His name was Tobias Van Steenberg, and he was seventy years of sge He lust his reason through his unrequitted adinira- tion for the great singer. He spent the savings of years in followhg her abaut,but his aoproaches were always repulsed. He eventually took possession of a hovel in which he lived for forty years, and in which he died.</p>
        <p>- - -•--- On Sunday night a murder was perpetrated at Paulstown, about ten miles from Kilkenny. The victim was an old wondaa who lived alone on the mountain and kept a huxter's shop. On Monday morning, on entering the house, a neighbour came across tke desd body of th 3 de:eased lying on the floor of her bedroom. with several contused wounds on her head and body. dioblsery is assdined as the motive. An inquest was held at Carlisle. on Tuesday, on the body of Mrs. if olstead, aged forty-niae, wine of the treasurer of the local branch of tll3 Salvation Army, who died under tragic circumstances. A daughter of Mrs. Holstead and a neigh- bour quarrelled on the street and got to blows. Mrs. Ho:- stela rushed out to separate them, was struck on the face.and dropped down dead at the feet of the corabstants.—The ver- dict found was that death was due to heart disease.</p>
        <p>lrraffic was greatly delaye-d—on -Tuesday morning in Old- street, London b., a curious accident which occurred to a Grey's-inn-road tram. Close so the top of Charlotte-street a deep treach has been dug at right angles to the roadway for the purpose of laying gas and sewage pipes, and the near horsegiowerving at this point, slipped in, dragging its com- panion with it. It tJok an hour's work to rescue them by means of ropes.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert M'Laughlin, a Scripture reader, of Waltham Abney, was run down on Sunday by a couple of cyclists mounted on a tandem while he was walking with his wife on the high road. The cyclists were unseated, but finding Mr• ignaughlin to be unconscious they remounted and made off. Mr- M'Laughlin's skull Was fractured, and he expired on removal to the hospital. The following telegram has been received from Hampton. new Hampshire :--'• A terrific tornado struck a section of Hampton beach about three o'clock on Mo ,day afternoon, doing immense damage to property and causing great loss of life. In a short time twenty cottages w,..re blown down. and several small hotels were wrecked. A yafilt lying off the beach in the path or the storm was capsized, and of nine persons aboard her at the time fiva were drowned.'"</p>
        <p>A despatch from Beverley. Massachutetts. states that the excursion steamer Surf City. with 63 passengers on board, while half-way from Salem Willows to the wharf at, Beverley, a distance of two miles, was struck by a squall at six o'clook on Monday eu (suing and capsized. Seven persons are known to have been drowned. It is feared the loss of life may prove considerably greater, as mmy of the passengers were in their cabins at the time of the disaster.</p>
        <p>At Peik Forest on Tuesday, while blasting operations were being carried on at ono of the Buxtom Lime Cowpony's ouirries, a fatal accident o icurred, A man had charged and fired a fuz,, but before he hid left the spot it exploded and kiiltd him instintaneouslv. It is said that his name is Bennett. and that he resided at Burbage. Other workmen had remarkably narrow escapes.</p>
        <p>A period of almost phenomenal heat hag been experienced throughout the rn &amp;Me and north-eastern States. The ther- mometer in New yot k on Saturday recorded 106 deg Fahren- heit in the shade, 111degon Sunday. and 102 deg on Mond In Philadelphia Psi deg. Boston Meg, and Chicago 93 deg, leave been reAisrerei. The weather is extremely humid. Much suffering has been caused by the heat.and many deaths are reported.</p>
        <p>The pneumatic dynamite guns, stated to hare been used with such gre4t effect by the Vesuvius. on boar i of which they are mounted, sh ,w highly original construction. They do not carry so far as ordinary cannon, but are capable of launching ton didtanoe of several thousand feet charges of dynamite each contshairig 500 lbs. The shot is sent out by suddenly opening valves in the breech of the gun to admit volumes of compressed air stored beforehand at great pre- sores in in entirely sep irate reservoirs. In this way is avoided the danger.arnoanting alnaoit to certsinty,of the dynamite ex- ploding befoce It can get out of the muzzle,and the enormously destructive missile files harmless through the air until encoun, ering some h ird substance; the shock produces instant rietonatMn. A further advantage gained by using the pneuthatic gun is aimed entire ausenle of smoke and noise. After all, tills most modem of weapons is but an en.boratitm of the time-honoured tin-tube pea-shooter.</p>
        <p>Fever Hospital, Aikin Street'</p>
        <p>Warrington. Report for the month ending 31st June, 1893 In gospital on the Ist of May 5 Admitted during the month Cases of Smallpox Cases of Scarlet Fever (Warrington) Cases of Typhus Fever Nil Cues of Enteric Never, Warrington 2 Cases of Diphtheria Nil Other Diseases Nil</p>
        <p>Total 7</p>
        <p>Discharged Cured 6 Dead Nil In Hospital on the Ist of June Total 7</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GORNALL,</p>
        <p>CYCLISTS us F. PILLAR BRAND. Lnbrica'lng and Bnrning Oils and Cycle Enamel, iname:lette,and Brunswick Black. From all Dealers. AGENTS : bicLsiAN AND CO., 13, SEEL-I.STERET. LIVERPOOL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>ELECTRIC LIG EITING AND TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of the Runcorn Urban District Council was held at tne Council Coamber sn Thursday morning when Mr. H. Taylor presided, there being also present : Messrs. J. Wilson, D. Bisbrown, Mr. J. Sweetman E. unison, J. Dean, J. Pritchard, E. Gattey, William Handley, H. G. Grice, J. Horsefield, with Mr. E. Marshall (secretary), Mr. F. J. Lake (law. clerk), and Dr. McDougal (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>REALTR REPORT.</head>
        <p>REALTR REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer reported that the number of deaths during the month was 22 of these 2 were inquest cases. 'the average death rate was 15 per 1000 for the twelve innnths, and exclusive of the inquest cases aver- aged 13 07 for the same number and period. During the 3rith Si births were registered. For the corresponciiug period last year 31 deaths and 54 births were recorded.</p>
        <p>ALTERATIONS IN OREENWAY-ROAD. The Highways and Building C.)mmittee recommended that the footway on the west side of Greenway-road be flagged from Albert-street to a point opposite the road leading to the 0:d Slapary Works,at a cost of £50.-0o the motion ot Mr. Bisbrown the recommendations were agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>DEFECTIVE DRAINS.</head>
        <p>DEFECTIVE DRAINS.</p>
        <p>The renornmendationq of the Health Committee were: —That the tenders of Mr. Thomas J. R,rkham, for pro- viding and laying drain at the rear of Nos. 1 to 11 in- elusive Thomas-street ; and of Mr. J. Sefton for provid- ing and laying drain in Hignetts Court and at the rear of Nos. 43 to 51. inclusive Regent-street be accepted. A. numbn' of houses in Greenway reported as being badly provided with privy and ashpit accommodation were considered,and the usual 23 days notice was recom- mended to be served upon the owners.—The minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>GAME AND PAWNEROBING LICENSES. At the meeting ot too silarkts and Cemetery Commit- tee it was recommended that Pawnbrokers licenses be granted to the following persons :—William Craddock, 22 and 24, Church-stre.t ; Samuel Dodd, 55, Princess. street ; Henry Dodd, 50, Eerton-strent, and that a Licence to deal in game be granted to Eliza Brimelow,2s, Bridge-street, and that the Seal of the Council be affixed to such license.—The recommendations were confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>RUNCORN PUBLIC BATHS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PUBLIC BATHS.</p>
        <p>IThe Surveyor ia-u-d-e-:;:re-taTro-f the Council submitted a reprart upon the coestion of the establishment of public baths. —Mr. Coiner said they were not going to extinguish the market, but even if that was so it would not much matter. The whole town of Runcorn was a market, so far as rrpirt went. In the report of ex- penditure they had kept well within the limits, and he hoped that all the members would sound the public as to the qoa3tion. Hs truated lin time would come when the report would be favaurably received.—r. Irnison pointed out that upon the previous occasion when :he Council submitted the question to the public they were without a scheme. They had one this time and when the public were made acquainted with the subject at a public meeting, in magic lantern slides (if neeeesary) Me was sure it would receive a favourable hearing.—After some further remark it was decided to submit the report, which dealt with the conversion of the Market Hall into public baths, to the next Council. TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION.—THE ART SCIEDOLS &amp;C. The report of the Technical Instruction Committee adverted to the commun'cation received some time ago from the Science and Art Department with regard to the alternative of either paying back the £5OO received from the department on that portion of the institute set apart for art, or eetabliataing an art school.—The com- mittee had carefully considered the matter and recom- mended that an art school be established, as they found that the funds available in respect of the forthcoming Se3sion would allow of the extra cost. The department complained that as two rooms were used for art purposes and there was only one art teacher, a portion of the students were without constant supervision. If an art school was opened, the department would pay £l5 for a pupil teacher thus reducing the cost of the art teaching by such figure. It was recommended that the art school be opened tor two hours on three evenings every week during a session of forty weeks, and also on Saturday mornings for two hours. It was further recommended that Mr. J. Harmer Hutchings, who holds Art Mister's certificates, be appointed art master for the next session at a salary of £lOO for the session, such salary to in- clude travelling expenses. Mr. Lawrention advised the committee, through the Secretary, that he felt unable to take evening classes for three evenings a week, and that he proposed giving up teaching the Subjects of Physiography and Magnetism and Electricity. Teis would materially affect his remuneration, reducing it by £2O. The council would reeall the fact that the salary of £l5O paid to him as Head Master of the Day Schools was fixed at this low figure because the Council could not be certain, before the Schools were opened, whether the scheme would be a financial success or not.—The committee found, how- ever, that the financial position justified them in propos- ing that his salary be increased, and recommended that his salary be fixed at the rate of £2OO per annum,to take effect fromH e trheet arien- eodp e nti hneg otfe tahn eh iSncgh no els i u four Chemistry Classes at £5O for the Session, as before, It was recommended that Mr. H. E. Mayston, assist- ant master, be appointed teacher of the Paysiography Class at a Falary of £lO for the Session. It was recommended that Mr. B. Crosby be appointed teacher of Magnetism and Electricity at a salary of £lO for the Session and his railway faie. It was recommended that Mr. William Moore's price for the making of the boys' playground be accepted. It was intimated that Mr. William Mather of the Sal- ford Iron Works and the Costner Belner Company had consented to present the prizes some time during the autumn.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wilson, in moving the adoption of the minutes, said as the result of negotiations in the Education D partment, they were in the position of either havinz to pay back £5OO or else establishing an Art School. After due wasideration they recommended the establishment of an Art School, especially since they had secured the services of such an excellent teacher. With regard t 3 the headmaster's salary, and the increase recommended he pointed out the justice of the headmaster's claim. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Dar. Handley seceded, and explained that the change of art-master had become necessary because their pre. vious one was aot quite technically qualified, he being short of one certificate to make up his full complement. The minutes were unanimously confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>ELECTRIC LIGHTING FOR RUNCORN AND DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>ELECTRIC LIGHTING FOR RUNCORN AND DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>The Electric Lighting Committee having taken into consideration a letter received from the Electrical Power Distribution Company, of 78, Cheapside, London, ex- pressing their in:ention of applying to the Bard of Trade for a Provisional Order to supply electricity within the Urban District of Runcorn, and loin.v; of opinion that such an order ought to belong to the Coun- cil, made the following recommendation :—" That the Urban District Council of Runcorn do apply by memorial to the Board of Trade, in pursuance of the Electric Lighting Acts 1882 and 1888, for a Provisional 0 •der to be confirmed in Parliament in the ensuing Session, to authorise the aforesaid Councl to supply electrical energy for public or private purposes within the area of the district of the said Council.</p>
        <p>In supporsing the recommendation Mr. Wilson roferred to an application received some time ago from a com- pany who offered to install Runcorn with tlectricity at a very low rate,at which time it was considered that it was unopportune. Now, it appeared there was another company upon the scene evidently prepared to force the. hand of the Urban Ccuncil in the matter, hence the reason for the recommendation.</p>
        <p>Mr. Grice was desirous to know how long the Pro- visional Order wou'd keep the companies out. The Law Clerk (Mr. Lake) said there was no special time only that granted by the Local Government Board, but the Council could refuse its sanction to any com- pany, which might cause a dead-lock far a time ; but then„on the other hand the company could apply to the Local Government Board stating that they considered the refusal of the Council's consent was unreasonable, when it would depend upon the Local Government Board as to the result.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Collier thought they were going the slowest way to obtain the electric light. If they got the order it would be locked up in that safe, and it would depend upon the energy of individual members to see that it was not kept there. (Laughter.) Ha for 0110 would not rest in the matter. Mr. Imison hoped • that the Council would not attri- bute self motives to him,but he wished to point out that the cheapness of electricity as in the case of gas de- pended upon the quantity generated. He suggested that the matter should b 3 reconsidered with a view to corn. , biniug both the gas and electric lighting as it had been found in many cases to work better together. He would ask the Law Clerk whether it would necessiate a separ- ate procedure for including the Gas Works, SZ3. The Law Clerk considered such a course would neces- sitate a separate sanction: Mr. Grice did not care to be driven by any company. Mr. Pritchard supported the recommendation of the committee, and said they must remember that the Electric Companies were not coming to Runcorn as philanthropists. They came to make money, and if they once let them in, they would find, as other councils had found, that they would have to pay enormous sums CO buy them out. If a Provisional Order was to be ob- tained at all the Urban Council ought to be the ones to get it. After some futther discussion the recommendation of the committee was carried, and a special council will be called to carry out the details of the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>FINANCIAL.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL.</p>
        <p>The financial statement showed that amongst bills recennmended for payment were :—General fund,£3239 1839 I; water fund £lOl 3s 11d; technical instruction, £43 us 8 I ; library lund, £22 15; 03; burial fund, £34. 103 61: permanent works loao. £l3l 173 41; private emit works sinking fund,23B9 25 101.—The treasurer's balances were also submitted.—lt was further recom- mended that the sum of £2,400 be invested in 3i per cent India Stock redeemable 1931. (technical Institute Sinking Fund £900; Private Street Works Sinking Fund £lsoo).—The finances were approved and adopted.</p>
        <p>CALL A SPADE A SPADE. The following none° of motion was down for Mr.</p>
        <p>ImiSon :—" That this Council find that the repayments of capital and haterest on water account up to March 31st, 1898, have been partly made by the addition of a sum of £BOl 74. from sources entirely independent of and distinct front the revenue of the Water Department, and direct that the profit and lose account for the year en ling Marnh 31st, 1898, be amended by the insertion of the words (loss up to date £BOl 7i).' In opening the question Mr. Imison dealt with the statements of Mr. Pritchard concerning the deviation of,and the quality of the water supply and contradicted the statement that there had been no deviation or difference in the quality of the water.</p>
        <p>Considerable discussion ensued as to the admission of extraneous matter iato the discus3ion,whereupon Mr. Imison asked, that for future guidance, what course a member should pursue in the forwarding of a n7.tice of motion. As he considered that his written Irr.ter, if read, would show that he meant to discuss those q restions. However,he agreed to adhere to the terms of the printed notice for the present, and mov4sl its adoption. In do- ing so he submitted that £lBO 17s. Si. was taken out of the rates ; and £420 9s, 4d. out of the bank, and put to the credit of the water fund. Whereas, it should be considered as loss. At length he dealt with ti. wnole of the accounts and pointed out what he required, which was simply nothing more than had been dane In pre- vious years. The matter gave rise to a lengthy discussion as to the proper description to apply to the amount ; and an amendment "to also show the accumulated profit " was proposed and seconded. But upon a vote being taken Mr. Imison's motion was carried in its original form by 6 votes to 5 votes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meetmg of the above Council was held at the Dutton Workh )119 ) 00 Tuesday, Mr. Jos=ph Knowles presiding. Thera were also present :—Admiral Jones-Parrv, J.P ,Nl4ssrs. S. Woodward, E Clarke, T. Antwiss, P. Leather, P. fwis, S. Tudr, Gl.Filliag, W. Bradford, T. Wright, C. E Linaker, J.M. Williams, J. Edwards, J. G. Kydd, W. Fletcher, with Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), Mr. George A4laton (deouty-clerk) ; Me4srs. W. Diggle and J. Swinton ksurveyors),and Mr. J.Farrington (inspector).</p>
        <p>. _ STRETTON AND ITS TELEGRAM OFFICE. Mr. Ellison, clerk to the Stretton Parish Council, for- warded the following letter for the intormatinn of the Council :—"General Post o;Thle, Lendon,l3th May,lB9B. I Sir' with refereee.e to your letter of the Bth inst, asking whether the Parish Councils of Appleton and Wnitiey Superior may become joint guarantors with the Pari.li ' Council of Stretton in respect of atiarant.e re- quired for the Stretton Telegrsph affice, I beg leave to inform you that a Parish Council can itself, enter into a guarantee for telegraph office only when such telegraph office is situated within the lirnits of the parish. But if a Parish Council is unable or unwilling to incur such a liability alone, it inaV make application t 3 the Rural District Council within whose district the parish is, and wadi Rural District Council may, if it thinks fit, become itself responsible for the whole amount of guarantee, and assign the pro- vision of it in such proportions as may be deemed proper to the different parishes within its district as are interested in the telegraph office concerned. it will thus he seen that unless the these parishes of Appleton, Whitley Superior. and Stretton are within the one and Ithe same Rural District, no arrangement could be arrived at by which the thre i parishes might share in the provision of the guarantee. I am, sir, your obedient servant, F. BUCKLEY." Mt. Ellison, enclosed certain resolutions passsd by the Parish G.uncils of Appleton. Stretton, and Whitley Superior. He stated that Dr. Mobberley, of East Clay. don. Wilmslow ; Mr. T. H. Lyon,Appleton Hall: and Mr. H. Nield, Whitley Superior, became joint guarantors to the Post-Master General in the sum of £2B aunually for seven years. The amount to he made good for year ending August Bth, 1897, was 18i lid., and from inquiries he had made the defi:it for the year ending August 1898, would probably be less. This year, and in the future the Pest Office Authorities would pay one half of any deficit. Taking these facts into consideration the amount to be made good by the three parishes would be about £2. On the instructions of the Stretton Parish Council he now made application to the Runcorn Rural District Council to become responsible for the whole amount of the guarantee and assign the provision of it in such proportions as were stated in the different re- solutions.—lt was decided to comply with the request and undertake the guarantee. ALLEGED OBSTRUCTION AT DUTTON. Mr. Sydney E. Stokes, clerk to the Dutton Parish Council, forwarded a copy of the following resolution passed at the quarterly meeting of the Council, held on the 30th June :—" That the Clerk be instructed to write to the District Council resp,cting the action of the A:stem Estate obstructing the public footpath about 30 yards south of Birds' Wood,leading over the railway ; through Birds' Wood to Aston Grange, by placing barbel wire across it. This path has been used by the public as long as the oldest inhabitant of the district can remember. The Parish Council of Dutton ask the Runcorn District Cluccit if they voill kindly give this matter their earliest consideration. The footpath, it was stated, was shown 'on the Ordnance survey map.—Mr. E Linaker ex- I • plamed that in his opinion the path was not a public one at all ; and the way the barbed wire got there was doubtless owing to the gamekeepers putting something of the kind there to keep away trespassers. Anyone using the path at all had to climb the railway embank- ment and a couple of fences. He did not object to an. inquiry into the aubject.—After some further remarks the Clerk was instructed to obtain all the information possible concerning the path and report at the next Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>DUTTON WATER SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>THE DUTTON WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stokes also forwarded a copy of the following resolution passed at the quarterly meeting of the Dutt an Parish Council :—"That the Clerk be instructed to write the Clerk of the Rural District Council asking what pro- gress had been made with the scheme for supplying water to cottages in Dutton as stated in th 3 Council's letter to the Dutton Parish Council of March 10% 1895." The Clerk was instructed to write to the Dutton Council stating how the matter stood. This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>RUNCORN ODDFELLOWS AND THE CENTENARY OF THE ORDER. IMPOSING PROCESSION.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN ODDFELLOWS AND THE CENTENARY OF THE ORDER. IMPOSING PROCESSION.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, in favourable We-3:t—hat:, the Runcorn lodges of the Grand United Order of Oddfollows,celebra- ted the centenary ot the Order, by a grand united de- monstration which was witnessed by a large concourse of people, and followed in many instances to its destina- tion. Assembling at their various head-quarters, the lodges first of all indulged in a parade on their own account through several of the principle streets, after- wards assembling in Devonshire-square, they proceeded in the following order headed by their respective brass bands to Halton :—Gos- sage's Pr.ze Band, Runcorn's Pride Lodge ; Widnes Subscription Band. Rose of Sharon Lodge ; Weston Church Band, Rising Sun Lodge; Weston Wesleyan Band, Star of Freedom Lodge ; Runcorn Volunteer Band, Lily of the Valley Lodge, joined by certain mem- bers of the Doke of Edinburgh Lodge. The route taken was by way of Egerton-street, Ashridge-street, Rutland- street, Waterloo Bridge, Station-road, Lowlands-street, Shaw-street, down Greenway-road, High-street, to Bridge-street, Halton-road to Halton. The bands played lively music en route, and the variety of the regalia worn by the various lodges produced quite a brilliant spectacle as the procesgion moved along the streets. Arrived at Halton Castle the members and their friends disported themselves on the green whore the hands in turn played for dancing. The refreshment buffet was under the management of Mr. Joseph Walker. of the Castle Hotel, whose catering gave general satisfaction. The festivities were kept up with great enthusiasm until about eight o'clock when the procession was re-formed in the order observed on the march out, and a move made towards home. The arrangements were in the hands of a committee representing each lodge, of which Mr. William Massey was the indefatigable hon. secre- tary, and were carried out in a most satisfactory manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>RUNCORN WESLEYAN CYCLE CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN WESLEYAN CYCLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday the members of the above club were invited to a run with their president (Mr. Bellamy) and family, the destination being High Legh. Tho route taken was through Moore. Thelwall and Grappenhall. Upon the destination being reamed, a capital tea was provided for the members, at the invitation of their president, at the C.T.G. Farm, High Legh. A vote of thanks was accorded Mr. Bellamy on the proposition of Mr. A. Dodd, seconded by Mr. J. Nanc,sllis, and sup- ported by Mr. E. Marshall. Mr. Bellamy in respondir explained that owing to his family being a small bicycle club of its own, as it were, he had been unabli) to par- take more generally in the club runs, but possibly they might be oftener together in the future. He compli- mented the club upon its position. After a stroll round, the return journey was made by way of Arloy, and Great Budworth. Runcorn being reached about 9.30. There were about 19 members present at the run.</p>
        <p>WILL OF THE LATE AL-DERMAN GREENALL, WARRINGTON. Probate of the will which bears date February 14th, 1898, of Mr. Edward Greenall, of 2, Roby- Place Altrincham, Cheshire, who died on the 2nd May last, aged 50 years, has been granted to his widow, Mrs. Anna Maria Greenall and his brother Mr. Henry Greenall, of Warrington. The testator's personal estate has been valued at £15,433 7s. 7d, fle bequeathed to the executors £5O each, and to his wile .£lOO and his furniture and household effects, and the use and enjoyment f the effects left to him by his father,the late Mr. Edward Green- all, which, subject to Mrs. Greenall's use, are to be in trust for the testator's nephew, Gerald Greenall, land are to devolve as heirlooms. The testator be- queathed Archdeacon Greenall's plate to his brother ' Henry, and he left his residuary estate in trust for his wife during her life, and subject to her life in- terest in trust for his brothers Peter and Henry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>THE WAS.</p>
        <p>CERVERA'S DISASTER. A SPANISH ADMIRAL KILLED. An official telegram has been received at Havana frnin Admiral Cervera stating that he ia a prisoner with of his officers and men. He had all killed, including Admiral Villand and Captain L..maga. Captains and Conas ware wounded, and are prisoners. All the prisoners are treated well. In recogniti 3a of his CO spicuous heroism the Americans albwed Captain Eulate to keep his sword.</p>
        <p>DESTRUCTION OF A SPANISH. LINE The liner Alfonso XII. on Tuesday when eight mild from Havana, was chased by some of the American war: ships. She endeavoured to enter Mariel harbour, bO stranded at the entrance. The enemy's ships °per their grins upon her until she caught fire. Sne is to lost. Forces of infantry and artillery ran down to shore and succeeded in saving the crow and part of cargo._</p>
        <p>'ALLEGED ATTEMPT TO BLOW UP ROYAL APARTMENTS.</p>
        <p>NEW YORE, Tin:raspslr.. The " N.-?.w York Sun" publishes to-nay a Madri despatch stating that an attempt has boon made to blo' up the apartments of Queen aristiaa. Several are' have been made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>SURRENDERING OF SANTIAGO.</head>
        <p>THE SURRENDERING OF SANTIAGO.</p>
        <p>It is reported that President Itil'Einley received late ori Wednesday night indications that General L. 13 ores woald soon surrender Santiago without further bloodshed. Spanish troeps in the line are deserting in largo numbers to the American camp. StIOCICING TRAGEDY Ali SWANSEA Yesterday morning a respectable greman named O'Neill, living in Swansea, was seize.. with frenzv, ali taking a knife stabbed his wife eight times as she lay lar bed. She was remlved to the hospital, but died in hal,. an hour. The murderer ran off to trio cansl,jumped int° the lock and drowned himself. The couple lived happili together,and the murderer was a teeto slier. The oulf explanation of the tra,zedy is sudden madness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>HIGH TREASON IN AUSTRIA.</head>
        <p>HIGH TREASON IN AUSTRIA.</p>
