        <head>A. RIINCORNSBOUND- SCRoONER. SfiN</head>
        <p>A. RIINCORNSBOUND- SCRoONER. SfiN</p>
        <p>TWO LIVES LOST,</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning about 8.30; the schooner TrY, bound from Runcorn for Annan, with a cargo of Indian corn in bulk, sank in the Cro,by Channel,the captain, named Tames Walker, and a youth named, Robert Ferguson being drowned, while Samuel Guillon, the only remaining hand on board, woe saved. The schooner, it appears, had been conveyed some distance out of port by a tug named the Swii but, encountering some heavy weather during the' night, and there being a considerable list in the vessel, owing, it is believed, to the shifting of the cargo, the captain thought it advisable to minimise the danger as far as po,sible by returning to port. While in the Crosby Channel. the vessel was seen from the observatory on sidston Hill and other parts to suddenly sink beam endi, first. The captain appears to have been drowned zEtnast imaaecliatelY while ()Milieu] and Ferguson did their best to sal.e themselves by swimming. Guillon succeeded In, doing so, keeping afloat until extricated from W. perilous position by the Crew of the; steamer Flying Falcon. Ferguson managed t'°' keep afloat for some time, but at length called • his companion that he was unable to hold out an, longer, and sank utterly exhausted. CaPtell'ir ' Walker was fifty-three Years of age, and a native Dalbeattie, Scotland. Ferguson is said to have he: longed to Whitehaven. So far the borlis are 00" ii reported as having been recovered. Gullion, t"„ rescued youth, who belongs to Wigtonshire, c)- arriving at the Landing-stage between nine and ten o'clock, reported the ca•uality to the Custorne • authorities, and was able to proceed to Prin,•"; street, Everton, to tho hof IS relative. As Oa! sunken vessel lies in the tairway of outgoing 'le incoming traffic, with masts standing out 02,44.11,3 water, the Dock Board authoritie3 have mean";.l, placed a watch vessel on duty. The sunkea veS" belonged to Messrs. Robinson and Sons, of Annan i.: Captain W. Ferguson, of the steamer Iveagb, n ward bound from Newry, reports having passed the Try, and seeing that she was in a very dangeroUn condition, offered the assistance of his crew, WWI'. was declined. This happened about an hour bE._ fore she went down, and, being unable to persua.n! Captain Walker to accept the proferred aid, 116. informed the crews of two tug-boats in the neign bourhood.</p>
