        <p>THE LATE Inn. °LAI:ATONE. The MAYOR said he had receove i the following letter from Mr. Heary Gladstone : —"LI awarcien Castle, June idth. Dear Sire—On behalf of my mother and family, I beg to thank the Town Ceuncil of Werrington for too ir very kied message of sympathy." The Mayor moved that the letter be entered on the minutes.—Alder Sutton seconded and it was carried. WATCH COMMITTEE. —THEATRE LICENSES. At a meeting of the Watch Committee it was reselved that the lieenees granted for the performance nf stage Plays in the R oyal Court Theatre and Theatre Royal be renewed to Mr. Joseph Potter for 12 inentes.—The Minutes were approved, on the motion of the Mayor Reminded hy Councillor Heaton. THE PARR HALL CO eIMITTEE'S A.CCOUN re.— COUNCILLOR SMETFIURsT SEES AN IRREGULARITY. Councillor SHAW-GREEN moved the adoption of the Minutes of the Finance end Estate Committee, which contained the report of the Borough Audit.—Tal Deputy Mayor seconded. Councillor SMETHURST said ke would like to sey a few words with regard to a remark in the report of the Soroueh Auditor's which read as follows :—" We have also examined the Veuchers. the Town Clerk's Reenters of Mortgages and Annuities, and the separate accouets relating to the /ear Hall. The latter are net included in the Borough Treasurer's department, nor published in the Annual Statement, nor ee the payments appear to have been passed by any Committee." Ho (Ceuneiller Smethurst) eaid he considered it a very bad state of things when these accounts which were net Small ones, were allowed in other hande then those of the Borough Treasurer. He thought that every account connected with the Council Should pass through the Borough Treasurer's hands. It was evident from their statement that the Borough auditors were somewhat dissatisfied, and that they had Put the matter in this form to bring it before the Coun- cil. He should like to see a statement ply, before he Council of the balance owing on Parr Hell. He thought it was net fair to the ratepayers that an account like this should be kept outside the Council. There were aeveral other matters of the same kind, and unless eome- one spoke about them they weuld soon find themselves in a very wrong state of affairs. They were spending money at a very rapid rate and they still had a rate- of • Is in the £ ; and within half a mile eif the 'Crown Hall, he should think, there were £lOO,OOO or £200.000 laid out in manufactories which had never paid a dividend on ththeesaordinary shares, and it was not right to cernpel Deeple to pay rate! for things which they might not tee., elle proper. --r`gerrnan ROBERTS : We are not doing. tC°4II9IIIOE SMETHURST said he was very glad indeed at tbe auditors had had the courage to put the matter ;in their report, and he hoped and trusted that all things -t1 the future would come into the Treasurer's herrn. Councillor GRACE thought there Was a great deal in What the previene speaker said with regard to the ore- dines of the Parr Hall Committee. Ele did not think it was desirable that any " Star-chember " system of dealing with corporate money should exist. The Puler which said " nor do the statements appear to have been Passed by any committee," related to something which be considered utterly unconstitutional. He thought it Was quite time they had some statement as to how the affairs of the Parr Hall Committee wore carried on. (Hear, hear.) Councillor Bewererr said he would like to name an- ether matter. Among the various sanctions they hal bed to loans he saw there was men for the borrowing. of £7OO for laying out Q ieents Gardens. He was glad to see that but he should like to know why the Gordens !tore such a great time in being laid out. It is quite time the nubile had the opportunity of enjoying them. (Hear, hear), t Alderman Roamers said he felt bound to say a word or 4,rrwith regard to the remarks made. Ire the first place onna.teliieyere of Warrington had never been celled ue anything with regard to the Parr Hall ad lire, except Was one matter sanctioned by the Council. and that as the laying out of the streets. That was the only ex- Pense the ratepayers had had to subscribe to. The accounts of tee committee for the previous year at any Vete, were, after being audited, submitted to the General Purposes Committee, and the Mine process would be gone through this time. He could say there was no desire or attempt on the part of any member of the committee to eoriceal anything in connection with these accounts. Flo Should be delighted if the Borough Treasurer would take the accounts over, together with the responsibility attached to them. The reason thee accounts had not been scheduled with the remainder of the Borough accounts was this. They received this property on be- half of the Corporation in a comparatively unfurnished condition, and they had had to furnish and to borrow Money for the purpose. And with the idea of clearing off that debt the cenetnittee had been nursing the accounts until the profits arrived at the amount of the deficit. Councillor SMETHURST : How much profit have you Made in 12 months ? Alderman ROBERTS : I cannot tell you, but I know We have made a profit. Councillor SMETHURST : I know you have not. Alderman ROBERTS said if the Corporation was willing to take the accounts and deal with them, the Committee .tlias quite ready to hand them over, but all they had been trying to do was to so accanauiate the profits as to enable them to hand the establishment over to the Cor- Iteration free of debt as soon as possible. There had net been a single purchase made by anybody which had not been approved by the Committee, and for which Vouchers had been duly obtained. He had not the Slightest objeetion to this certificate. He thought the taditors would have been remiss in their duty if they al not called attention to the fact that the accounts in questioa the sad.telvere not scheduled, but he did net think that any. ei-orte considered for one moment that there was had ;..e'ction upon the Committee. If they did they 'Atter say so in so many words ; but he did net be- e thet was the suggestion. Col,neth • ac -er eueTntrasr : The suggestion is that the ripte should go throuell the treasurer's hands. ROBERTS : We have no ohie ition to that Whatever.. The committee would be only toe pleased to e relieved of this responsibility. All we desire to do is 2,band the place over complete, and without cost to the eayepayees. Alderman SUTTON said he thought the sooner the work of We committee was done in the same way as that of _Other committees, and reports were made in the usual ' ZIT every month, the more satisfactory it would be. eneV would then know exactly what was going on. Alderman Hemmen said he was glad indeed that Alderman Roberts had given an explanation which must he satisfactory to everybedv so far as it went. He Should be the last to throw the slightest suspicion on auV gentleman who performed hie duties as that coin- lnittee had done, and he did not believe that any mem- ber of the Committee would be a party to any transac- t - ion which might be considered unfair or unsatisfactory. (Hear, hear.) They had had a great deal to contend :With and he highly appreciated the motive that had Induced the Committee to keep back the acceunte until they had suceeeded in plying off on incubus in order to eland over to the ratepayers of Warrington, Parr Hall !Pee from all debt and liability of any kind. He trusted that the statement made by Alderman Roberts would he satisfactory. Councillor Weems said he thought the matter ought to °Tire been left in the hands of the Finance and Estate Warrington. Parr Hall was a portion of the estate of He considered the General Purposes Cone- mattes were to blame for not having slid that this pro- Perty was a portion of the estate of Warrington just as much_ as the property purchased in Bridge-street and Other places. It ought to have been handed over to the 4unee and Estate Committee to be dealt with by the Treasurer's department. The Parr Hall Committee ap- geared to be doing something, which other people had lOhle before,and that was to manage a theatre when they 'spew nothing about it. (Laughter.) Councillor Sleeve-Gamut said he wanted teesupplement *bat Alderman Reberte had said. If these accounts Passed through the Treasurer's hands they would have to be published. Councillor GRAM?: So they ought to be. Councilloi.SMA.W-GREEer There is a considerable utterance of opinion about that. He thought they would Understand that a just and definite reason had been given for their not being published. The minutes were then passed. THE WIDENING OF BRIDGE-STREET.—MR. TOFT'S PROPERTY AND THE BUILDING LINE. Alderman Smerrom proposed the adoption of the ITl•inutes of the Street Improvement and Lighting Corn- ittee.—Aldermen BIACECIneRST Seconded. COUlleillOr BENNETT moved an amendment to the effect that the minutes be confirmed with the exceptien °..1 the approval of the Dian of the shop in Bridge-St. for r_4l" executors of John Tot t. His reason for opposing the 21.Thlutes was not because he had any objection to the Tans, for,indeed, he had not Seen them, but because the !teasing of the plan definitely fixed the line of the street, Zed.M fixed it in a manner different from what the corn- 'ee considered best. The Street Improvement Com- e._</p>
        <p>tidy as you might wish.' •If I was the conopiny,' chimes in the other lady in her turn, 'I should feel that the least I could do with an old servant like you, that's been with 'em no doubt for the best part of a lifs-time, was to see that your family didn't have to bear the expense of putting you away when--' say,' says the grimy-faced man, protestieg, 'give us a chawnce. You're reckoning me up at a fine old rate, you are. Why, I ain't bees in this service not fire minutes! This is my first trip.' *Then Ido think, remarks the old lady with dignity, 'you might have mentioned it at firit,tny good man.'"-- " Windsor" Magazine, CHIVALRY IN THE CRICKET-FIELD. If a man possesses any good qualities, cricket will most snrely develop them. It is a game which calls con- stantly for little acts of courtesy and self-denial, and no one who plays it can help feeling the better for its influ- ence. Indeed, it would be a well-nigh impossible task to chronicle the instances of true nobility which occur every summer on cricket-grounds. Late last season two friendly professionals found themselves opposed to each other in a match, which was fraught with the most vital consequences for both of them. One wanted but two runs to gain the batting average for his side, and the ether had merely to take one wicket to secure a large prize for the best bowling record. With ordieary luck both would have managed these simple tasks easily enough, but it so happened that the bowler sprained his shoulder, and could only manipulate the leather with great difficulty. On coming down to take his place at the crease, professional number one heard that his chum was to be taken off at the end of the over, and after a short but sharp struggle he decided to sacrifice himself for friendship's ak3. Therefore, instead of sending the last ball to the boundary, as he could easily have done, he spooned it into the air, and was caught, No one can tell what the gentle deed ceqt him, for he wao treated to a merciless fire of chaff from the disgusted spectators, and had the additional mortification of seeing his chum chaired twice round the field at the finish of the game. Time after time a good-hearted county player has thrown his wicket away in order Co save that of a fellow- cricketer. He was once within a single of his century, When his partner, a nervous young Colt on trial for the county, called him for an impossible run. "No, no, nay lad; stop there," cried the older man ; but his warning -was unheeded, for the foolish youngster came rushing like a madman to the opposite end. "You've lost your bead, my boy," said the county man, reproachfully, " but you may as well keep your wicket." He then unhesitatingly made for the other stumps,which were down almost before be had taken a couple of strides. The Colt stayed in and made forty runs, but the generous professional missed his talent money and considerably spoilt his average. Anyone visiting a provincial cricket-ground before the commencement of a match is sure to be amazed by the happy-go-lucky method of practising adopted. Nets are often totally dispensed with, and balls fly like hail about the ears of players and spectators alike. A cricketer, who was standing near the ropes of a village field,was startled by a loud shout of warnirg,arei looked up • time to see a ball making with terrific force straight for his bead. iH' s first impulse was to duck,but he remembered that a group of lad ie 3 were chatting un- consciously behind him, and that if he go: out of the way himself the ball would hit one of them. The thought nerved hizi to action, and his band shot up 0 stop the hurtling piece of leather. The momentum was so great, however, that four of hie fingers were broken, and the ball fell with a terrible sound upon his unprotected tem- ple. Brain fever followed the accident, and it was only after a prolonged struggle that the chivalrous cricketer. recovered from its effects. At a critical period of an exciting match in a manufacturing town,the umpire de- clared the last man on the home side to be caught out, though the ball was palpably a trapped one. Earaged by this decision,the crowd swarmed over the ropes and made for the pitch.evidently with the intention of mob- bing tho unfortunate umpire. " Gat into my jacket, quick," cried the player who had been wrongfully dis- missed, "and give me your smock. They won't notice the difference If you walk quietly out the bottom way." The exchange was speedily effeited,and then the daring player,ctamming the umpire's hat over his eyes went to meet the once:nu:lg crowd. For ten minutes he fought with conspicuous skill,assisted by the other players,and stuck to his new character until he felt sure that the umpire had got clear away. Then he removed his dis- guise, and the crowd,which had commenced by mauling him, ended by carrying him triumphantly, all bleeding and dishevelled as he was, right through the town 0 the club's headquarters.—" Cassell' s Saturday Journal."</p>
